#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:797
msgid ""
"Rsync can also be run as a daemon, in which case the following options are "
"accepted:"
msgstr "Rsync はデーモンとして実行することもできます。その場合、次のオプションが受け入れられます。"

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:813
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"--daemon                 run as an rsync daemon\n"
"--address=ADDRESS        bind to the specified address\n"
"--bwlimit=RATE           limit socket I/O bandwidth\n"
"--config=FILE            specify alternate rsyncd.conf file\n"
"--dparam=OVERRIDE, -M    override global daemon config parameter\n"
"--no-detach              do not detach from the parent\n"
"--port=PORT              listen on alternate port number\n"
"--log-file=FILE          override the \"log file\" setting\n"
"--log-file-format=FMT    override the \"log format\" setting\n"
"--sockopts=OPTIONS       specify custom TCP options\n"
"--verbose, -v            increase verbosity\n"
"--ipv4, -4               prefer IPv4\n"
"--ipv6, -6               prefer IPv6\n"
"--help, -h               show this help (when used with --daemon)\n"
msgstr ""
"--daemon                 rsync デーモンとして実行\n"
"--address=ADDRESS        指定されたアドレスにバインド\n"
"--bwlimit=RATE           ソケット I/O 帯域幅を制限\n"
"--config=FILE            別の rsyncd.conf ファイルを指定\n"
"--dparam=OVERRIDE, -M    グローバルデーモン config パラメータを上書き\n"
"--no-detach              親からデタッチしない\n"
"--port=PORT              代替ポート番号で待ち受けする\n"
"--log-file=FILE          「ログ・ファイル」の設定を上書き\n"
"--log-file-format=FMT    「ログ形式」の設定を上書き\n"
"--sockopts=OPTIONS       カスタム TCP オプションを指定\n"
"--verbose, -v            冗長表示\n"
"--ipv4, -4               IPv4 を優先\n"
"--ipv6, -6               IPv6 を優先\n"
"--help, -h               このヘルプを表示 (--daemon と共に使うとき)\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:821
msgid ""
"Rsync accepts both long (double-dash + word) and short (single-dash + "
"letter)  options.  The full list of the available options are described "
"below.  If an option can be specified in more than one way, the choices are "
"comma-separated.  Some options only have a long variant, not a short."
msgstr "Rsync は長いオプション (二重ダッシュ+単語) と短いオプション (一重ダッシュ+文字) の両方を受け付けます。使用可能なオプションの完全なリストを以下に示します。オプションを複数の方法で指定できる場合、選択肢はコンマで区切られます。一部のオプションには、ショートではなくロング版のみがあります。"

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:827
msgid ""
"If the option takes a parameter, the parameter is only listed after the long "
"variant, even though it must also be specified for the short.  When "
"specifying a parameter, you can either use the form B<--option=param>, B<--"
"option\\ param>, B<-o=param>, B<-o\\ param>, or B<-oparam> (the latter "
"choices assume that your option has a short variant)."
msgstr "オプションがパラメータを取る場合、ショート版にも指定する必要がありますが、パラメータはロング版の後にのみリストされます。パラメータを指定するときは、B<--option=param>、B<--option\\ param>、B<-o=param>、B<-o\\ param>、または B<-oparam> のいずれかの形式を使用できます (後者の選択肢は、オプションにショート版があることを前提としています)。"

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:833
msgid ""
"The parameter may need to be quoted in some manner for it to survive the "
"shell's command-line parsing.  Also keep in mind that a leading tilde (B<~>) "
"in a pathname is substituted by your shell, so make sure that you separate "
"the option name from the pathname using a space (rather than an equal sign) "
"if you want the local shell to expand it."
msgstr "パラメータは、シェルのコマンドライン解析に耐えられるように、何らかの方法で引用符で囲む必要があるかもしれません。また、パス名の先頭のチルダ (B<~>) はシェルによって置き換えられることに注意してください。そのため、ローカルシェルで展開したい場合は、(等号ではなく) スペースを使用してオプション名とパス名を区切るようにしてください。"

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:834
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--help>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:838
msgid ""
"Print a short help page describing the options available in rsync and exit.  "
"You can also use B<-h> for B<--help> when it is used without any other "
"options or arguments (since otherwise it means B<--human-readable>)."
msgstr "Rsync で利用可能なオプションを説明した短いヘルプページを印刷し終了します。他のオプションや引数なしで使用された場合 B<--help> の代わりに B<-h> を使用することもできます (そうでない場合は B<--human-readable>を意味します)。"

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:838
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--version>, B<-V>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:842
msgid ""
"Print the rsync version plus other info and exit.  When repeated, the "
"information is output in a JSON format that is still fairly readable (client "
"side only)."
msgstr "Rsync のバージョンとその他の情報を出力して終了します。繰り返されると、情報はかなり読みやすい JSON 形式で出力されます (クライアント側のみ)。"

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:847
msgid ""
"The output includes a list of compiled-in capabilities, a list of "
"optimizations, the default list of checksum algorithms, the default list of "
"compression algorithms, the default list of daemon auth digests, a link to "
"the rsync web site, and a few other items."
msgstr "出力には、コンパイル済み機能のリスト、最適化のリスト、チェックサムアルゴリズムの既定値リスト、圧縮アルゴリズムの既定値リスト、デーモン認証ダイジェストの既定値リスト、rsync ウェブサイトへのリンク、その他いくつかの項目が含まれています。"

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:847 original/man1/rsync.1:4276
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--verbose>, B<-v>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:854
msgid ""
"This option increases the amount of information you are given during the "
"transfer.  By default, rsync works silently.  A single B<-v> will give you "
"information about what files are being transferred and a brief summary at "
"the end.  Two B<-v> options will give you information on what files are "
"being skipped and slightly more information at the end.  More than two B<-v> "
"options should only be used if you are debugging rsync."
msgstr "このオプションを使用すると、転送中に提供される情報量が増加します。既定値では、rsync は静かに動作します。1つだけ B<-v>は、転送されているファイルに関する情報と、最後に簡単な要約を提供します。2 つの B<-v> オプションは、どのファイルがスキップされているかについての情報を提供し、最後にもう少し詳しい情報を提供します。2 つ以上の B<-v> オプションは、rsync をデバッグする場合にのみ使用してください。"

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:866
msgid ""
"The end-of-run summary tells you the number of bytes sent to the remote "
"rsync (which is the receiving side on a local copy), the number of bytes "
"received from the remote host, and the average bytes per second of the "
"transferred data computed over the entire length of the rsync run. The "
"second line shows the total size (in bytes), which is the sum of all the "
"file sizes that rsync considered transferring.  It also shows a \"speedup\" "
"value, which is a ratio of the total file size divided by the sum of the "
"sent and received bytes (which is really just a feel-good bigger-is-better "
"number).  Note that these byte values can be made more (or less)  human-"
"readable by using the B<--human-readable> (or B<--no-human-readable>) "
"options."
msgstr "実行終了のサマリーからは、リモート rsync (ローカル・コピーの受信側)に送信されたバイト数、リモート・ホストから受信したバイト数、rsync の実行全体にわたって計算された転送データの 1秒あたりの平均バイト数がわかります。2 行目は合計サイズ (バイト単位) を示します。これは、rsync が転送を考慮したすべてのファイルサイズの合計です。また、「高速化」値も示されています。これは、ファイルの合計サイズを送受信バイトの合計で割った比率です (これは実際には、大きいほど良いという気分になるだけの数字です)。これらのバイト値は、B<--human-readable> (または B<--no-human-readable> オプションを使用して、人間が読みやすく (または読みにくく) することができます。"

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:874
msgid ""
"The B<-v> option is equivalent to the setting of groups of B<--info> and B<--"
"debug> options.  You can choose to use these newer options in addition to, "
"or in place of using B<--verbose>, as any fine-grained settings override the "
"implied settings of B<-v>.  Both B<--info> and B<--debug> have help texts "
"available that tells you exactly what flags are set for each increase in "
"verbosity (use B<--info=help> or B<--debug=help> to see them)."
msgstr "B<-v> オプションは、B<--info> および B<--debug> オプションのグループを設定することと同じです。B<--verbose> を使用する代わりに、またはそれに加えて、これらの新しいオプションを使用することを選択できます。これは、B<-v> の暗黙の設定を上書きする細かい設定のためです。 B<--info> と B<--debug> のどちらにもヘルプ・テキストが用意されており、冗長性が高くなるたびにどのフラグが設定されているかを正確に知ることができます( B<--info=help> または B<--debug=help> を使用して確認してください)。"

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:880
msgid ""
"However, do keep in mind that a daemon's \"B<max\\ verbosity>\" setting will "
"limit how high the various individual flags can be set on the daemon side.  "
"For instance, if the max is 2, then any info and/or debug flag that is set "
"to a higher value than what would be set by B<-vv> will be downgraded to the "
"B<-vv> level in the daemon's logging."
msgstr "ただし、デーモンの \"B<max\\ verbosity>\" 設定により、デーモン側で設定できる個々のフラグの上限が制限されることに注意してください。たとえば、最大値が 2 の場合、info フラグや debug フラグが B<-vv> で設定される値よりも大きい値に設定されていると、デーモンのロギングで B<-vv> レベルにダウングレードされます。"

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:880
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--info=FLAGS>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:888
msgid ""
"This option lets you have fine-grained control over the information output "
"you want to see.  An individual flag name may be followed by a level number, "
"with 0 meaning to silence that output, 1 being the default output level, and "
"higher numbers increasing the output of that flag (for those that support "
"higher levels).  Use B<--info=help> to see all the available flag names, "
"what they output, and what flag names are added for each increase in the "
"verbose level.  Some examples:"
msgstr "このオプションを使用すると、表示する情報出力を詳細に制御できます。個々のフラグ名の後にはレベル番号を続けることができます。0 はその出力をなくすことを意味し、1 は既定値の出力レベルを表し、より高い番号はそのフラグの出力を増加させます (より高いレベルをサポートするもののため)。B<--info=help> を使用すると、使用可能なすべてのフラグ名、出力内容、および詳細レベルを上げるたびに追加されるフラグ名が表示されますを使用します。次に例を示します:"

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:893
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"rsync -a --info=progress2 src/ dest/\n"
"rsync -avv --info=stats2,misc1,flist0 src/ dest/\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:899
msgid ""
"Note that B<--info=name>'s output is affected by the B<--out-format> and B<--"
"itemize-changes> (B<-i>) options.  See those options for more information on "
"what is output and when."
msgstr "B<--info=name> の出力は、B<--out-format> および B<--itemize-changes> (B<-i>) オプションの影響を受けます。何がいつ出力されるかについての詳細は、これらのオプションを参照してください。"

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:904
msgid ""
"This option was added to 3.1.0 (released September 2013), so an older rsync "
"on the server side might reject your attempts at fine-grained control.  See "
"also the \"B<max\\ verbosity>\" caveat above when dealing with a daemon."
msgstr "このオプションは 3.1.0 (2013 年 9 月リリース) で追加されたため、サーバー・サイドの古い rsync では、きめ細かな制御を行おうとしても拒否される可能性があります。デーモンを扱う場合は、上記の \"B<max\\ verbosity>\" に関する注意も参照してください。"

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:904
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--debug=FLAGS>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:912
msgid ""
"This option lets you have fine-grained control over the debug output you "
"want to see.  An individual flag name may be followed by a level number, "
"with 0 meaning to silence that output, 1 being the default output level, and "
"higher numbers increasing the output of that flag (for those that support "
"higher levels).  Use B<--debug=help> to see all the available flag names, "
"what they output, and what flag names are added for each increase in the "
"verbose level.  Some examples:"
msgstr "このオプションを使用すると、表示するデバッグ出力をきめ細かく制御できます。個々のフラグ名の後にはレベル番号を続けることができます。0 はその出力をなくすことを意味し、1 は既定値の出力レベルを表し、より高い番号はそのフラグの出力を増加させます (より高いレベルをサポートするもののため)。B<--debug=help> を使用すると、使用可能なすべてのフラグ名、出力内容、および詳細レベルを上げるたびに追加されるフラグ名が表示されますを使用します。次に例を示します:"

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:917
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"rsync -avvv --debug=none src/ dest/\n"
"rsync -avA --del --debug=del2,acl src/ dest/\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:922
msgid ""
"Note that some debug messages will only be output when the B<--stderr=all> "
"option is specified, especially those pertaining to I/O and buffer debugging."
msgstr "一部のデバッグメッセージ、特に I/O およびバッファのデバッグに関するメッセージは、B<--stderr=all> オプションが指定されている場合にのみ出力されることに注意してください。"

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:929
msgid ""
"This option is not forwarded by the client to the server side, so that you "
"can specify different debug values for each side of the transfer.  This also "
"allows you to specify debug options that are only present in one of the "
"rsync versions.  If you want to duplicate the same option on both sides, "
"using brace expansion is an easy way to save you some typing.  This works in "
"zsh and bash:"
msgstr "このオプションはクライアントからサーバー側に転送されないため、転送の側ごとに異なるデバッグ値を指定できます。これにより、rsync のいずれかのバージョンにのみ存在するデバッグオプションを指定することもできます。両側で同じオプションを複製したい場合は、ブレース展開を使用すると、入力の手間を省く簡単な方法です。これは zsh と bash で動作します:"

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:933
#, no-wrap
msgid "rsync -aiv {-M,}--debug=del2 src/ dest/\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:935
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--stderr=errors|all|client>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:940
msgid ""
"This option controls which processes output to stderr, and whether info "
"messages are output to stderr rather than stdout.  The mode strings can be "
"abbreviated down as far as a single letter.  The 3 possible choices are:"
msgstr "このオプションは、どのプロセスが stderr に出力するか、info メッセージを stdout ではなく stderr に出力するかどうかを制御します。モード文字列は、1 文字まで省略できます。次の 3 つの選択肢があります。"

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:949
msgid ""
"B<errors> - (the default) causes all the rsync processes to send errors "
"directly to stderr, even if the process is on the remote side of the "
"transfer.  Info messages are sent to the client side via the protocol "
"stream.  If stderr is not available (i.e. when directly connecting with a "
"daemon via a socket) errors fall back to being sent via the protocol stream."
msgstr "B<errors> - (既定値) を指定すると、すべての rsync プロセスでエラーが stderr に直接送信されます。プロセスが転送のリモート側にある場合でも同様です。Info メッセージは、プロトコルストリームを介してクライアント側に送信されます。stderr が使用できない場合 (つまり、ソケット経由でデーモンに直接接続している場合)、エラーはプロトコルストリーム経由で送信されるようになります。"

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:958
msgid ""
"B<all> - causes all rsync messages (info and error) to be written directly "
"to stderr from all (possible) processes.  This causes stderr to become line-"
"buffered (instead of unbuffered) and eliminates the ability to divide up the "
"info and error messages by file handle.  For those doing debugging or using "
"several levels of verbosity, this option can help to avoid clogging up the "
"transfer stream (which should prevent any chance of a deadlock bug hanging "
"things up).  It also allows B<--debug> to enable some extra I/O related "
"messages."
msgstr "B<all> - すべての rsync メッセージ (情報とエラー) が、すべての (可能な) プロセスから stderr に直接書き込まれます。これにより、stderr は (unbuffered ではなく) line-buffered になり、ファイルハンドルによって情報メッセージとエラーメッセージを分割する機能がなくなります。デバッグを行ったり、いくつかの冗長レベルを使用したりする場合は、このオプションを使用すると、転送ストリームが停滞するのを防ぐことができます (これにより、デッドロックのバグによって問題が発生する可能性がなくなります)。また、B<--debug> がいくつかの特別な I/O 関連メッセージを使用可能にするも許可します。"

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:966
msgid ""
"B<client> - causes all rsync messages to be sent to the client side via the "
"protocol stream.  One client process outputs all messages, with errors on "
"stderr and info messages on stdout.  This B<was> the default in older rsync "
"versions, but can cause error delays when a lot of transfer data is ahead of "
"the messages.  If you're pushing files to an older rsync, you may want to "
"use B<--stderr=all> since that idiom has been around for several releases."
msgstr "B<client> - すべての rsync メッセージを、プロトコル・ストリームを介してクライアント側に送信します。1 つのクライアント・プロセスがすべてのメッセージを出力し、stderr にはエラー、stdout には情報メッセージが出力されます。これはB<古い>バージョンの既定値での rsync でしたが、大量の送信データがメッセージの前にあると、エラー遅延が発生することがあります。ファイルを古い rsync にプッシュしている場合は、B<--stderr=all> を使用することをお勧めします。このイディオムはいくつかのリリースで使用されているためです。"

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:972
msgid ""
"Rsync also accepts the old B<--msgs2stderr> and B<--no-msgs2stderr> options, "
"equivalent to the B<all> and B<client> settings, respectively, and uses them "
"when forwarding non-default settings to the remote side, in case the remote "
"rsync is an older version."
msgstr "また、Rsync は、 B<--msgs2stderr> と B<--no-msgs2stderr> の設定にそれぞれ相当する古い B<all> と B<client> のオプションを受け入れ、リモート rsync が古いバージョンである場合に、デフォルト以外の設定をリモート側に転送するときにこれらのオプションを使用します。"

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:972
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--quiet>, B<-q>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:976
msgid ""
"This option decreases the amount of information you are given during the "
"transfer, notably suppressing information messages from the remote server.  "
"This option is useful when invoking rsync from cron."
msgstr "このオプションを使用すると、転送中に提供される情報の量が減り、特にリモートサーバーからの情報メッセージが抑制されます。このオプションは、cron から rsync を起動するときに便利です。"

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:976
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--no-motd>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:983
msgid ""
"This option affects the information that is output by the client at the "
"start of a daemon transfer.  This suppresses the message-of-the-day (MOTD)  "
"text, but it also affects the list of modules that the daemon sends in "
"response to the \"rsync host::\" request (due to a limitation in the rsync "
"protocol), so omit this option if you want to request the list of modules "
"from the daemon."
msgstr "このオプションは、デーモン転送の開始時にクライアントが出力する情報に影響を与えます。これにより、Message-of-The-Day(MOTD) テキストが抑制されますが、「rsync host::」要求に応答してデーモンが送信するモジュールのリストにも影響します (rsyncプロトコルの制限による)。したがって、デーモンからモジュールのリストを要求する場合は、このオプションを省略してください。"

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:983
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--ignore-times>, B<-I>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:987
msgid ""
"Normally rsync will skip any files that are already the same size and have "
"the same modification timestamp.  This option turns off this \"quick check\" "
"behavior, causing all files to be updated."
msgstr "通常、rsync は、すでに同じサイズで同じ変更タイムスタンプを持つファイルをスキップします。このオプションは、この「クイックチェック」動作をオフにし、すべてのファイルが更新されるようにします。"

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:991
msgid ""
"This option can be confusing compared to B<--ignore-existing> and B<--ignore-"
"non-existing> in that that they cause rsync to transfer fewer files, while "
"this option causes rsync to transfer more files."
msgstr "このオプションは、B<--ignore-existing> および B<--ignore-non-existing> と比較して混乱を招く可能性があります。これらのオプションでは、 rsync が転送するファイルの数が少なくなりますが、このオプションでは、rsync が転送するファイルの数が多くなります。"

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:991
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--size-only>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:998
msgid ""
"This modifies rsync's \"quick check\" algorithm for finding files that need "
"to be transferred, changing it from the default of transferring files with "
"either a changed size or a changed last-modified time to just looking for "
"files that have changed in size.  This is useful when starting to use rsync "
"after using another mirroring system which may not preserve timestamps "
"exactly."
msgstr "これは、転送が必要なファイルを見つけるための rsync の「クイックチェック」アルゴリズムを修正し、変更されたサイズまたは変更された最終変更時刻を持つファイルを転送する既定値から、サイズが変更されたファイルを探すだけに変更します。これは、タイムスタンプを正確に保存しない可能性がある別のミラーリングシステムを使用した後に、rsync の使用を開始する場合に便利です。"

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:998
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--modify-window=NUM>, B<-@>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1007
msgid ""
"When comparing two timestamps, rsync treats the timestamps as being equal if "
"they differ by no more than the modify-window value.  The default is 0, "
"which matches just integer seconds.  If you specify a negative value (and "
"the receiver is at least version 3.1.3, released January 2018)  then "
"nanoseconds will also be taken into account.  Specifying 1 is useful for "
"copies to/from MS Windows FAT filesystems, because FAT represents times with "
"a 2-second resolution (allowing times to differ from the original by up to 1 "
"second)."
msgstr "2 つのタイムスタンプを比較するとき、rsync は、modify-window 値以上の差がなければ、タイムスタンプを等しいものとして扱います。既定値は 0 で、整数の秒と一致します。負の値を指定すると (受信側が 2018 年 1 月にリリースされたバージョン 3.1.3 以上である場合)、ナノ秒も考慮されます。MS Windows FAT ファイルシステムとの間でコピーを行う場合は、1 を指定すると便利です。これは、FAT が 2 秒の精度で時刻を表すためです (元の時刻との差は最大 1 秒まで許容されます)。"

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1010
msgid ""
"If you want all your transfers to default to comparing nanoseconds, you can "
"create a B<~/.popt> file and put these lines in it:"
msgstr "既定値への転送をすべてナノ秒で比較したい場合は、B<~/.popt>ファイルを作成し、その中に次の行を入れます。"

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1015
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"rsync alias -a -a@-1\n"
"rsync alias -t -t@-1\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1021
msgid ""
"With that as the default, you'd need to specify B<--modify-window=0> (aka B<-"
"@0>) to override it and ignore nanoseconds, e.g. if you're copying between "
"ext3 and ext4, or if the receiving rsync is older than 3.1.3."
msgstr "これを既定値とした場合、 B<--modify-window=0> (別名 B<-@0>)から上書きまでを指定し、ナノ秒を無視する必要があります。たとえば、 ext3 と ext4 の間でコピーを行う場合や、受信する rsync が 3.1.3よりも古い場合などです。"

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1021
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--checksum>, B<-c>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1031
msgid ""
"This changes the way rsync checks if the files have been changed and are in "
"need of a transfer.  Without this option, rsync uses a \"quick check\" that "
"(by default) checks if each file's size and time of last modification match "
"between the sender and receiver.  This option changes this to compare a "
"checksum for each file that has a matching size.  Generating the checksums "
"means that both sides will expend a lot of disk I/O reading all the data in "
"the files in the transfer, so this can slow things down significantly (and "
"this is prior to any reading that will be done to transfer changed files)"
msgstr "これにより、ファイルが変更され、転送が必要かどうかを rsync がチェックする方法が変更されます。このオプションなしの場合、rsync は「クイックチェック」を使います。これは (既定値によって) それぞれのファイルのサイズと最終更新時間が送信者と受信者の間で一致しているかどうかをチェックします。このオプションは、サイズが一致する各ファイルのチェックサムを比較するように変更します。チェックサムを生成すると、転送の両側でファイル内のすべてのデータを読み取るために大量のディスク I/O が発生するため、処理速度が大幅に低下する可能性があります (また、これは、変更されたファイルを転送するために行われる読み取りの前に行われます)。"

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1037
msgid ""
"The sending side generates its checksums while it is doing the file-system "
"scan that builds the list of the available files.  The receiver generates "
"its checksums when it is scanning for changed files, and will checksum any "
"file that has the same size as the corresponding sender's file: files with "
"either a changed size or a changed checksum are selected for transfer."
msgstr "送信側は、利用可能なファイルのリストを構築するファイルシステムスキャンを実行している間に、チェックサムを生成します。受信側は、変更されたファイルをスキャンするときにチェックサムを生成し、対応する送信側のファイルと同じサイズを持つファイルのチェックサムを計算します。サイズまたはチェックサムが変更されたファイルが転送用に選択されます。"

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1043
msgid ""
"Note that rsync normally verifies that each I<transferred> file was "
"correctly reconstructed on the receiving side by checking a whole-file "
"checksum that is generated as the file is transferred, but that automatic "
"after-the-transfer verification has nothing to do with this option's before-"
"the-transfer \"Does this file need to be updated?\" check."
msgstr "rsync は通常、ファイル転送時に生成されるファイル全体のチェックサムをチェックすることによって、各I<転送された>ファイルが受信側で正しく再構築されたことを検証しますが、転送後の自動検証は、このオプションの転送前の「このファイルを更新する必要がありますか?」チェックとは何の関係もありません。"

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1048
msgid ""
"The checksum used is auto-negotiated between the client and the server, but "
"can be overridden using either the B<--checksum-choice> (B<--cc>)  option or "
"an environment variable that is discussed in that option's section."
msgstr "使用されるチェックサムは、クライアントとサーバー間で自動ネゴシエートされますが、B<--checksum-choice> (B<--cc>) オプション、またはこのオプションのセクションで説明されている環境変数を使用して上書きできます。"

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1048
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--archive>, B<-a>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1053
msgid ""
"This is equivalent to B<-rlptgoD>.  It is a quick way of saying you want "
"recursion and want to preserve almost everything.  Be aware that it does "
"B<not> include preserving ACLs (B<-A>), xattrs (B<-X>), atimes (B<-U>), "
"crtimes (B<-N>), nor the finding and preserving of hardlinks (B<-H>)."
msgstr "これは、B<-rlptgoD> と同じです。これは、再帰が必要で、ほぼすべてを保持したいことを簡単に伝える方法です。ACL  (B<-A>)、xattrs(B<-X>)、atimes  (B<-U>)、crtimes  (B<-N>) の保持は含まれないことに注意してください。また、ハードリンク (B<-H>) の検索と保持も含まれません。"

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1056
msgid ""
"The only exception to the above equivalence is when B<--files-from> is "
"specified, in which case B<-r> is not implied."
msgstr "上記の等価性に対する唯一の例外は、B<--files-from> が指定された場合です。この場合、B<-r> は暗黙指定されません。"

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1056
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--no-OPTION>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1064
msgid ""
"You may turn off one or more implied options by prefixing the option name "
"with \"no-\".  Not all positive options have a negated opposite, but a lot "
"do, including those that can be used to disable an implied option (e.g.  B<--"
"no-D>, B<--no-perms>) or have different defaults in various circumstances (e."
"g. B<--no-whole-file>, B<--no-blocking-io>, B<--no-dirs>).  Every valid "
"negated option accepts both the short and the long option name after the "
"\"no-\" prefix (e.g. B<--no-R> is the same as B<--no-relative>)."
msgstr "オプション名の前に「no-」を付けると、1つまたは複数の暗黙のオプションをオフにできます。すべての肯定的なオプションが否定された反対のものを持っているわけではありませんが、暗黙のオプションを無効にするために使用できるもの (例: B<--no-D>、 B<--no-perms>) や、さまざまな状況で異なる既定値を持つもの (例: B<--no-whole-file>、 B<--no-blocking-io>、 B<--no-dirs>) を含め、多くのものに否定のものがあります。全ての有効な否定オプションは、\"no-\" プレフィックスの後に短いオプション名と長いオプション名の両方を受け付けます(例えば、B<--no-R>は B<--no-relative> と同じです)。"

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1068
msgid ""
"As an example, if you want to use B<--archive> (B<-a>) but don't want B<--"
"owner> (B<-o>), instead of converting B<-a> into B<-rlptgD>, you can specify "
"B<-a\\ --no-o> (aka B<--archive\\ --no-owner>)."
msgstr "たとえば、 B<--archive>  (B<-a>) を使用したいが B<--owner> (B<-o>) は使用したくない場合、B<-a> を B<-rlptgD> に変換する代わりに、 B<-a\\ --no-o> (aka B<--archive\\ --no-owner>) を指定できます。"

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1075
msgid ""
"The order of the options is important: if you specify B<--no-r\\ -a>, the B<-"
"r> option would end up being turned on, the opposite of B<-a\\ --no-r>.  "
"Note also that the side-effects of the B<--files-from> option are NOT "
"positional, as it affects the default state of several options and slightly "
"changes the meaning of B<-a> (see the B<--files-from> option for more "
"details)."
msgstr "オプションの順序は重要です。「 B<--no-r\\ -a>」を指定すると、「 B<-r> 」オプションは「B<-a\\ --no-r>」とは逆にオンになります。また、 B<--files-from> オプションの副作用は位置的なものではないことにも注意してください。これは、いくつかのオプションの既定値状態に影響し、 B<-a> の意味をわずかに変更するためです(詳細については、 B<--files-from> オプションを参照してください)。"

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1075
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--recursive>, B<-r>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1079
msgid ""
"This tells rsync to copy directories recursively.  See also B<--dirs> (B<-"
"d>) for an option that allows the scanning of a single directory."
msgstr "これは rsync にディレクトリを再帰的にコピーするように伝えます。単一ディレクトリのスキャンを許可するオプションについては、B<--dirs> (B<-d>) も参照してください。"

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1082
msgid ""
"See the B<--inc-recursive> option for a discussion of the incremental "
"recursion for creating the list of files to transfer."
msgstr "転送するファイルのリストを作成するための増分再帰の説明については、B<--inc-recursive> オプションを参照してください。"

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1082
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--inc-recursive>, B<--i-r>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1087
msgid ""
"This option explicitly enables incremental recursion when scanning for "
"files, which is enabled by default when using the B<--recursive> option and "
"both sides of the transfer are running rsync 3.0.0 (released March 2008) or "
"newer."
msgstr "このオプションは、ファイルのスキャン時に増分反復を明示的に有効にします。これは、B<--recursive> オプションを使用し、転送の両側で rsync 3.0.0 ( 2008 年 3 月リリース) 以降を実行している場合に、既定値によって有効にされます。"

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1092
msgid ""
"Incremental recursion uses much less memory than non-incremental, while also "
"beginning the transfer more quickly (since it doesn't need to scan the "
"entire transfer hierarchy before it starts transferring files).  If no "
"recursion is enabled in the source files, this option has no effect."
msgstr "インクリメンタル再帰では、非インクリメンタル再帰に比べて使用するメモリ量がはるかに少なくなります。また、転送をより迅速に開始できます (ファイル転送を開始する前に転送階層全体をスキャンする必要がないため)。ソースファイルで再帰が有効になっていない場合、このオプションは効果がありません。"

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1095
msgid ""
"Some options require rsync to know the full file list, so these options "
"disable the incremental recursion mode.  These include:"
msgstr "いくつかのオプションは、完全なファイルリストを知るために rsync を必要とするので、これらのオプションは増分再帰モードを無効にします。次のようなものが含まれています:"

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1099
msgid "B<--delete-before> (the old default of B<--delete>)"
msgstr "B<--delete-before> (B<--delete> の旧既定値)"

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1101 original/man1/rsync.1:2287
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--delete-after>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1103
msgid "B<--prune-empty-dirs>"
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1105 original/man1/rsync.1:3817
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--delay-updates>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1109
msgid ""
"In order to be compatible with incremental recursion, B<--delete-during> is "
"the default delete mode for B<--delete>."
msgstr "増分反復と互換性を持たせるために、B<--delete-during> は B<--delete> の既定値削除モードです。"

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1120
msgid ""
"One side-effect of incremental recursion is that any missing sub-directories "
"inside a recursively-scanned directory are (by default)  created prior to "
"recursing into the sub-directories.  This earlier creation point (compared "
"to a non-incremental recursion) allows rsync to then set the modify time of "
"the finished directory right away (without having to delay that until a "
"bunch of recursive copying has finished).  However, these early directories "
"don't yet have their completed mode, mtime, or ownership set\\ -- they have "
"more restrictive rights until the subdirectory's copying actually begins.  "
"This early-creation idiom can be avoided by using the B<--omit-dir-times> "
"option."
msgstr "漸進再帰の副作用の 1 つは、再帰的にスキャンされたディレクトリ内の欠落したサブディレクトリが、サブディレクトリに再帰する前に (既定値によって) 作成されることです。この (非インクリメンタル再帰と比較して) より早い作成ポイントにより、rsync は終了したディレクトリの修正時間をすぐに設定することができます (一連の再帰的なコピーが終了するまで遅延させる必要はありません)。ただし、これらの初期ディレクトリには、まだ完全なモード、mtime、または所有権が設定されていません。これらのディレクトリは、サブディレクトリのコピーが実際に開始されるまで、より制限的な権限を持っています。この初期作成のイディオムは、B<--omit-dir-times> オプションを使用することで回避できます。"

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1123
msgid ""
"Incremental recursion can be disabled using the B<--no-inc-recursive> (B<--"
"no-i-r>) option."
msgstr "増分再帰を無効にするには、B<--no-inc-recursive>(B<--no-i-r> オプションを使用します。"

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1123
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--no-inc-recursive>, B<--no-i-r>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1128
msgid ""
"Disables the incremental recursion algorithm of the B<--recursive> option.  "
"This makes rsync scan the full file list before it begins to transfer "
"files.  See B<--inc-recursive> for more info."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1128
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--relative>, B<-R>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1133
msgid ""
"Use relative paths.  This means that the full path names specified on the "
"command line are sent to the server rather than just the last parts of the "
"filenames.  This is particularly useful when you want to send several "
"different directories at the same time.  For example, this command:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1137
#, no-wrap
msgid "rsync -av /foo/bar/baz.c remote:/tmp/\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1142
msgid ""
"would create a file named baz.c in /tmp/ on the remote machine.  If instead "
"you used"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1146
#, no-wrap
msgid "rsync -avR /foo/bar/baz.c remote:/tmp/\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1153
msgid ""
"then a file named /tmp/foo/bar/baz.c would be created on the remote machine, "
"preserving its full path.  These extra path elements are called \"implied "
"directories\" (i.e. the \"foo\" and the \"foo/bar\" directories in the above "
"example)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1162
msgid ""
"Rsync always sends these implied directories as real directories in the file "
"list, even if a path element is really a symlink on the sending side.  This "
"prevents some really unexpected behaviors when copying the full path of a "
"file that has a symlink in its path.  If you want to duplicate a server-side "
"symlink, include both the symlink via its path, and the referent directory "
"via its real path.  If you're dealing with an older rsync on the sending "
"side, you may need to use the B<--no-implied-dirs> option."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1168
msgid ""
"It is also possible to limit the amount of path information that is sent as "
"implied directories for each path you specify.  With a modern rsync on the "
"sending side (beginning with 2.6.7, released March 2006), you can insert a "
"dot and a slash into the source path, like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1172
#, no-wrap
msgid "rsync -avR /foo/./bar/baz.c remote:/tmp/\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1179
msgid ""
"That would create /tmp/bar/baz.c on the receiving machine. (Note that the "
"dot must be followed by a slash, so \"/foo/.\" would not be abbreviated.) "
"For older rsync versions, you would need to change directory to limit the "
"source path.  For example, when pushing files:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1183
#, no-wrap
msgid "(cd /foo; rsync -avR bar/baz.c remote:/tmp/)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1190
msgid ""
"(Note that the parentheses put the two commands into a sub-shell, so that "
"the \"cd\" command doesn't remain in effect for future commands.) If you're "
"pulling files from an older rsync, use this idiom (but only for a non-daemon "
"transfer):"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1195
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"rsync -avR --rsync-path=\"cd /foo; rsync\" \\e\n"
"     remote:bar/baz.c /tmp/\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1197
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--no-implied-dirs>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1205
msgid ""
"This option affects the default behavior of the B<--relative> option.  When "
"it is specified, the attributes of the implied directories from the source "
"names are not included in the transfer.  This means that the corresponding "
"path elements on the destination system are left unchanged if they exist, "
"and any missing implied directories are created with default attributes.  "
"This even allows these implied path elements to have big differences, such "
"as being a symlink to a directory on the receiving side."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1222
msgid ""
"For instance, if a command-line argument or a files-from entry told rsync to "
"transfer the file \"path/foo/file\", the directories \"path\" and \"path/"
"foo\" are implied when B<--relative> is used.  If \"path/foo\" is a symlink "
"to \"bar\" on the destination system, the receiving rsync would ordinarily "
"delete \"path/foo\", recreate it as a directory, and receive the file into "
"the new directory.  With B<--no-implied-dirs>, the receiving rsync leaves "
"the existing \"path/foo\" symlink in place and follows it, so the file is "
"written through the symlink and ends up in \"path/bar\".  Note the security "
"implication: a pre-existing in-tree symlink-to-directory on the receiving "
"side will redirect where the file is written, so only use B<--no-implied-"
"dirs> when you trust the destination's existing path elements.  (A symlink "
"whose target is absolute or escapes the destination tree is still refused by "
"the secure path resolver rather than followed.)  Another way to accomplish "
"this link preservation is to use the B<--keep-dirlinks> option (which will "
"also affect symlinks to directories in the rest of the transfer)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1227
msgid ""
"When pulling files from an rsync older than 3.0.0 (March 2008), you may need "
"to use this option if the sending side has a symlink in the path you request "
"and you wish the implied directories to be transferred as normal directories."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1227
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--backup>, B<-b>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1233
msgid ""
"With this option, preexisting destination files are renamed as each file is "
"transferred or deleted (that is, each file to be deleted or overwritten gets "
"renamed instead).  You can control where the backup file goes and what (if "
"any) suffix gets appended using the B<--backup-dir> and B<--suffix> options."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1235
msgid "If you don't specify B<--backup-dir>:"
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1237 original/man1/rsync.1:3706
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3772 original/man1/rsync.1:3799
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3839 original/man1/rsync.1:3996
#, no-wrap
msgid "1."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1239
msgid "the B<--omit-dir-times> option will be forced on"
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1239 original/man1/rsync.1:3710
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3774 original/man1/rsync.1:3802
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3843 original/man1/rsync.1:3999
#, no-wrap
msgid "2."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1245
msgid ""
"the use of B<--delete> (without B<--delete-excluded>), causes rsync to add a "
"\"protect\" filter-rule for the backup suffix to the end of all your "
"existing filters that looks like this: B<-f\\ \"P\\ *~\">.  This rule "
"prevents previously backed-up files from being deleted."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1252
msgid ""
"Note that if you are supplying your own filter rules, you may need to "
"manually insert your own exclude/protect rule somewhere higher up in the "
"list so that it has a high enough priority to be effective (e.g. if your "
"rules specify a trailing inclusion/exclusion of B<*>, the auto-added rule "
"would never be reached)."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1252
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--backup-dir=DIR>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1258
msgid ""
"This implies the B<--backup> option, and tells rsync to store all backups in "
"the specified directory on the receiving side.  This can be used for "
"incremental backups.  You can additionally specify a backup suffix using the "
"B<--suffix> option (otherwise the files backed up in the specified directory "
"will keep their original filenames)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1265
msgid ""
"Note that if you specify a relative path, the backup directory will be "
"relative to the destination directory, so you probably want to specify "
"either an absolute path or a path that starts with \"../\".  If an rsync "
"daemon is the receiver, the backup directory cannot go outside the module's "
"path hierarchy, so take extra care not to delete or copy into the backup "
"directory inadvertently."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1265
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--suffix=SUFFIX>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1269
msgid ""
"This option allows you to override the default backup suffix used with the "
"B<--backup> (B<-b>) option.  The default suffix is a B<~> if no B<--backup-"
"dir> was specified, otherwise it is an empty string."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1269
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--update>, B<-u>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1274
msgid ""
"This forces rsync to skip any files which exist on the destination and have "
"a modified time that is newer than the source file. (If an existing "
"destination file has a modification time equal to the source file's, it will "
"be updated if the sizes are different.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1281
msgid ""
"Note that this does not affect the copying of directories, symlinks, or "
"other special files.  Also, a difference of object type between the sender "
"and receiver is always considered to be important enough for an update, no "
"matter what date is on the objects.  For example, if the source has a "
"directory where the destination has a file, the transfer would occur "
"regardless of the timestamps."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1284 original/man1/rsync.1:2174
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2181 original/man1/rsync.1:2364
msgid ""
"This option is a TRANSFER RULE, so don't expect any exclude side effects."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1291
msgid ""
"A caution for those that choose to combine B<--inplace> with B<--update>: an "
"interrupted transfer will leave behind a partial file on the receiving side "
"that has a very recent modified time, so re-running the transfer will "
"probably B<not> continue the interrupted file.  As such, it is usually best "
"to avoid combining this with B<--inplace> unless you have implemented manual "
"steps to handle any interrupted in-progress files."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1291
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--inplace>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1296
msgid ""
"This option changes how rsync transfers a file when its data needs to be "
"updated: instead of the default method of creating a new copy of the file "
"and moving it into place when it is complete, rsync instead writes the "
"updated data directly to the destination file."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1298
msgid "This has several effects:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1306
msgid ""
"Hard links are not broken.  This means the new data will be visible through "
"other hard links to the destination file.  Moreover, attempts to copy "
"differing source files onto a multiply-linked destination file will result "
"in a \"tug of war\" with the destination data changing back and forth."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1310
msgid ""
"In-use binaries cannot be updated (either the OS will prevent this from "
"happening, or binaries that attempt to swap-in their data will misbehave or "
"crash)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1314
msgid ""
"The file's data will be in an inconsistent state during the transfer and "
"will be left that way if the transfer is interrupted or if an update fails."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1318
msgid ""
"A file that rsync cannot write to cannot be updated.  While a super user can "
"update any file, a normal user needs to be granted write permission for the "
"open of the file for writing to be successful."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1324
msgid ""
"The efficiency of rsync's delta-transfer algorithm may be reduced if some "
"data in the destination file is overwritten before it can be copied to a "
"position later in the file.  This does not apply if you use B<--backup>, "
"since rsync is smart enough to use the backup file as the basis file for the "
"transfer."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1328
msgid ""
"WARNING: you should not use this option to update files that are being "
"accessed by others, so be careful when choosing to use this for a copy."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1333
msgid ""
"This option is useful for transferring large files with block-based changes "
"or appended data, and also on systems that are disk bound, not network "
"bound.  It can also help keep a copy-on-write filesystem snapshot from "
"diverging the entire contents of a file that only has minor changes."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1337
msgid ""
"The option implies B<--partial> (since an interrupted transfer does not "
"delete the file), but conflicts with B<--partial-dir> and B<--delay-updates>."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1337
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--append>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1344
msgid ""
"This special copy mode is only applicable to update files that are known to "
"be growing larger, that is, where any existing content on the receiving side "
"is also known to be the same as the content on the sender.  The use of B<--"
"append> B<can be dangerous> if you aren't 100% sure that all the files in "
"the transfer are shared, growing files.  You should thus use filter rules to "
"ensure that you weed out any files that do not fit this criteria."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1352
msgid ""
"Rsync updates these growing file in-place without verifying any of the "
"existing content in the file (it only verifies the content that it is "
"appending).  Rsync skips any files that exist on the receiving side that are "
"not shorter than the associated file on the sending side.  It also skips any "
"files whose size on the sending side gets shorter during the send "
"negotiations (rsync warns about a \"diminished\" file when this happens)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1357
msgid ""
"This does not interfere with the updating of a file's non-content attributes "
"(e.g.  permissions, ownership, etc.) when the file does not need to be "
"transferred, nor does it affect the updating of any directories or non-"
"regular files."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1357
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--append-verify>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1363
msgid ""
"This special copy mode works like B<--append> except that all the data in "
"the file is included in the checksum verification, making it less efficient "
"but also potentially safer.  This option B<can be dangerous> if you aren't "
"100% sure that all the files in the transfer are shared, growing files.  See "
"the B<--append> option for more details."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1368
msgid ""
"Note: prior to rsync 3.0.0 (March 2008), the B<--append> option worked like "
"B<--append-verify>, so if you are interacting with an older rsync (or the "
"transfer is using a protocol prior to 30), specifying either append option "
"will initiate an B<--append-verify> transfer."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1368
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--dirs>, B<-d>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1376
msgid ""
"Tell the sending side to include any directories that are encountered.  "
"Unlike B<--recursive>, a directory's contents are not copied unless the "
"directory name specified is \".\" or ends with a trailing slash (e.g.  \"."
"\", \"dir/.\", \"dir/\", etc.).  Without this option or the B<--recursive> "
"option, rsync will skip all directories it encounters (and output a message "
"to that effect for each one).  If you specify both B<--dirs> and B<--"
"recursive>, B<--recursive> takes precedence."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1382
msgid ""
"The B<--dirs> option is implied by the B<--files-from> option or the B<--"
"list-only> option (including an implied B<--list-only> usage) if B<--"
"recursive> wasn't specified (so that directories are seen in the listing).  "
"Specify B<--no-dirs> (or B<--no-d>) if you want to turn this off."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1384
msgid "See also the backward-compatibility helper option B<--old-dirs>."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1384
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--old-dirs>, B<--old-d>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1388
msgid ""
"This backward-compatibility helper tells rsync to use a hack of B<-r\\ --"
"exclude='/*/*'> to get an older remote rsync to list a single directory "
"without recursing."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1388
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--mkpath>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1390
msgid "Create all missing path components of the destination path."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1397
msgid ""
"By default, rsync will create only the final component of the destination "
"path if it does not exist.  If any other component does not exist that is an "
"error, as this can help to catch mistakes in the destination path "
"specification.  With this option, rsync creates all the missing destination-"
"path components, just as if B<mkdir\\ -p\\ $DEST_PATH> had been run on the "
"receiving side."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1403
msgid ""
"When specifying a destination path, including a trailing slash ensures that "
"the whole path is treated as directory names to be created, even when the "
"file list has a single item. See the COPYING TO A DIFFERENT NAME section for "
"full details on how rsync decides if a final destination-path component "
"should be created as a directory or not."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1409
msgid ""
"If you would like the newly-created destination directories to match the "
"directories on the sending side, you should be using B<--relative> (B<-R>) "
"instead of B<--mkpath>.  For instance, the following two commands result in "
"the same destination tree, but only the second command ensures that the "
"\"some/extra/path\" components match the directories on the sending side:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1414
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"rsync -ai --mkpath host:some/extra/path/*.c some/extra/path/\n"
"rsync -aiR host:some/extra/path/*.c ./\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1416
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--links>, B<-l>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1420
msgid ""
"Add symlinks to the transferred files instead of noisily ignoring them with "
"a \"non-regular file\" warning for each symlink encountered.  You can "
"alternatively silence the warning by specifying B<--info=nonreg0>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1423
msgid ""
"The default handling of symlinks is to recreate each symlink's unchanged "
"value on the receiving side."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1425 original/man1/rsync.1:1441
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1464 original/man1/rsync.1:1484
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1514 original/man1/rsync.1:1611
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1637
msgid "See the SYMBOLIC LINKS section for multi-option info."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1425
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--copy-links>, B<-L>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1430
msgid ""
"The sender transforms each symlink encountered in the transfer into the "
"referent item, following the symlink chain to the file or directory that it "
"references.  If a symlink chain is broken, an error is output and the file "
"is dropped from the transfer."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1433
msgid ""
"This option supersedes any other options that affect symlinks in the "
"transfer, since there are no symlinks left in the transfer."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1436
msgid ""
"This option does not change the handling of existing symlinks on the "
"receiving side."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1439
msgid ""
"See the B<--keep-dirlinks> (B<-K>) if you need a symlink to a directory to "
"be treated as a real directory on the receiving side."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1441 original/man1/rsync.1:5117
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--copy-unsafe-links>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1446
msgid ""
"This tells rsync to copy the referent of symbolic links that point outside "
"the copied tree.  Absolute symlinks are also treated like ordinary files, "
"and so are any symlinks in the source path itself when B<--relative> is used."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1458
msgid ""
"A symlink is judged unsafe by a lexical test on its value, without resolving "
"it on disk.  An absolute or empty target is always unsafe.  A relative "
"target is unsafe if its \"..\" components would climb above the top of the "
"transfer; a \"..\" that appears anywhere other than as a leading prefix (an "
"embedded or trailing \"/..\") is also treated as unsafe.  The top is set by "
"how the source is specified: a trailing slash on the source (e.g.  \"/src/"
"subdir/\" copied to \"/dest/subdir\") makes that directory itself the top, "
"so a symlink may not point above it; without the trailing slash (e.g.  \"/"
"src/subdir\" copied to \"/dest/\") the source's parent (\"/src\") is the "
"top, so \"subdir\" is a name inside the transfer and a relative symlink may "
"point to any other name within it."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1462
msgid ""
"Note that safe symlinks are only copied if B<--links> was also specified or "
"implied. The B<--copy-unsafe-links> option has no extra effect when combined "
"with B<--copy-links>."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1464
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--safe-links>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1468
msgid ""
"This tells the receiving rsync to ignore any symbolic links in the transfer "
"which point outside the copied tree.  All absolute symlinks are also ignored."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1474
msgid ""
"Since this ignoring is happening on the receiving side, it will still be "
"effective even when the sending side has munged symlinks (when it is using "
"B<--munge-links>). It also affects deletions, since the file being present "
"in the transfer prevents any matching file on the receiver from being "
"deleted when the symlink is deemed to be unsafe and is skipped."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1479
msgid ""
"This option has no effect unless it is combined with B<--links> (or an "
"option that implies B<--links> such as B<--archive>), because without B<--"
"links> there will not be any symlinks in the transfer.  Its effect is "
"superseded by B<--copy-unsafe-links>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1482
msgid ""
"Using this option in conjunction with B<--relative> may give unexpected "
"results."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1484
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--insecure-links>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1499
msgid ""
"By default rsync resolves the directory and file paths that the B<operator> "
"supplies on the command line with a defensive directory-tree walk that "
"B<follows a symlink component only when it is owned by uid\\ 0 or by the "
"running user>, and refuses one owned by any other user.  This stops an "
"attacker who can write inside one of those directories from planting a "
"symlink that redirects a privileged rsync to a target outside the intended "
"tree, while still honouring the operator's own symlinks.  The rule is the "
"same for absolute and relative paths.  This applies to the destination "
"directory and to the parameters to the following options: B<--backup-dir>, "
"B<--temp-dir>/B<-T>, B<--partial-dir>, B<--link-dest>, B<--compare-dest>, "
"B<--copy-dest>, B<--log-file>, B<--password-file>, B<--files-from>, B<--"
"include-from>, B<--exclude-from>, B<--filter> merge files, B<--write-batch> "
"and B<--read-batch>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1504
msgid ""
"B<--insecure-links> turns that defence off, restoring the historical "
"behaviour of following any symlink in those paths.  Use it only when you "
"fully trust every directory along each operator-supplied path, as it re-"
"exposes the symlink-redirection attacks the walk prevents."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1512
msgid ""
"The option is B<local only>: it is not sent to the remote side of the "
"transfer.  A remote-shell peer that needs the opt-out must set it there, e."
"g.  via B<--rsync-path>), and a daemon never honours it\\ -- a daemon that "
"receives B<--insecure-links> from a client refuses the request.  A daemon "
"administrator who wants the legacy behaviour for a single trusted module "
"sets \"B<insecure\\ links\\ =\\ yes>\" in that module's B<rsyncd.conf>(5) "
"section instead; a client can never enable it."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1514
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--confine-root=DIR>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1522
msgid ""
"In the default symlink mode this bounds where operator option paths and "
"paths selected from a B<--files-from> list are allowed to resolve: one that "
"ends up outside DIR is refused, even if every symlink along it was owned by "
"a trusted user. Explicit source roots remain operator-selected transfer "
"roots.  The ownership walk asks who planted a link; this asks where the path "
"came out."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1525
msgid ""
"DIR must be absolute.  Nothing is confined by default, and B<--confine-root=/"
"> is a no-op."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1536
msgid ""
"It exists for a wrapper that serves a restricted directory over a remote "
"shell, B<rrsync> being the one shipped here, which passes it automatically "
"whenever its restricted dir is not \"B</>\".  Such a wrapper can vet the "
"argv it is handed, but filter rules travel over the protocol instead: a "
"client can name a merge file outside the restricted dir in a dir-merge rule "
"and have the server read it in as filter rules.  On a pull that needs "
"neither B<--delete> nor any verbosity, because an exclude-only merge (the "
"\"B<->\" modifier) makes every line a pattern, and the client reads the "
"file's contents off which of its own names went missing.  Confining the open "
"is what closes that; a merge file inside DIR keeps working as before."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1540
msgid ""
"A daemon ignores this option\\ -- its module directory is already the "
"boundary, and the option arrives in a client-supplied argv, so honouring it "
"could only widen the module."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1544
msgid ""
"B<--insecure-links> is refused alongside it.  That opt-out restores the "
"historical open, which skips the walk that enforces the root, so the two "
"together would silently mean no confinement at all."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1547
msgid ""
"Like B<--drop-D>, it is not forwarded to the remote side: it is meant to be "
"applied to one end of a connection by itself."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1547
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--munge-links>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1555
msgid ""
"This option affects just one side of the transfer and tells rsync to munge "
"symlink values when it is receiving files or unmunge symlink values when it "
"is sending files.  \"Munging\" changes the symlink target so that the "
"symlink cannot be followed successfully, but allows the original target of "
"the symlink to be recovered. \"Unmunging\" reverses this process so that the "
"symlink points to the original target."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1564
msgid ""
"This option can be enabled on the server side without the client's "
"knowledge, such as in an rsync daemon's configuration file or by an option "
"given to the rrsync (restricted rsync) script.  When specified on the client "
"side, specify the option normally if it is the client side that has or needs "
"the munged symlinks, or use B<-M--munge-links> to give the option to the "
"server when it has or needs the munged symlinks.  Note that on a local "
"transfer, the client is the sender, so specifying the option directly "
"unmunges symlinks while specifying it as a remote option munges symlinks."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1568
msgid ""
"This option has no effect when sent to a daemon via B<--remote-option> "
"because the daemon configures whether it wants munged symlinks via its "
"\"B<munge\\ symlinks>\" parameter."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1576
msgid ""
"The symlink value is munged or unmunged once it is in the transfer, so any "
"option that transforms symlinks into non-symlinks occurs prior to the "
"munging/unmunging B<except> for B<--safe-links>, which is a choice that the "
"receiver makes, so it bases its decision on the munged/unmunged value.  This "
"does mean that if a receiver has munging enabled, using B<--safe-links> will "
"cause all symlinks to be ignored (since munging makes them all absolute)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1585
msgid ""
"The method that rsync uses to munge a symlink is to prefix its value with "
"the string \"/rsyncd-munged/\".  This prevents the link from being used, "
"provided that the /rsyncd-munged directory does not exist.  When this option "
"is enabled, rsync will refuse to run if that path is a directory or a "
"symlink to a directory (though it only checks at startup).  See also the "
"\"munge-symlinks\" python script in the support directory of the source code "
"for a way to munge/unmunge one or more symlinks in-place."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1585
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--copy-dirlinks>, B<-k>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1590
msgid ""
"This option causes the sending side to treat a symlink to a directory as "
"though it were a real directory.  This is useful if you don't want symlinks "
"to non-directories to be affected, as they would be using B<--copy-links>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1595
msgid ""
"Without this option, if the sending side has replaced a directory with a "
"symlink to a directory, the receiving side will delete anything that is in "
"the way of the new symlink, including a directory hierarchy (as long as B<--"
"force> or B<--delete> is in effect)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1598
msgid ""
"See also B<--keep-dirlinks> for an analogous option for the receiving side."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1603
msgid ""
"B<--copy-dirlinks> applies to all symlinks to directories in the source.  If "
"you want to follow only a few specified symlinks, a trick you can use is to "
"pass them as additional source arguments with a trailing slash, using B<--"
"relative> to make the paths match up right.  For example:"
msgstr "B<--copy-dirlinks> applies to all symlinks to directories in the source.  If you want to follow only a few specified symlinks, a trick you can use is to pass them as additional source arguments with a trailing slash, using B<--relative> to make the paths match up right.  例:"

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1607
#, no-wrap
msgid "rsync -r --relative src/./ src/./follow-me/ dest/\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1611
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--keep-dirlinks>, B<-K>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1616
msgid ""
"This option causes the receiving side to treat a symlink to a directory as "
"though it were a real directory, but only if it matches a real directory "
"from the sender.  Without this option, the receiver's symlink would be "
"deleted and replaced with a real directory."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1623
msgid ""
"For example, suppose you transfer a directory \"foo\" that contains a file "
"\"file\", but on the receiver, \"foo\" is a symlink to directory \"bar\".  "
"Without B<--keep-dirlinks>, the receiver deletes symlink \"foo\", recreates "
"it as a directory, and receives the file into the new directory.  With B<--"
"keep-dirlinks>, the receiver keeps the symlink and \"file\" ends up in "
"\"bar\"."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1632
msgid ""
"One note of caution: if you use B<--keep-dirlinks>, you must either trust "
"all the symlinks in the copy, or enable the B<--munge-links> option on the "
"receiving side! If it is possible for an untrusted user to create their own "
"symlink to any real directory, the user could then (on a subsequent copy) "
"replace the symlink with a real directory and affect the content of whatever "
"directory the symlink references.  For backup copies, you are better off "
"using something like a bind mount instead of a symlink to modify your "
"receiving hierarchy."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1635
msgid ""
"See also B<--copy-dirlinks> for an analogous option for the sending side."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1637
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--hard-links>, B<-H>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1642
msgid ""
"This tells rsync to look for hard-linked files in the source and link "
"together the corresponding files on the destination.  Without this option, "
"hard-linked files in the source are treated as though they were separate "
"files."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1646
msgid ""
"This option does NOT necessarily ensure that the pattern of hard links on "
"the destination exactly matches that on the source.  Cases in which the "
"destination may end up with extra hard links include the following:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1654
msgid ""
"If the destination contains extraneous hard-links (more linking than what is "
"present in the source file list), the copying algorithm will not break them "
"explicitly.  However, if one or more of the paths have content differences, "
"the normal file-update process will break those extra links (unless you are "
"using the B<--inplace> option)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1659
msgid ""
"If you specify a B<--link-dest> directory that contains hard links, the "
"linking of the destination files against the B<--link-dest> files can cause "
"some paths in the destination to become linked together due to the B<--link-"
"dest> associations."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1668
msgid ""
"Note that rsync can only detect hard links between files that are inside the "
"transfer set.  If rsync updates a file that has extra hard-link connections "
"to files outside the transfer, that linkage will be broken.  If you are "
"tempted to use the B<--inplace> option to avoid this breakage, be very "
"careful that you know how your files are being updated so that you are "
"certain that no unintended changes happen due to lingering hard links (and "
"see the B<--inplace> option for more caveats)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1678
msgid ""
"If incremental recursion is active (see B<--inc-recursive>), rsync may "
"transfer a missing hard-linked file before it finds that another link for "
"that contents exists elsewhere in the hierarchy.  This does not affect the "
"accuracy of the transfer (i.e. which files are hard-linked together), just "
"its efficiency (i.e. copying the data for a new, early copy of a hard-linked "
"file that could have been found later in the transfer in another member of "
"the hard-linked set of files).  One way to avoid this inefficiency is to "
"disable incremental recursion using the B<--no-inc-recursive> option."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1678
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--perms>, B<-p>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1683
msgid ""
"This option causes the receiving rsync to set the destination permissions to "
"be the same as the source permissions. (See also the B<--chmod> option for a "
"way to modify what rsync considers to be the source permissions.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1685
msgid "When this option is I<off>, permissions are set as follows:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1691
msgid ""
"Existing files (including updated files) retain their existing permissions, "
"though the B<--executability> option might change just the execute "
"permission for the file."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1698
msgid ""
"New files get their \"normal\" permission bits set to the source file's "
"permissions masked with the receiving directory's default permissions "
"(either the receiving process's umask, or the permissions specified via the "
"destination directory's default ACL), and their special permission bits "
"disabled except in the case where a new directory inherits a setgid bit from "
"its parent directory."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1703
msgid ""
"Thus, when B<--perms> and B<--executability> are both disabled, rsync's "
"behavior is the same as that of other file-copy utilities, such as B<cp>(1)  "
"and B<tar>(1)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1713
msgid ""
"In summary: to give destination files (both old and new) the source "
"permissions, use B<--perms>.  To give new files the destination-default "
"permissions (while leaving existing files unchanged), make sure that the B<--"
"perms> option is off and use B<--chmod=ugo=rwX> (which ensures that all non-"
"masked bits get enabled).  If you'd care to make this latter behavior easier "
"to type, you could define a popt alias for it, such as putting this line in "
"the file B<~/.popt> (the following defines the B<-Z> option, and includes "
"B<--no-g> to use the default group of the destination directory):"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1717
#, no-wrap
msgid "rsync alias -Z --no-p --no-g --chmod=ugo=rwX\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1721
msgid "You could then use this new option in a command such as this one:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1725
#, no-wrap
msgid "rsync -avZ src/ dest/\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1730
msgid ""
"(Caveat: make sure that B<-a> does not follow B<-Z>, or it will re-enable "
"the two B<--no-*> options mentioned above.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1734
msgid ""
"Note that if the remote rsync is older than 2.6.7 (March 2006) and it is the "
"receiver, the behavior when B<--perms> is off may differ from that described "
"above."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1734
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--executability>, B<-E>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1741
msgid ""
"This option causes rsync to preserve the executability or non-executability "
"of regular files when B<--perms> is not enabled.  A regular file is "
"considered to be executable if at least one 'x' is turned on in its "
"permissions.  When an existing destination file's executability differs from "
"that of the corresponding source file, rsync modifies the destination file's "
"permissions as follows:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1745
msgid "To make a file non-executable, rsync turns off all its 'x' permissions."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1748
msgid ""
"To make a file executable, rsync turns on each 'x' permission that has a "
"corresponding 'r' permission enabled."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1751
msgid "If B<--perms> is enabled, this option is ignored."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1751
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--acls>, B<-A>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1754
msgid ""
"This option causes rsync to update the destination ACLs to be the same as "
"the source ACLs.  The option also implies B<--perms>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1758
msgid ""
"The source and destination systems must have compatible ACL entries for this "
"option to work properly.  See the B<--fake-super> option for a way to backup "
"and restore ACLs that are not compatible."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1758
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--xattrs>, B<-X>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1761
msgid ""
"This option causes rsync to update the destination extended attributes to be "
"the same as the source ones."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1766
msgid ""
"For systems that support extended-attribute namespaces, a copy being done by "
"a super-user copies all namespaces except system.*.  A normal user only "
"copies the user.* namespace.  To be able to backup and restore non-user "
"namespaces as a normal user, see the B<--fake-super> option."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1773
msgid ""
"The above name filtering can be overridden by using one or more filter "
"options with the B<x> modifier.  When you specify an xattr-affecting filter "
"rule, rsync requires that you do your own system/user filtering, as well as "
"any additional filtering for what xattr names are copied and what names are "
"allowed to be deleted.  For example, to skip the system namespace, you could "
"specify:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1777
#, no-wrap
msgid "--filter='-x system.*'\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1782
msgid ""
"To skip all namespaces except the user namespace, you could specify a "
"negated-user match:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1786
#, no-wrap
msgid "--filter='-x! user.*'\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1791
msgid ""
"To prevent any attributes from being deleted, you could specify a receiver-"
"only rule that excludes all names:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1795
#, no-wrap
msgid "--filter='-xr *'\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1801
msgid ""
"Note that the B<-X> option does not copy rsync's special xattr values (e.g.  "
"those used by B<--fake-super>) unless you repeat the option (e.g. B<-XX>).  "
"This \"copy all xattrs\" mode cannot be used with B<--fake-super>."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1801
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--chmod=CHMOD>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1807
msgid ""
"This option tells rsync to apply one or more comma-separated \"chmod\" modes "
"to the permission of the files in the transfer.  The resulting value is "
"treated as though it were the permissions that the sending side supplied for "
"the file, which means that this option can seem to have no effect on "
"existing files if B<--perms> is not enabled."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1815
msgid ""
"In addition to the normal parsing rules specified in the B<chmod>(1)  "
"manpage, you can specify an item that should only apply to a directory by "
"prefixing it with a 'D', or specify an item that should only apply to a file "
"by prefixing it with a 'F'.  For example, the following will ensure that all "
"directories get marked set-gid, that no files are other-writable, that both "
"are user-writable and group-writable, and that both have consistent "
"executability across all bits:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1819
#, no-wrap
msgid "--chmod=Dg+s,ug+w,Fo-w,+X\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1823
msgid "Using octal mode numbers is also allowed:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1827
#, no-wrap
msgid "--chmod=D2775,F664\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1835
msgid ""
"Symbolic permission-copy modes are also allowed, such as B<g=u>, B<o=g> or "
"B<g-o>.  A permission-copy item may copy from one class only (B<u>, B<g> or "
"B<o>) and cannot be combined with B<rwxXst> permission letters in the same "
"item.  Use comma-separated items when you need both behaviours, such as "
"B<g=o,o=>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1839
msgid ""
"A permission-copy B<=> item also clears the special bit for each destination "
"class it updates (B<u> clears setuid, B<g> clears setgid, and B<o> clears "
"sticky), matching GNU B<chmod> behaviour."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1842
msgid ""
"It is also legal to specify multiple B<--chmod> options, as each additional "
"option is just appended to the list of changes to make."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1845
msgid ""
"See the B<--perms> and B<--executability> options for how the resulting "
"permission value can be applied to the files in the transfer."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1845
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--owner>, B<-o>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1852
msgid ""
"This option causes rsync to set the owner of each destination filesystem "
"object (file, directory, etc.) to be the same as the source object, but only "
"if the receiving rsync is being run as the super-user (see also the B<--"
"super> and B<--fake-super> options).  Without this option, the owner of new "
"and/or transferred files are set to the invoking user on the receiving side."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1856
msgid ""
"The preservation of ownership will associate matching names by default, but "
"may fall back to using the ID number in some circumstances (see also the B<--"
"numeric-ids> option for a full discussion)."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1856
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--group>, B<-g>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1864
msgid ""
"This option causes rsync to set the group of each destination filesystem "
"object (file, directory, etc.) to be the same as the source object.  If the "
"receiving program is not running as the super-user (or if B<--no-super> was "
"specified), only groups that the invoking user on the receiving side is a "
"member of will be preserved.  Without this option, the group is set to the "
"default group of the invoking user on the receiving side."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1868
msgid ""
"The preservation of group information will associate matching names by "
"default, but may fall back to using the ID number in some circumstances (see "
"also the B<--numeric-ids> option for a full discussion)."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1868
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--devices>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1873
msgid ""
"This option causes rsync to transfer character and block device files to the "
"remote system to recreate these devices.  This option has no effect if the "
"receiving rsync is not run as the super-user and neither B<--super> nor B<--"
"fake-super> is in effect."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1877
msgid ""
"When a device file is not created, rsync generates the usual \"non-regular "
"file\" warning.  You can silence the warning by specifying B<--info=nonreg0>."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1877
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--specials>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1882
msgid ""
"This option causes rsync to transfer special files, such as named sockets "
"and fifos.  Creating these files does not normally require super-user "
"privileges.  Use B<--drop-D> to make the receiving rsync refuse to create "
"them regardless of what the transfer requested."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1886
msgid ""
"By default, rsync generates a \"non-regular file\" warning for each special "
"file encountered when this option is not set.  You can silence the warning "
"by specifying B<--info=nonreg0>."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1886
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<-D>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1889
msgid "The B<-D> option is equivalent to \"B<--devices> B<--specials>\"."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1889
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--drop-D>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1894
msgid ""
"This tells the receiving rsync to refuse to create device files and special "
"files, whatever the transfer requested.  Entries for them are skipped "
"exactly as if B<-D> had not been used, with the usual \"non-regular file\" "
"warning."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1903
msgid ""
"This differs from B<--no-D> in that it changes only what is created, not how "
"the file list is encoded.  B<--no-D> also turns off the rdev fields that "
"devices and special files carry on the wire, so applying it to one end of a "
"connection alone leaves the two ends disagreeing about the encoding and the "
"transfer fails.  B<--drop-D> can therefore be added on the receiving side by "
"itself, which is what it exists for\\ -- the B<rrsync> wrapper uses it to "
"stop a restricted directory's clients creating device and special files in "
"it.  Nodes already present there are left alone."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1905
msgid ""
"Because it only withholds creation, it has no effect on a sending rsync."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1912
msgid ""
"This option is not forwarded to the remote side, since its whole purpose is "
"to be applied to one end of a connection by itself.  It therefore affects "
"the rsync process you give it to and no other: on a local copy, or on the "
"receiving end of a B<--server> invocation such as the one rrsync builds.  To "
"set it on a remote receiver from the command line, send it explicitly with "
"B<--remote-option> (B<-M>):"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1916
#, no-wrap
msgid "rsync -av -M--drop-D src/ host:dest/\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1921
msgid ""
"Passing a plain B<--drop-D> to a push affects only the local sender, where "
"it does nothing."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1921
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--copy-devices>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1925
msgid ""
"This tells rsync to treat a device on the sending side as a regular file, "
"allowing it to be copied to a normal destination file (or another device if "
"B<--write-devices> was also specified)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1927 original/man1/rsync.1:1937
msgid "This option is refused by default by an rsync daemon."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1927
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--write-devices>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1930
msgid ""
"This tells rsync to treat a device on the receiving side as a regular file, "
"allowing the writing of file data into a device."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1932
msgid "This option implies the B<--inplace> option."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1935
msgid ""
"Be careful using this, as you should know what devices are present on the "
"receiving side of the transfer, especially when running rsync as root."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1937
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--times>, B<-t>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1948
msgid ""
"This tells rsync to set the modification times of the destination files "
"(including directories, symlinks, devices, etc.) to be the same as the "
"source files.  Note that if this option is not used, the optimization that "
"excludes files that have not been modified cannot be effective; in other "
"words, a missing B<-t> (or B<-a>) will cause the next transfer to behave as "
"if it used B<--ignore-times> (B<-I>), causing all files to be updated. "
"(Although rsync's delta-transfer algorithm will make the update fairly "
"efficient if the files haven't actually changed, you're much better off "
"using B<-t>.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1955
msgid ""
"If the negotiated protocol is older than 30\\ -- usually because the remote "
"rsync is older than 3.0.0 (March 2008), but also if B<--protocol> forces "
"it\\ -- the range of modification times that can be conveyed is restricted.  "
"If you have files dated older than 1970, make sure your rsync executables "
"are upgraded so that the full range of dates can be conveyed."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1955
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--atimes>, B<-U>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1958
msgid ""
"This tells rsync to set the access (use) times of the destination files to "
"the same value as the source files."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1963
msgid ""
"If repeated, it also sets the B<--open-noatime> option, which can help you "
"to make the sending and receiving systems have the same access times on the "
"transferred files without needing to run rsync an extra time after a file is "
"transferred."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1963
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--open-noatime>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1970
msgid ""
"This tells rsync to open files with the O_NOATIME flag (on systems that "
"support it) to avoid changing the access time of the files that are being "
"transferred.  If your OS does not support the O_NOATIME flag then rsync will "
"silently ignore this option.  Note also that some filesystems are mounted to "
"avoid updating the atime on read access even without the O_NOATIME flag "
"being set."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1970
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--crtimes>, B<-N,>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1975
msgid ""
"This tells rsync to set the create times (newness) of the destination files "
"to the same value as the source files. Your OS & filesystem must support the "
"setting of arbitrary creation (birth) times for this option to be supported."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1975
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--omit-dir-times>, B<-O>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1980
msgid ""
"This tells rsync to omit directories when it is preserving modification, "
"access, and create times.  If NFS is sharing the directories on the "
"receiving side, it is a good idea to use B<-O>.  This option is inferred if "
"you use B<--backup> without B<--backup-dir>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1984
msgid ""
"This option also has the side-effect of avoiding early creation of missing "
"sub-directories when incremental recursion is enabled, as discussed in the "
"B<--inc-recursive> section."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1984
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--omit-link-times>, B<-J>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1987
msgid ""
"This tells rsync to omit symlinks when it is preserving modification, "
"access, and create times."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1987
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--super>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1997
msgid ""
"This tells the receiving side to attempt super-user activities even if the "
"receiving rsync wasn't run by the super-user.  These activities include: "
"preserving users via the B<--owner> option, preserving all groups (not just "
"the current user's groups) via the B<--group> option, and copying devices "
"via the B<--devices> option.  This is useful for systems that allow such "
"activities without being the super-user, and also for ensuring that you will "
"get errors if the receiving side isn't being run as the super-user.  To turn "
"off super-user activities, the super-user can use B<--no-super>."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:1997
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--fake-super>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2009
msgid ""
"When this option is enabled, rsync simulates super-user activities by saving/"
"restoring the privileged attributes via special extended attributes that are "
"attached to each file (as needed).  This includes the file's owner and group "
"(if it is not the default), the file's device info (device & special files "
"are created as empty text files), and any permission bits that we won't "
"allow to be set on the real file (e.g. the real file gets u-s,g-s,o-t for "
"safety) or that would limit the owner's access (since the real super-user "
"can always access/change a file, the files we create can always be accessed/"
"changed by the creating user).  This option also handles ACLs (if B<--acls> "
"was specified) and non-user extended attributes (if B<--xattrs> was "
"specified)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2012
msgid ""
"This is a good way to backup data without using a super-user, and to store "
"ACLs from incompatible systems."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2016
msgid ""
"The B<--fake-super> option only affects the side where the option is used.  "
"To affect the remote side of a remote-shell connection, use the B<--remote-"
"option> (B<-M>) option:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2020
#, no-wrap
msgid "rsync -av -M--fake-super /src/ host:/dest/\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2027
msgid ""
"For a local copy, this option affects both the source and the destination.  "
"If you wish a local copy to enable this option just for the destination "
"files, specify B<-M--fake-super>.  If you wish a local copy to enable this "
"option just for the source files, combine B<--fake-super> with B<-M--super>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2029
msgid "This option is overridden by both B<--super> and B<--no-super>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2032
msgid "See also the B<fake\\ super> setting in the daemon's rsyncd.conf file."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2032
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--sparse>, B<-S>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2037
msgid ""
"Try to handle sparse files efficiently so they take up less space on the "
"destination.  This relies on the destination filesystem supporting sparse "
"files, that is, files where some parts of the file don't have corresponding "
"disk blocks allocated for them because they contain all zero bytes."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2043
msgid ""
"If combined with B<--inplace> the file created may not end up with sparse "
"blocks (depending on the filesystem type and kernel version), unless the B<--"
"whole-file> option is also in effect.  Note that versions of rsync older "
"than 3.1.3 (January 2018) will reject the combination of B<--sparse> and B<--"
"inplace>."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2043
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--preallocate>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2049
msgid ""
"This tells the receiver to allocate each destination file to its eventual "
"size before writing data to the file.  Rsync will only use the real "
"filesystem-level preallocation support provided by Linux's B<fallocate>(2)  "
"system call or Cygwin's B<posix_fallocate>(3), not the slow glibc "
"implementation that writes a null byte into each block."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2054
msgid ""
"Without this option, larger files may not be entirely contiguous on the "
"filesystem, but with this option rsync will probably copy more slowly.  If "
"the destination is not an extent-supporting filesystem (such as ext4, xfs, "
"NTFS, etc.), this option may have no positive effect at all."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2058
msgid ""
"If combined with B<--sparse>, the file will only have sparse blocks (as "
"opposed to allocated sequences of null bytes) if the kernel version and "
"filesystem type support creating holes in the allocated data."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2058
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--dry-run>, B<-n>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2064
msgid ""
"This makes rsync perform a trial run that doesn't make any changes, and "
"produces mostly the same output as a real run.  It is most commonly used in "
"combination with the B<--verbose> (B<-v>) and/or B<--itemize-changes> (B<-"
"i>) options to see what an rsync command is going to do before one actually "
"runs it."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2074
msgid ""
"The output of B<--itemize-changes> is supposed to be exactly the same on a "
"dry run and a subsequent real run (barring external changes to the source or "
"destination and system call failures); if it isn't, that's a bug.  Other "
"output should be mostly unchanged, but may differ in some areas.  Notably, a "
"dry run does not send the actual data for file transfers, so B<--progress> "
"has no effect, the \"bytes sent\", \"bytes received\", \"literal data\", and "
"\"matched data\" statistics are too small, and the \"speedup\" value is "
"equivalent to a run where no file transfers were needed."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2074
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--whole-file>, B<-W>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2082
msgid ""
"This option disables rsync's delta-transfer algorithm, causing the whole of "
"all transferred files to be sent.  The transfer may be faster if this option "
"is used when the bandwidth between the source and destination machines is "
"higher than the bandwidth to disk (especially when the \"disk\" is actually "
"a networked filesystem).  This is the default when both the source and "
"destination are specified as local paths, but only if no batch-writing "
"option is in effect."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2082
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--no-whole-file>, B<--no-W>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2087
msgid ""
"Disable whole-file updating when it is enabled by default for a local "
"transfer.  This usually slows rsync down, but it can be useful if you are "
"trying to minimize the writes to the destination file (if combined with B<--"
"inplace>) or for testing the checksum-based update algorithm."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2089
msgid "See also the B<--whole-file> option."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2089
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--checksum-choice=STR>, B<--cc=STR>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2096
msgid ""
"This option overrides the checksum algorithms.  If one algorithm name is "
"specified, it is used for both the transfer checksums and the pre-transfer "
"checksums (note that the pre-transfer checksum is only performed if B<--"
"checksum> is specified).  If two comma-separated names are supplied, the "
"first name affects the transfer checksums, and the second name affects the "
"pre-transfer checksums."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2098
msgid "The checksum options that you may be able to use are:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2102
msgid "B<auto> (the default automatic choice)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2104
msgid "B<xxh128>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2106
msgid "B<xxh3>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2108
msgid "B<xxh64> (aka B<xxhash>)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2110
msgid "B<md5>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2112
msgid "B<md4>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2114
msgid "B<sha1>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2116 original/man1/rsync.1:3119
msgid "B<none>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2120
msgid ""
"Run B<rsync\\ --version> to see the default checksum list compiled into your "
"version (which may differ from the list above)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2125
msgid ""
"If \"none\" is specified for the first (or only) name, the B<--whole-file> "
"option is forced on and no checksum verification is performed on the "
"transferred data.  If \"none\" is specified for the second (or only) name, "
"the B<--checksum> option cannot be used."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2128
msgid ""
"The \"auto\" option is the default, where rsync bases its algorithm choice "
"on a negotiation between the client and the server as follows:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2137
msgid ""
"When both sides of the transfer are at least 3.2.0 (released June 2020), "
"each side chooses its own most-preferred algorithm that also appears in the "
"peer's list.  Both sides order their lists strongest-first, so they converge "
"on the strongest mutual choice.  If no common checksum choice is found, "
"rsync exits with an error.  If the remote rsync is too old to support "
"checksum negotiation, a value is chosen based on the protocol version (which "
"chooses between MD5 and various flavors of MD4 based on protocol age)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2147
msgid ""
"The default order can be customized by setting the environment variable "
"B<RSYNC_CHECKSUM_LIST> to a space-separated list of acceptable checksum "
"names.  If the string contains a \"B<&>\" character, it is separated into "
"the \"client string & server string\", otherwise the same string applies to "
"both.  If the string (or string portion) contains no non-whitespace "
"characters, the default checksum list is used.  This method does not allow "
"you to specify the transfer checksum separately from the pre-transfer "
"checksum, and it discards \"auto\" and all unknown checksum names.  A list "
"with only invalid names results in a failed negotiation."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2149
msgid ""
"The use of the B<--checksum-choice> option overrides this environment list."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2149
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--one-file-system>, B<-x>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2156
msgid ""
"This tells rsync to avoid crossing a filesystem boundary when recursing.  "
"This does not limit the user's ability to specify items to copy from "
"multiple filesystems, just rsync's recursion through the hierarchy of each "
"directory that the user specified, and also the analogous recursion on the "
"receiving side during deletion.  Also keep in mind that rsync treats a "
"\"bind\" mount to the same device as being on the same filesystem."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2161
msgid ""
"If this option is repeated, rsync omits all mount-point directories from the "
"copy.  Otherwise, it includes an empty directory at each mount-point it "
"encounters (using the attributes of the mounted directory because those of "
"the underlying mount-point directory are inaccessible)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2166
msgid ""
"If rsync has been told to collapse symlinks (via B<--copy-links> or B<--copy-"
"unsafe-links>), a symlink to a directory on another device is treated like a "
"mount-point.  Symlinks to non-directories are unaffected by this option."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2166
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--ignore-non-existing>, B<--existing>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2171
msgid ""
"This tells rsync to skip creating files (including directories) that do not "
"exist yet on the destination.  If this option is combined with the B<--"
"ignore-existing> option, no files will be updated (which can be useful if "
"all you want to do is delete extraneous files)."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2174
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--ignore-existing>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2178
msgid ""
"This tells rsync to skip updating files that already exist on the "
"destination (this does I<not> ignore existing directories, or nothing would "
"get done).  See also B<--ignore-non-existing>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2190
msgid ""
"This option can be useful for those doing backups using the B<--link-dest> "
"option when they need to continue a backup run that got interrupted.  Since "
"a B<--link-dest> run is copied into a new directory hierarchy (when it is "
"used properly), using [B<--ignore-existing> will ensure that the already-"
"handled files don't get tweaked (which avoids a change in permissions on the "
"hard-linked files).  This does mean that this option is only looking at the "
"existing files in the destination hierarchy itself."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2197
msgid ""
"When B<--info=skip2> is used rsync will output \"FILENAME exists (INFO)\" "
"messages where the INFO indicates one of \"type change\", \"sum change\" "
"(requires B<-c>), \"file change\" (based on the quick check), \"attr "
"change\", or \"uptodate\".  Using B<--info=skip1> (which is also implied by "
"2 B<-v> options) outputs the exists message without the INFO suffix."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2197
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--remove-source-files>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2201
msgid ""
"This tells rsync to remove from the sending side the files (meaning non-"
"directories) that are a part of the transfer and have been successfully "
"duplicated on the receiving side."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2212
msgid ""
"Note that you should only use this option on source files that are "
"quiescent.  If you are using this to move files that show up in a particular "
"directory over to another host, make sure that the finished files get "
"renamed into the source directory, not directly written into it, so that "
"rsync can't possibly transfer a file that is not yet fully written.  If you "
"can't first write the files into a different directory, you should use a "
"naming idiom that lets rsync avoid transferring files that are not yet "
"finished (e.g. name the file \"foo.new\" when it is written, rename it to "
"\"foo\" when it is done, and then use the option B<--exclude='*.new'> for "
"the rsync transfer)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2215
msgid ""
"Rsync will skip the sender-side removal, and output an error, if the file's "
"size or modify time has not stayed unchanged."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2219
msgid ""
"If the copy is local, rsync will ensure that the sender does not remove a "
"file the receiver just verified, such as when the user accidentally makes "
"the source and destination directory the same path."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2219
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--delete>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2231
msgid ""
"This tells rsync to delete extraneous files from the receiving side (those "
"that don't exist on the sending side), but only for the directories that are "
"being synchronized.  You must have asked rsync to send the whole directory "
"(e.g. \"B<dir>\" or \"B<dir/>\"), rather than asking for all the files in a "
"directory via a wildcard (e.g. \"B<dir/*>\"), since the wildcard is expanded "
"by the shell and rsync thus gets a request to transfer individual files, not "
"the files' parent directory.  Files that are excluded from the transfer are "
"also excluded from being deleted unless you use the B<--delete-excluded> "
"option or mark the rules as only matching on the sending side (see the "
"include/exclude modifiers in the FILTER RULES section)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2235
msgid ""
"This option has no effect unless either B<--recursive> or B<--dirs> (B<-d>) "
"is enabled.  In the latter case, deletions only occur in directories whose "
"contents are being copied."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2239
msgid ""
"This option can be dangerous if used incorrectly! It is a very good idea to "
"first try a run using the B<--dry-run> (B<-n>) option to see what files are "
"going to be deleted."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2245
msgid ""
"If the sending side detects any I/O errors, then the deletion of any files "
"at the destination will be automatically disabled.  This is to prevent "
"temporary filesystem failures (such as NFS errors) on the sending side from "
"causing a massive deletion of files on the destination.  You can override "
"this with the B<--ignore-errors> option."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2253
msgid ""
"The B<--delete> option may be combined with one of the --delete-WHEN options "
"without conflict, as well as B<--delete-excluded>.  However, if none of the "
"B<--delete-WHEN> options are specified, rsync will choose the B<--delete-"
"during> algorithm when talking to rsync 3.0.0 (March 2008) or newer, or the "
"B<--delete-before> algorithm when talking to an older rsync.  See also B<--"
"delete-delay> and B<--delete-after>."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2253
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--delete-before>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2257
msgid ""
"Request that the file-deletions on the receiving side be done before the "
"transfer starts.  See B<--delete> (which is implied) for more details on "
"file-deletion."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2265
msgid ""
"Deleting before the transfer is helpful if the filesystem is tight for space "
"and removing extraneous files would help to make the transfer possible.  "
"However, it does introduce a delay before the start of the transfer, and "
"this delay might cause the transfer to timeout (if B<--timeout> was "
"specified).  It also forces rsync to use the old, non-incremental recursion "
"algorithm that requires rsync to scan all the files in the transfer into "
"memory at once (see B<--recursive>)."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2265
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--delete-during>, B<--del>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2273
msgid ""
"Request that the file-deletions on the receiving side be done incrementally "
"as the transfer happens.  The per-directory delete scan is done right before "
"each directory is checked for updates, so it behaves like a more efficient "
"B<--delete-before>, including doing the deletions prior to any per-directory "
"filter files being updated.  See B<--delete> (which is implied) for more "
"details on file-deletion."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2273
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--delete-delay>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2287
msgid ""
"Request that the file-deletions on the receiving side be computed during the "
"transfer (like B<--delete-during>), and then removed after the transfer "
"completes.  This is useful when combined with B<--delay-updates> and/or B<--"
"fuzzy>, and is more efficient than using B<--delete-after>, but can behave "
"differently, since B<--delete-after> computes the deletions in a separate "
"pass after all updates are done.  If the number of removed files overflows "
"an internal buffer, a temporary file will be created on the receiving side "
"to hold the names (it is removed while open, so you shouldn't see it during "
"the transfer).  If the creation of the temporary file fails, rsync will try "
"to fall back to using B<--delete-after> (which it cannot do if B<--"
"recursive> is doing an incremental scan).  See B<--delete> (which is "
"implied) for more details on file-deletion."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2296
msgid ""
"Request that the file-deletions on the receiving side be done after the "
"transfer has completed.  This is useful if you are sending new per-directory "
"merge files as a part of the transfer and you want their exclusions to take "
"effect for the delete phase of the current transfer.  It also forces rsync "
"to use the old, non-incremental recursion algorithm that requires rsync to "
"scan all the files in the transfer into memory at once (see B<--recursive>). "
"See B<--delete> (which is implied) for more details on file-deletion."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2299
msgid ""
"See also the B<--delete-delay> option that might be a faster choice for "
"those that just want the deletions to occur at the end of the transfer."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2299
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--delete-excluded>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2302
msgid ""
"This option turns any unqualified exclude/include rules into server-side "
"rules that do not affect the receiver's deletions."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2309
msgid ""
"By default, an exclude or include has both a server-side effect (to \"hide\" "
"and \"show\" files when building the server's file list) and a receiver-side "
"effect (to \"protect\" and \"risk\" files when deletions are occurring).  "
"Any rule that has no modifier to specify what sides it is executed on will "
"be instead treated as if it were a server-side rule only, avoiding any "
"\"protect\" effects of the rules."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2315
msgid ""
"A rule can still apply to both sides even with this option specified if the "
"rule is given both the sender & receiver modifier letters (e.g., B<-f'-sr\\ "
"foo'>).  Receiver-side protect/risk rules can also be explicitly specified "
"to limit the deletions.  This saves you from having to edit a bunch of B<-f'-"
"\\ foo'> rules into B<-f'-s\\ foo'> (aka B<-f'H\\ foo'>) rules (not to "
"mention the corresponding includes)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2318
msgid ""
"See the FILTER RULES section for more information.  See B<--delete> (which "
"is implied) for more details on deletion."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2318
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--ignore-missing-args>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2325
msgid ""
"When rsync is first processing the explicitly requested source files (e.g.  "
"command-line arguments or B<--files-from> entries), it is normally an error "
"if the file cannot be found.  This option suppresses that error, and does "
"not try to transfer the file.  This does not affect subsequent vanished-file "
"errors if a file was initially found to be present and later is no longer "
"there."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2325
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--delete-missing-args>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2333
msgid ""
"This option takes the behavior of the (implied)  B<--ignore-missing-args> "
"option a step farther: each missing argument will become a deletion request "
"of the corresponding destination file on the receiving side (should it "
"exist).  If the destination file is a non-empty directory, it will only be "
"successfully deleted if B<--force> or B<--delete> are in effect.  Other than "
"that, this option is independent of any other type of delete processing."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2336
msgid ""
"The missing source files are represented by special file-list entries which "
"display as a \"B<*missing>\" entry in the B<--list-only> output."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2336
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--ignore-errors>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2339
msgid ""
"Tells B<--delete> to go ahead and delete files even when there are I/O "
"errors."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2339
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--force>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2343
msgid ""
"This option tells rsync to delete a non-empty directory when it is to be "
"replaced by a non-directory.  This is only relevant if deletions are not "
"active (see B<--delete> for details)."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2343
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--max-delete=NUM>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2349
msgid ""
"This tells rsync not to delete more than NUM files or directories.  If that "
"limit is exceeded, all further deletions are skipped through the end of the "
"transfer.  At the end, rsync outputs a warning (including a count of the "
"skipped deletions) and exits with an error code of 25 (unless some more "
"important error condition also occurred)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2356
msgid ""
"You may specify B<--max-delete=0> to be warned about any extraneous files in "
"the destination without removing any of them.  CAUTION: a client rsync older "
"than 3.0.0 (March 2008) treats B<--max-delete=0> as unlimited, so use B<--"
"max-delete=-1> if the command might be run by such an old rsync.  (A 3.0.0 "
"or newer client protects an older remote by forwarding the option as B<--max-"
"delete=-1>.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2356
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--max-size=SIZE>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2361
msgid ""
"This tells rsync to avoid transferring any file that is larger than the "
"specified SIZE.  A numeric value can be suffixed with a string to indicate "
"the numeric units or left unqualified to specify bytes, and the numeric "
"value can have a fractional part, such as B<--max-size=1.5m>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2371
msgid ""
"The first letter of a units string can be B<B> (bytes), B<K> (kilo), B<M> "
"(mega), B<G> (giga), B<T> (tera), or B<P> (peta).  If the string is a single "
"char or has \"ib\" added to it (e.g. \"G\" or \"GiB\") then the units are "
"multiples of 1024.  If you use a two-letter suffix that ends with a \"B\" (e."
"g. \"kb\") then you get units that are multiples of 1000.  The string's "
"letters can be any mix of upper and lower-case that you want to use."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2375
msgid ""
"Finally, if the string ends with either \"+1\" or \"-1\", it is offset by "
"one byte in the indicated direction.  The largest possible value is usually "
"B<8192P-1>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2378
msgid ""
"Examples: B<--max-size=1.5mb-1> is 1499999 bytes, and B<--max-size=2g+1> is "
"2147483649 bytes."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2378
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--min-size=SIZE>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2382
msgid ""
"This tells rsync to avoid transferring any file that is smaller than the "
"specified SIZE, which can help in not transferring small, junk files.  See "
"the B<--max-size> option for a description of SIZE and other info."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2382
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--max-alloc=SIZE>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2390
msgid ""
"By default rsync limits an individual malloc/realloc to about 1GB in size.  "
"For most people this limit works just fine and prevents a protocol error "
"causing rsync to request massive amounts of memory.  However, if you have "
"many millions of files in a transfer, a large amount of server memory, and "
"you don't want to split up your transfer into multiple parts, you can "
"increase the per-allocation limit to something larger, allowing rsync to "
"consume more memory."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2393
msgid ""
"Keep in mind that this is not a limit on the total size of allocated "
"memory.  It is a sanity-check value for each individual allocation."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2396
msgid ""
"See the B<--max-size> option for a description of how SIZE can be "
"specified.  The default suffix if none is given is bytes."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2400
msgid ""
"A value of 0 is an easy way to say \"the largest limit this build "
"supports\".  It resolves to the same ceiling an explicit SIZE is checked "
"against, so it is never a higher limit than one you could have typed out "
"yourself."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2406
msgid ""
"Because the option is passed to the remote rsync as you wrote it, each side "
"resolves a 0 against its own maximum.  That makes 0 the only spelling that "
"is correct for both ends of a transfer between hosts of different word "
"sizes: a literal value large enough to be useful on a 64-bit client is "
"rejected as too large by a 32-bit daemon."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2409
msgid ""
"A value of 0 was accepted beginning in 3.2.3 and rejected in 3.5.0; the "
"release after 3.5.0 accepts it again."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2413
msgid ""
"A daemon administrator who does not want clients to change the configured "
"allocation ceiling can set B<refuse\\ options\\ =\\ max-alloc> in the "
"module's B<rsyncd.conf>.  This refuses every client-supplied value, "
"including 0."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2420
msgid ""
"You can set a default value using the environment variable "
"B<RSYNC_MAX_ALLOC> using the same SIZE values as supported by this option.  "
"If the remote rsync doesn't understand the B<--max-alloc> option, you can "
"override an environmental value by specifying B<--max-alloc=1g>, which will "
"make rsync avoid sending the option to the remote side (because \"1G\" is "
"the default)."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2420
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--block-size=SIZE>, B<-B>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2424
msgid ""
"This forces the block size used in rsync's delta-transfer algorithm to a "
"fixed value.  It is normally selected based on the size of each file being "
"updated.  See the technical report for details."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2427
msgid ""
"The SIZE can be specified with a suffix as detailed in the B<--max-size> "
"option."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2427
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--rsh=COMMAND>, B<-e>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2431
msgid ""
"This option allows you to choose an alternative remote shell program to use "
"for communication between the local and remote copies of rsync.  Typically, "
"rsync is configured to use ssh by default."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2438
msgid ""
"If this option is used with B<[user@]host::module/path>, then the remote "
"shell I<COMMAND> will be used to run an rsync daemon on the remote host, and "
"all data will be transmitted through that remote shell connection, rather "
"than through a direct socket connection to a running rsync daemon on the "
"remote host.  See the USING RSYNC-DAEMON FEATURES VIA A REMOTE-SHELL "
"CONNECTION section above."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2448
msgid ""
"The B<RSYNC_PORT> environment variable will be set when a daemon connection "
"is being made via a remote-shell connection.  It is set to 0 if the default "
"daemon port is being assumed, or it is set to the value of the rsync port "
"that was specified via either the B<--port> option or a non-empty port value "
"in an B<rsync://> URL (for example B<rsync://host.example.com:984>).  This "
"is useful if the program being run is not actually rsync but rather "
"something such as an SSL or stunnel helper script; the script can use "
"B<RSYNC_PORT> to determine whether it should connect to a default or "
"alternate port."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2457
msgid ""
"Command-line arguments are permitted in COMMAND provided that COMMAND is "
"presented to rsync as a single argument.  You must use spaces (not tabs or "
"other whitespace) to separate the command and arguments from each other, and "
"you can use single- and/or double-quotes to preserve spaces in an argument "
"(but not backslashes).  Note that doubling a single-quote inside a single-"
"quoted string gives you a single-quote; likewise for double-quotes (though "
"you need to pay attention to which quotes your shell is parsing and which "
"quotes rsync is parsing).  Some examples:"
msgstr "Command-line arguments are permitted in COMMAND provided that COMMAND is presented to rsync as a single argument.  You must use spaces (not tabs or other whitespace) to separate the command and arguments from each other, and you can use single- and/or double-quotes to preserve spaces in an argument (but not backslashes).  Note that doubling a single-quote inside a single-quoted string gives you a single-quote; likewise for double-quotes (though you need to pay attention to which quotes your shell is parsing and which quotes rsync is parsing).  次に例を示します:"

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2462
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"-e 'ssh -p 2234'\n"
"-e 'ssh -o \"ProxyCommand nohup ssh firewall nc -w1 %h %p\"'\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2467
msgid ""
"(Note that ssh users can alternately customize site-specific connect options "
"in their .ssh/config file.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2470
msgid ""
"You can also choose the remote shell program using the B<RSYNC_RSH> "
"environment variable, which accepts the same range of values as B<-e>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2473
msgid "See also the B<--blocking-io> option which is affected by this option."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2473
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--rsync-path=PROGRAM>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2480
msgid ""
"Use this to specify what program is to be run on the remote machine to start-"
"up rsync.  Often used when rsync is not in the default remote-shell's path "
"(e.g. B<--rsync-path=/usr/local/bin/rsync>).  Note that PROGRAM is run with "
"the help of a shell, so it can be any program, script, or command sequence "
"you'd care to run, so long as it does not corrupt the standard-in & standard-"
"out that rsync is using to communicate."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2483
msgid ""
"One tricky example is to set a different default directory on the remote "
"machine for use with the B<--relative> option.  For instance:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2487
#, no-wrap
msgid "rsync -avR --rsync-path=\"cd /a/b && rsync\" host:c/d /e/\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2489
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--remote-option=OPTION>, B<-M>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2494
msgid ""
"This option is used for more advanced situations where you want certain "
"effects to be limited to one side of the transfer only.  For instance, if "
"you want to pass B<--log-file=FILE> and B<--fake-super> to the remote "
"system, specify it like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2498
#, no-wrap
msgid "rsync -av -M --log-file=foo -M--fake-super src/ dest/\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2504
msgid ""
"If you want to have an option affect only the local side of a transfer when "
"it normally affects both sides, send its negation to the remote side.  Like "
"this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2508
#, no-wrap
msgid "rsync -av -x -M--no-x src/ dest/\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2514
msgid ""
"Be cautious using this, as it is possible to toggle an option that will "
"cause rsync to have a different idea about what data to expect next over the "
"socket, and that will make it fail in a cryptic fashion."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2519
msgid ""
"Note that you should use a separate B<-M> option for each remote option you "
"want to pass.  On older rsync versions, the presence of any spaces in the "
"remote-option argument could cause it to be split into separate remote "
"arguments, but this requires the use of B<--old-args> in a modern rsync."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2522
msgid ""
"When performing a local transfer, the \"local\" side is the sender and the "
"\"remote\" side is the receiver."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2528
msgid ""
"Note some versions of the popt option-parsing library have a bug in them "
"that prevents you from using an adjacent argument with an equal in it next "
"to a short option letter (e.g. B<-M--log-file=/tmp/foo>).  If this bug "
"affects your version of popt, you can use the version of popt that is "
"included with rsync."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2528
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--cvs-exclude>, B<-C>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2532
msgid ""
"This is a useful shorthand for excluding a broad range of files that you "
"often don't want to transfer between systems.  It uses a similar algorithm "
"to CVS to determine if a file should be ignored."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2536
msgid ""
"The exclude list is initialized to exclude the following items (these "
"initial items are marked as perishable\\ -- see the FILTER RULES section):"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2574
msgid ""
"B<RCS> B<SCCS> B<CVS> B<CVS.adm> B<RCSLOG> B<cvslog.*> B<tags> B<TAGS> B<."
"make.state> B<.nse_depinfo> B<*~> B<#*> B<.#*> B<,*> B<_$*> B<*$> B<*.old> "
"B<*.bak> B<*.BAK> B<*.orig> B<*.rej> B<.del-*> B<*.a> B<*.olb> B<*.o> B<*."
"obj> B<*.so> B<*.exe> B<*.Z> B<*.elc> B<*.ln> B<core> B<.svn/> B<.git/> B<."
"hg/> B<.bzr/>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2579
msgid ""
"then, files listed in a $HOME/.cvsignore are added to the list and any files "
"listed in the CVSIGNORE environment variable (all cvsignore names are "
"delimited by whitespace)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2584
msgid ""
"Finally, any file is ignored if it is in the same directory as a .cvsignore "
"file and matches one of the patterns listed therein.  Unlike rsync's filter/"
"exclude files, these patterns are split on whitespace.  See the B<cvs>(1) "
"manual for more information."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2596
msgid ""
"If you're combining B<-C> with your own B<--filter> rules, you should note "
"that these CVS excludes are appended at the end of your own rules, "
"regardless of where the B<-C> was placed on the command-line.  This makes "
"them a lower priority than any rules you specified explicitly.  If you want "
"to control where these CVS excludes get inserted into your filter rules, you "
"should omit the B<-C> as a command-line option and use a combination of B<--"
"filter=:C> and B<--filter=-C> (either on your command-line or by putting the "
"\":C\" and \"-C\" rules into a filter file with your other rules).  The "
"first option turns on the per-directory scanning for the .cvsignore file.  "
"The second option does a one-time import of the CVS excludes mentioned above."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2596
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--filter=RULE>, B<-f>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2600
msgid ""
"This option allows you to add rules to selectively exclude certain files "
"from the list of files to be transferred.  This is most useful in "
"combination with a recursive transfer."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2606
msgid ""
"You may use as many B<--filter> options on the command line as you like to "
"build up the list of files to exclude.  If the filter contains whitespace, "
"be sure to quote it so that the shell gives the rule to rsync as a single "
"argument.  You can use an underscore instead of a space to separate a rule "
"from its argument."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2608 original/man1/rsync.1:2639
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2659
msgid "See the FILTER RULES section for detailed information on this option."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2608
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<-F>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2611
msgid ""
"The B<-F> option is a shorthand for adding two B<--filter> rules to your "
"command.  The first time it is used is a shorthand for this rule:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2615
#, no-wrap
msgid "--filter='dir-merge /.rsync-filter'\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2622
msgid ""
"This tells rsync to look for per-directory .rsync-filter files that have "
"been sprinkled through the hierarchy and use their rules to filter the files "
"in the transfer.  If B<-F> is repeated, it is a shorthand for this rule:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2626
#, no-wrap
msgid "--filter='exclude .rsync-filter'\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2630
msgid "This filters out the .rsync-filter files themselves from the transfer."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2633
msgid ""
"See the FILTER RULES section for detailed information on how these options "
"work."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2633
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--exclude=PATTERN>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2637
msgid ""
"This option is a simplified form of the B<--filter> option that specifies an "
"exclude rule and does not allow the full rule-parsing syntax of normal "
"filter rules.  This is equivalent to specifying B<-f'-\\ PATTERN'>."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2639
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--exclude-from=FILE>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2644
msgid ""
"This option is related to the B<--exclude> option, but it specifies a FILE "
"that contains exclude patterns (one per line).  Blank lines in the file are "
"ignored, as are whole-line comments that start with 'B<;>' or 'B<#>' "
"(filename rules that contain those characters are unaffected)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2648
msgid ""
"If a line begins with \"B<-\\ >\" (dash, space) or \"B<+\\ >\" (plus, "
"space), then the type of rule is being explicitly specified as an exclude or "
"an include (respectively).  Any rules without such a prefix are taken to be "
"an exclude."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2651 original/man1/rsync.1:2671
msgid ""
"If a line consists of just \"B<!>\", then the current filter rules are "
"cleared before adding any further rules."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2653 original/man1/rsync.1:2673
msgid "If I<FILE> is 'B<->', the list will be read from standard input."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2653
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--include=PATTERN>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2657
msgid ""
"This option is a simplified form of the B<--filter> option that specifies an "
"include rule and does not allow the full rule-parsing syntax of normal "
"filter rules.  This is equivalent to specifying B<-f'+\\ PATTERN'>."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2659
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--include-from=FILE>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2664
msgid ""
"This option is related to the B<--include> option, but it specifies a FILE "
"that contains include patterns (one per line).  Blank lines in the file are "
"ignored, as are whole-line comments that start with 'B<;>' or 'B<#>' "
"(filename rules that contain those characters are unaffected)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2668
msgid ""
"If a line begins with \"B<-\\ >\" (dash, space) or \"B<+\\ >\" (plus, "
"space), then the type of rule is being explicitly specified as an exclude or "
"an include (respectively).  Any rules without such a prefix are taken to be "
"an include."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2673
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--files-from=FILE>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2678
msgid ""
"Using this option tells rsync to read the exact list of files to transfer "
"from FILE (or standard input if FILE is 'B<->').  It also tweaks the default "
"behavior of rsync to make transferring just the specified files and "
"directories easier:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2684
msgid ""
"The B<--relative> (B<-R>) option is implied, which preserves the path "
"information that is specified for each item in the file (use B<--no-"
"relative> or B<--no-R> if you want to turn that off)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2688
msgid ""
"The B<--dirs> (B<-d>) option is implied, which will create directories "
"specified in the list on the destination rather than noisily skipping them "
"(use B<--no-dirs> or B<--no-d> if you want to turn that off)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2691
msgid ""
"The B<--archive> (B<-a>) option's behavior does not imply B<--recursive> (B<-"
"r>), so specify it explicitly, if you want it."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2696
msgid ""
"These side-effects change the default state of rsync, so the position of the "
"B<--files-from> option on the command-line has no bearing on how other "
"options are parsed (e.g. B<-a> works the same before or after B<--files-"
"from>, as does B<--no-R> and all other options)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2703
msgid ""
"Listing individual files with B<--files-from> does not make unlisted "
"siblings eligible for deletion.  The B<--delete> option only removes entries "
"from directories whose complete contents are being synchronised, so list the "
"directory itself and enable recursion if that deletion scope is intended."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2711
msgid ""
"The filenames that are read from the FILE are all relative to the source "
"directory: any leading slash is removed, and \"..\" components are resolved "
"away so an entry cannot rise above the source directory\\ -- e.g. \"../foo\" "
"is taken as \"foo\" within the source directory.  An entry that still "
"contains an active \"..\" after that resolution (one that cannot be "
"collapsed) is rejected with an error.  Blank entries are ignored, as are "
"whole-entry comments that start with 'B<;>' or 'B<#>'.  For example, take "
"this command:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2715
#, no-wrap
msgid "rsync -a --files-from=/tmp/foo /usr remote:/backup\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2729
msgid ""
"If /tmp/foo contains the string \"bin\" (or even \"/bin\"), the /usr/bin "
"directory will be created as /backup/bin on the remote host.  If it contains "
"\"bin/\" (note the trailing slash), the immediate contents of the directory "
"would also be sent, without needing to be explicitly mentioned in the file.  "
"In both cases, if the B<-r> option was enabled, that directory's entire "
"hierarchy would also be transferred (keep in mind that B<-r> needs to be "
"specified explicitly with B<--files-from>, since it is not implied by B<-"
"a>.  Also note that the effect of the (enabled by default) B<-r> option is "
"to duplicate only the path info that is read from the file\\ -- it does not "
"force the duplication of the source-spec path (/usr in this case)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2735
msgid ""
"In addition, the B<--files-from> file can be read from the remote host "
"instead of the local host if you specify a \"host:\" in front of the file "
"(the host must match one end of the transfer).  As a short-cut, you can "
"specify just a prefix of \":\" to mean \"use the remote end of the "
"transfer\".  For example:"
msgstr "In addition, the B<--files-from> file can be read from the remote host instead of the local host if you specify a \"host:\" in front of the file (the host must match one end of the transfer).  As a short-cut, you can specify just a prefix of \":\" to mean \"use the remote end of the transfer\".  例:"

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2739
#, no-wrap
msgid "rsync -a --files-from=:/path/file-list src:/ /tmp/copy\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2744
msgid ""
"This would copy all the files specified in the /path/file-list file that was "
"located on the remote \"src\" host."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2749
msgid ""
"If the B<--iconv> and B<--secluded-args> options are specified and the B<--"
"files-from> filenames are being sent from one host to another, the filenames "
"will be translated from the sending host's charset to the receiving host's "
"charset."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2756
msgid ""
"NOTE: sorting the list of files in the B<--files-from> input helps rsync to "
"be more efficient, as it will avoid re-visiting the path elements that are "
"shared between adjacent entries.  If the input is not sorted, some path "
"elements (implied directories) may end up being scanned multiple times, and "
"rsync will eventually unduplicate them after they get turned into file-list "
"elements."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2756
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--from0>, B<-0>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2763
msgid ""
"This tells rsync that the rules or filenames it reads from a file are "
"terminated by a null ('\\e0') character, not a NL, CR, or CR+LF.  This "
"affects B<--exclude-from>, B<--include-from>, B<--files-from>, and any "
"merged files specified in a B<--filter> rule.  It does not affect B<--cvs-"
"exclude> (since all names read from a .cvsignore file are split on "
"whitespace)."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2763
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--old-args>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2768
msgid ""
"This option tells rsync not to protect the argument values sent to the "
"remote side from unintended word-splitting or other misinterpretation.  It "
"also allows the client to treat an empty argument as a \".\" instead of "
"generating an error."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2774
msgid ""
"The default in a modern rsync is for \"shell-active\" characters (including "
"spaces) to be backslash-escaped in the arguments that are sent to the remote "
"shell.  The wildcard characters B<*>, B<?>, B<[>, & B<]> are not escaped in "
"filename arguments (allowing them to expand into multiple filenames) while "
"being protected in option arguments, such as B<--usermap>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2778
msgid ""
"If you have a script that wants to use old-style argument splitting in its "
"filenames, specify this option once.  If the remote shell has a problem with "
"any backslash escapes at all, specify this option twice."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2785
msgid ""
"You may also control this setting via the B<RSYNC_OLD_ARGS> environment "
"variable.  If it has the value \"1\", rsync will default to a single-option "
"setting.  If it has the value \"2\" (or more), rsync will default to a "
"repeated-option setting.  If it is \"0\", you'll get the default escaping "
"behavior.  The environment is always overridden by manually specified "
"positive or negative options (the negative is B<--no-old-args>)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2791
msgid ""
"Note that this option also disables the extra safety check that ensures that "
"a remote sender isn't including extra top-level items in the file-list that "
"you didn't request.  This side-effect is necessary because we can't know for "
"sure what names to expect when the remote shell is interpreting the "
"arguments."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2793
msgid "This option conflicts with the B<--secluded-args> option."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2793
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--secluded-args>, B<-s>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2798
msgid ""
"This option sends all filenames and most options to the remote rsync via the "
"protocol (rather than via the remote shell command line), which avoids the "
"possibility of the remote shell modifying them.  Wildcards are expanded on "
"the remote host by rsync instead of a shell."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2807
msgid ""
"Without this option, rsync does backslash-escaping of arguments to prevent "
"things like space splitting and unwanted special-character side-effects, "
"which is normally sufficient to avoid unwanted modifications by the shell.  "
"This option is useful for those times that you need the argument's character "
"set to be converted for the remote host, if the remote shell is incompatible "
"with the default backslash-escaping method, or there is some other reason "
"that you want the majority of the options and arguments to bypass the "
"command-line of the remote shell."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2811
msgid ""
"This option is incompatible with remote rsync versions prior to 3.0.0 (March "
"2008).  It also has the drawback of being refused by restricted shells that "
"want to be able to inspect all the option values for safety."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2816
msgid ""
"If you combine this option with B<--iconv>, the arguments related to the "
"remote side will be translated from the local to the remote character-set.  "
"The translation happens before wild-cards are expanded.  See also the B<--"
"files-from> option."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2824
msgid ""
"You may also control this setting via the B<RSYNC_PROTECT_ARGS> environment "
"variable.  If it has a non-zero value, this setting will be enabled by "
"default, otherwise it will be disabled by default.  Either state is "
"overridden by a manually specified positive or negative version of this "
"option (note that B<--no-s> and B<--no-secluded-args> are the negative "
"versions).  This environment variable is also superseded by a non-zero "
"B<RSYNC_OLD_ARGS> environment variable."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2826
msgid "This option conflicts with the B<--old-args> option."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2831
msgid ""
"This option used to be called B<--protect-args> (before 3.2.6, September "
"2022) and that older name can still be used (though specifying it as B<-s> "
"is always the easiest and most compatible choice)."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2831
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--trust-sender>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2837
msgid ""
"This option disables two extra validation checks that a local client "
"performs on the file list generated by a remote sender.  This option should "
"only be used if you trust the sender to not put something malicious in the "
"file list (something that could possibly be done via a modified rsync, a "
"modified shell, or some other similar manipulation)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2840
msgid ""
"Normally, the rsync client runs two extra validation checks when pulling "
"files from a remote rsync:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2845
msgid ""
"It verifies that additional argument items didn't get added at the top of "
"the transfer."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2848
msgid ""
"It verifies that none of the items in the file list are names that should "
"have been excluded (if filter rules were specified)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2852
msgid ""
"Note that various options can turn off one or both of these checks if the "
"option interferes with the validation.  For instance:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2857
msgid ""
"Using a per-directory filter file reads filter rules that only the server "
"knows about, so the filter checking is disabled."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2860
msgid ""
"Using the B<--old-args> option allows the sender to manipulate the requested "
"arguments, so the argument checking is disabled."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2863
msgid ""
"Reading the files-from list from the server side means that the client "
"doesn't know the argument list, so the argument checking is disabled."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2866
msgid ""
"Using B<--read-batch> disables both checks since the batch file's contents "
"will have been verified when it was created."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2872
msgid ""
"This option may help an under-powered client server if the extra pattern "
"matching is slowing things down on a huge transfer.  It could also be used "
"for a transfer from a trusted sender as a workaround if there appeared to be "
"a bug in the verification logic."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2875
msgid ""
"When using this option it is a good idea to specify a dedicated destination "
"directory, as discussed in the SECURITY section."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2875
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--copy-as=USER[:GROUP]>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2880
msgid ""
"This option instructs rsync to use the USER and (if specified after a colon) "
"the GROUP for the copy operations.  This only works if the user that is "
"running rsync has the ability to change users.  If the group is not "
"specified then the user's default groups are used."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2889
msgid ""
"This option can help to reduce the risk in the case where rsync is being run "
"as root, copying into or out of a directory that might have live changes "
"happening to it, and you want to make sure that root-level read or write "
"actions of system files are not possible.  While you could alternatively run "
"all of rsync as the specified user, sometimes you need the root-level host-"
"access credentials to be used, so this allows rsync to drop root for the "
"copying part of the operation after the remote-shell or daemon connection is "
"established."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2899
msgid ""
"The option only affects one side of the transfer unless the transfer is "
"local, in which case it affects both sides.  Use the B<--remote-option> to "
"affect the remote side, such as B<-M--copy-as=joe>.  For a local transfer, "
"the lsh (or lsh.sh) support file provides a local-shell helper script that "
"can be used to allow a \"localhost:\" or \"lh:\" host-spec to be specified "
"without needing to setup any remote shells, allowing you to specify remote "
"options that affect the side of the transfer that is using the host-spec "
"(and using hostname \"lh\" avoids the overriding of the remote directory to "
"the user's home directory)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2901
msgid ""
"For example, the following rsync writes the local files as user \"joe\":"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2905
#, no-wrap
msgid "sudo rsync -aiv --copy-as=joe host1:backups/joe/ /home/joe/\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2912
msgid ""
"This makes all files owned by user \"joe\", limits the groups to those that "
"are available to that user, and makes it impossible for the joe user to do a "
"timed exploit of the path to induce a change to a file that the joe user has "
"no permissions to change."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2915
msgid ""
"The following command does a local copy into the \"dest/\" directory as user "
"\"joe\" (assuming you've installed support/lsh into a directory on your "
"$PATH):"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2919
#, no-wrap
msgid "sudo rsync -aive lsh -M--copy-as=joe src/ lh:dest/\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2921
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--temp-dir=DIR>, B<-T>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2928
msgid ""
"This option instructs rsync to use DIR as a scratch directory when creating "
"temporary copies of the files transferred on the receiving side.  The "
"default behavior is to create each temporary file in the same directory as "
"the associated destination file.  The temp-file names inside the specified "
"DIR will not be prefixed with an extra dot, though they will still have a "
"random suffix added."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2940
msgid ""
"This option is most often used when the receiving disk partition does not "
"have enough free space to hold a copy of the largest file in the transfer.  "
"In this case (i.e. when the scratch directory is on a different disk "
"partition), rsync will not be able to rename each received temporary file "
"over the top of the associated destination file, but instead must copy it "
"into place.  Rsync does this by copying the file over the top of the "
"destination file, which means that the destination file will contain "
"truncated data during this copy.  If this were not done this way, it would "
"be possible for the old file to continue taking up disk space (if someone "
"had it open), and thus there might not be enough room to fit the new version "
"on the disk at the same time."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2953
msgid ""
"If you are using this option for reasons other than a shortage of disk "
"space, you may wish to combine it with the B<--delay-updates> option, which "
"will ensure that all copied files get put into subdirectories in the "
"destination hierarchy, awaiting the end of the transfer.  If you don't have "
"enough room to duplicate all the arriving files on the destination "
"partition, another way to tell rsync that you aren't overly concerned about "
"disk space is to use the B<--partial-dir> option with a relative path; "
"because this tells rsync that it is OK to stash off a copy of a single file "
"in a subdirectory in the destination hierarchy, rsync will use the partial-"
"dir as a staging area to bring over the copied file, and then rename it into "
"place from there. (Specifying a B<--partial-dir> with an absolute path does "
"not have this side-effect.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2953
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--fuzzy>, B<-y>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2959
msgid ""
"This option tells rsync that it should look for a basis file for any "
"destination file that is missing.  With this option, rsync looks in the same "
"directory as the destination file for either a file that has an identical "
"size and modified-time, or a similarly-named file.  If found, rsync uses the "
"fuzzy basis file to try to speed up the transfer."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2963
msgid ""
"If the option is repeated, the fuzzy scan will also be done in any matching "
"alternate destination directories that are specified via B<--compare-dest>, "
"B<--copy-dest>, or B<--link-dest>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2967
msgid ""
"Note that the use of the B<--delete> option might get rid of any potential "
"fuzzy-match files, so either use B<--delete-after> or specify some filename "
"exclusions if you need to prevent this."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2967
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--compare-dest=DIR>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2976
msgid ""
"This option instructs rsync to use I<DIR> on the destination machine as an "
"additional hierarchy to compare destination files against doing transfers "
"(if the files are missing in the destination directory).  If a file is found "
"in I<DIR> that is identical to the sender's file, the file will NOT be "
"transferred to the destination directory.  This is useful for creating a "
"sparse backup of just files that have changed from an earlier backup.  This "
"option is typically used to copy into an empty (or newly created)  directory."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2983
msgid ""
"Multiple B<--compare-dest> directories may be provided, which will cause "
"rsync to search the list in the order specified for an exact match.  If a "
"match is found that differs only in attributes, a local copy is made and the "
"attributes updated.  If a match is not found, a basis file from one of the "
"I<DIRs> will be selected to try to speed up the transfer."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2986
msgid ""
"If I<DIR> is a relative path, it is relative to the destination directory.  "
"See also B<--copy-dest> and B<--link-dest>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2991
msgid ""
"NOTE: rsync will remove a file from a non-empty destination hierarchy if an "
"exact match is found in one of the compare-dest hierarchies, making the end "
"result more closely match a fresh copy."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2991
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--copy-dest=DIR>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:2997
msgid ""
"This option behaves like B<--compare-dest>, but rsync will also copy "
"unchanged files found in I<DIR> to the destination directory using a local "
"copy.  This is useful for doing transfers to a new destination while leaving "
"existing files intact, and then doing a flash-cutover when all files have "
"been successfully transferred."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3002
msgid ""
"Multiple B<--copy-dest> directories may be provided, which will cause rsync "
"to search the list in the order specified for an unchanged file.  If a match "
"is not found, a basis file from one of the I<DIRs> will be selected to try "
"to speed up the transfer."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3005
msgid ""
"If I<DIR> is a relative path, it is relative to the destination directory.  "
"See also B<--compare-dest> and B<--link-dest>."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3005
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--link-dest=DIR>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3010
msgid ""
"This option behaves like B<--copy-dest>, but unchanged files are hard linked "
"from I<DIR> to the destination directory.  The files must be identical in "
"all preserved attributes (e.g. permissions, possibly ownership) in order for "
"the files to be linked together.  An example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3014
#, no-wrap
msgid "rsync -av --link-dest=$PWD/prior_dir host:src_dir/ new_dir/\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3022
msgid ""
"If files aren't linking, double-check their attributes.  Also check if some "
"attributes are getting forced outside of rsync's control, such a mount "
"option that squishes root to a single user, or mounts a removable drive with "
"generic ownership (such as OS X's \"Ignore ownership on this volume\" "
"option)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3029
msgid ""
"Multiple B<--link-dest> directories may be provided, which will cause rsync "
"to search the list in the order specified for an exact match (there is a "
"limit of 20 such directories).  If a match is found that differs only in "
"attributes, a local copy is made and the attributes updated.  If a match is "
"not found, a basis file from one of the I<DIRs> will be selected to try to "
"speed up the transfer."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3037
msgid ""
"Not every filesystem can hard-link a symlink, a device node, a FIFO or a "
"socket, and the destination need not agree with the one rsync was built on\\ "
"-- macOS builds on APFS, which can, and may write to HFS+, which cannot.  "
"Where the destination refuses to link such an entry, it is copied instead "
"and the transfer carries on, so only that entry loses the space saving.  "
"This applies to any refusal, because the error alone does not identify one: "
"link(2) reports B<EPERM> both for a filesystem without hard links and for an "
"ordinary permission refusal.  Regular files have always behaved this way."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3045
msgid ""
"One case is not covered.  With B<--hard-links> (B<-H>), a group of such "
"entries hard-linked to I<each other> in the source needs a second link, from "
"the first member to the rest, inside the destination itself.  Where that "
"link is refused too\\ -- a destination that cannot hard-link the type at "
"all\\ -- the members after the first are not created and the transfer "
"fails.  A B<--link-dest> on another filesystem is fine: only the link to "
"I<DIR> is impossible there, and the ones within the destination still "
"succeed."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3052
msgid ""
"This option works best when copying into an empty destination hierarchy, as "
"existing files may get their attributes tweaked, and that can affect "
"alternate destination files via hard-links.  Also, itemizing of changes can "
"get a bit muddled.  Note that prior to version 3.1.0 (September 2013), an "
"alternate-directory exact match would never be found (nor linked into the "
"destination) when a destination file already exists."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3057
msgid ""
"Note that if you combine this option with B<--ignore-times>, rsync will not "
"link any files together because it only links identical files together as a "
"substitute for transferring the file, never as an additional check after the "
"file is updated."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3060
msgid ""
"If I<DIR> is a relative path, it is relative to the destination directory.  "
"See also B<--compare-dest> and B<--copy-dest>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3066
msgid ""
"Note that rsync versions prior to 2.6.1 (April 2004) had a bug that could "
"prevent B<--link-dest> from working properly for a non-super-user when B<--"
"owner> (B<-o>) was specified (or implied).  You can work-around this bug by "
"avoiding the B<-o> option (or using B<--no-o>) when sending to a really old "
"remote rsync."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3066
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--compress>, B<-z>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3070
msgid ""
"With this option, rsync compresses the file data as it is sent to the "
"destination machine, which reduces the amount of data being transmitted\\ -- "
"something that is useful over a slow connection."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3074
msgid ""
"Rsync supports multiple compression methods and will choose one for you "
"unless you force the choice using the B<--compress-choice> (B<--zc>)  option."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3077
msgid ""
"Run B<rsync\\ --version> to see the default compress list compiled into your "
"version."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3085
msgid ""
"When both sides of the transfer are at least 3.2.0 (June 2020), each side "
"chooses its own most-preferred algorithm that also appears in the peer's "
"list.  Both sides order their lists strongest-first, so they converge on the "
"strongest mutual choice.  If no common compress choice is found, rsync exits "
"with an error.  If the remote rsync is too old to support checksum "
"negotiation, its list is assumed to be \"zlib\"."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3094
msgid ""
"The default order can be customized by setting the environment variable "
"B<RSYNC_COMPRESS_LIST> to a space-separated list of acceptable compression "
"names.  If the string contains a \"B<&>\" character, it is separated into "
"the \"client string & server string\", otherwise the same string applies to "
"both.  If the string (or string portion) contains no non-whitespace "
"characters, the default compress list is used.  Any unknown compression "
"names are discarded from the list, but a list with only invalid names "
"results in a failed negotiation."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3100
msgid ""
"There are some older rsync versions that were configured to reject a B<-z> "
"option and require the use of B<-zz> because their compression library was "
"not compatible with the default zlib compression method.  You can usually "
"ignore this weirdness unless the rsync server complains and tells you to "
"specify B<-zz>."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3100
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--compress-choice=STR>, B<--zc=STR>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3105
msgid ""
"This option can be used to override the automatic negotiation of the "
"compression algorithm that occurs when B<--compress> is used.  The option "
"implies B<--compress> unless \"none\" was specified, which instead implies "
"B<--no-compress>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3107
msgid "The compression options that you may be able to use are:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3111
msgid "B<zstd>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3113
msgid "B<lz4>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3115
msgid "B<zlibx>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3117
msgid "B<zlib>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3123
msgid ""
"Run B<rsync\\ --version> to see the default compress list compiled into your "
"version (which may differ from the list above)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3129
msgid ""
"Note that if you see an error about an option named B<--old-compress> or B<--"
"new-compress>, this is rsync trying to send the B<--compress-choice=zlib> or "
"B<--compress-choice=zlibx> option in a backward-compatible manner that more "
"rsync versions understand.  This error indicates that the older rsync "
"version on the server will not allow you to force the compression type."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3133
msgid ""
"Note that the \"zlibx\" compression algorithm is just the \"zlib\" algorithm "
"with matched data excluded from the compression stream (to try to make it "
"more compatible with an external zlib implementation)."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3133
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--compress-level=NUM>, B<--zl=NUM>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3139
msgid ""
"Explicitly set the compression level to use (see B<--compress>, B<-z>) "
"instead of using the default.  The B<--compress> option is implied as long "
"as the level chosen is not a \"don't compress\" level for the compression "
"algorithm that is in effect (e.g. zlib compression treats level 0 as "
"\"off\")."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3145
msgid ""
"The level values vary depending on the checksum in effect.  Because rsync "
"will negotiate a checksum choice by default (when the remote rsync is new "
"enough), it can be good to combine this option with a B<--compress-choice> "
"(B<--zc>) option unless you're sure of the choice in effect.  For example:"
msgstr "The level values vary depending on the checksum in effect.  Because rsync will negotiate a checksum choice by default (when the remote rsync is new enough), it can be good to combine this option with a B<--compress-choice> (B<--zc>) option unless you're sure of the choice in effect.  例:"

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3149
#, no-wrap
msgid "rsync -aiv --zc=zstd --zl=22 host:src/ dest/\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3155
msgid ""
"For zlib & zlibx compression the valid values are from 1 to 9 with 6 being "
"the default.  Specifying B<--zl=0> turns compression off, and specifying B<--"
"zl=-1> chooses the default level of 6."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3158
msgid ""
"For zstd compression the valid values are from -131072 to 22 with 3 being "
"the default. Specifying 0 chooses the default of 3."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3160
msgid "For lz4 compression there are no levels, so the value is always 0."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3165
msgid ""
"If you specify a too-large or too-small value, the number is silently "
"limited to a valid value.  This allows you to specify something like B<--"
"zl=999999999> and be assured that you'll end up with the maximum compression "
"level no matter what algorithm was chosen."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3170
msgid ""
"If you want to know the compression level that is in effect, specify B<--"
"debug=nstr> to see the \"negotiated string\" results.  This will report "
"something like \"B<Client\\ compress:\\ zstd\\ (level\\ 3)>\" (along with "
"the checksum choice in effect)."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3170
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--compress-threads=NUM>, B<--zt=NUM>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3175
msgid ""
"Set the number of threads to spawn when compressing data. Setting this "
"option to 1 or more will instruct the compression library to spawn 1 or more "
"threads for compression. Ideally, increasing the number of threads will "
"increase transfer speed if the transfer is CPU bound on the sender."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3177
msgid "This option does not affect decompression."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3179
msgid "Compression algorithms that allow threading:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3183
msgid "B<zstd> (only when libzstd is compiled with threading support)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3187
msgid ""
"This option is ignored if one of the above alogithms is not selected as the "
"B<--compression-choice> or if compression not enabled."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3187
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--skip-compress=LIST>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3190
msgid ""
"B<NOTE:> no compression method currently supports per-file compression "
"changes, so this option has no effect."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3197
msgid ""
"Override the list of file suffixes that will be compressed as little as "
"possible.  Rsync can set the compression level on a per-file basis based on "
"the file's suffix.  If the compression algorithm has an \"off\" level, then "
"no compression occurs for those files.  Other algorithms that support "
"changing the streaming level on-the-fly will have the level minimized to "
"reduces the CPU usage as much as possible for a matching file."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3201
msgid ""
"The B<LIST> should be one or more file suffixes (without the dot) separated "
"by slashes (B</>).  You may specify an empty string to indicate that no "
"files should be skipped."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3205
msgid ""
"Simple character-class matching is supported: each must consist of a list of "
"letters inside the square brackets (e.g. no special classes, such as \"[:"
"alpha:]\", are supported, and '-' has no special meaning)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3207
msgid ""
"The characters asterisk (B<*>) and question-mark (B<?>) have no special "
"meaning."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3210
msgid ""
"Here's an example that specifies 6 suffixes to skip (since 1 of the 5 rules "
"matches 2 suffixes):"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3214
#, no-wrap
msgid "--skip-compress=gz/jpg/mp[34]/7z/bz2\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3219
msgid ""
"The default file suffixes in the skip-compress list in this version of rsync "
"are:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3317
msgid ""
"3g2 3gp 7z aac ace apk avi bz2 deb dmg ear f4v flac flv gpg gz iso jar jpeg "
"jpg lrz lz lz4 lzma lzo m1a m1v m2a m2ts m2v m4a m4b m4p m4r m4v mka mkv mov "
"mp1 mp2 mp3 mp4 mpa mpeg mpg mpv mts odb odf odg odi odm odp ods odt oga ogg "
"ogm ogv ogx opus otg oth otp ots ott oxt png qt rar rpm rz rzip spx squashfs "
"sxc sxd sxg sxm sxw sz tbz tbz2 tgz tlz ts txz tzo vob war webm webp xz z "
"zip zst"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3323
msgid ""
"This list will be replaced by your B<--skip-compress> list in all but one "
"situation: a copy from a daemon rsync will add your skipped suffixes to its "
"list of non-compressing files (and its list may be configured to a different "
"default)."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3323
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--numeric-ids>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3326
msgid ""
"With this option rsync will transfer numeric group and user IDs rather than "
"using user and group names and mapping them at both ends."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3331
msgid ""
"By default rsync will use the username and groupname to determine what "
"ownership to give files.  The special uid 0 and the special group 0 are "
"never mapped via user/group names even if the B<--numeric-ids> option is not "
"specified."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3338
msgid ""
"If a user or group has no name on the source system or it has no match on "
"the destination system, then the numeric ID from the source system is used "
"instead.  See also the B<use\\ chroot> setting in the rsyncd.conf manpage "
"for some comments on how the chroot setting affects rsync's ability to look "
"up the names of the users and groups and what you can do about it."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3338
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--usermap=STRING>, B<--groupmap=STRING>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3349
msgid ""
"These options allow you to specify users and groups that should be mapped to "
"other values by the receiving side.  The B<STRING> is one or more B<FROM>:"
"B<TO> pairs of values separated by commas.  Any matching B<FROM> value from "
"the sender is replaced with a B<TO> value from the receiver.  You may "
"specify usernames or user IDs for the B<FROM> and B<TO> values, and the "
"B<FROM> value may also be a wild-card string, which will be matched against "
"the sender's names (wild-cards do NOT match against ID numbers, though see "
"below for why a 'B<*>' matches everything).  You may instead specify a range "
"of ID numbers via an inclusive range: LOW-HIGH.  For example:"
msgstr "These options allow you to specify users and groups that should be mapped to other values by the receiving side.  The B<STRING> is one or more B<FROM>:B<TO> pairs of values separated by commas.  Any matching B<FROM> value from the sender is replaced with a B<TO> value from the receiver.  You may specify usernames or user IDs for the B<FROM> and B<TO> values, and the B<FROM> value may also be a wild-card string, which will be matched against the sender's names (wild-cards do NOT match against ID numbers, though see below for why a 'B<*>' matches everything).  You may instead specify a range of ID numbers via an inclusive range: LOW-HIGH.  例:"

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3353
#, no-wrap
msgid "--usermap=0-99:nobody,wayne:admin,*:normal --groupmap=usr:1,1:usr\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3359
msgid ""
"The first match in the list is the one that is used.  You should specify all "
"your user mappings using a single B<--usermap> option, and/or all your group "
"mappings using a single B<--groupmap> option."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3365
msgid ""
"Note that the sender's name for the 0 user and group are not transmitted to "
"the receiver, so you should either match these values using a 0, or use the "
"names in effect on the receiving side (typically \"root\").  All other "
"B<FROM> names match those in use on the sending side.  All B<TO> names match "
"those in use on the receiving side."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3369
msgid ""
"Any IDs that do not have a name on the sending side are treated as having an "
"empty name for the purpose of matching.  This allows them to be matched via "
"a \"B<*>\" or using an empty name.  For instance:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3373
#, no-wrap
msgid "--usermap=:nobody --groupmap=*:nobody\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3379
msgid ""
"An empty B<FROM> value matches only sender-side IDs that have no name.  It "
"is not a wildcard for named users or groups; use \"B<*>\" when you want to "
"map every sender-side name."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3384
msgid ""
"When the B<--numeric-ids> option is used, the sender does not send any "
"names, so all the IDs are treated as having an empty name.  This means that "
"you will need to specify numeric B<FROM> values if you want to map these "
"nameless IDs to different values."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3389
msgid ""
"For the B<--usermap> option to work, the receiver will need to be running as "
"a super-user (see also the B<--super> and B<--fake-super> options).  For the "
"B<--groupmap> option to work, the receiver will need to have permissions to "
"set that group."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3394
msgid ""
"The B<--usermap> option implies the B<--owner> (B<-o>) option, and the B<--"
"groupmap> option implies the B<--group> (B<-g>) option (since rsync needs to "
"have those options enabled for the mapping options to work)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3397 original/man1/rsync.1:3411
msgid ""
"An older rsync client may need to use B<-s> to avoid a complaint about "
"wildcard characters, but a modern rsync handles this automatically."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3397
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--chown=USER:GROUP>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3404
msgid ""
"This option forces all files to be owned by USER with group GROUP.  This is "
"a simpler interface than using B<--usermap> & B<--groupmap> directly, but it "
"is implemented using those options internally, thus they cannot be mixed.  "
"If either the USER or GROUP is empty, no mapping for the omitted user/group "
"will occur.  If GROUP is empty, the trailing colon may be omitted, but if "
"USER is empty, a leading colon must be supplied."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3408
msgid ""
"If you specify \"B<--chown=foo:bar>\", this is exactly the same as "
"specifying \"B<--usermap=*:foo\\ --groupmap=*:bar>\", only easier (and with "
"the same implied B<--owner> and/or B<--group> options)."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3411
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--timeout=SECONDS>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3415
msgid ""
"This option allows you to set a maximum I/O timeout in seconds.  If no data "
"is transferred for the specified time then rsync will exit.  The default is "
"0, which means no timeout."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3415
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--contimeout=SECONDS>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3419
msgid ""
"This option allows you to set the amount of time that rsync will wait for "
"its connection to an rsync daemon to succeed.  If the timeout is reached, "
"rsync exits with an error."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3419 original/man1/rsync.1:4211
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--address=ADDRESS>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3423
msgid ""
"By default rsync will bind to the wildcard address when connecting to an "
"rsync daemon.  The B<--address> option allows you to specify a specific IP "
"address (or hostname) to bind to."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3425
msgid "See also the daemon version of the B<--address> option."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3425 original/man1/rsync.1:4252
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--port=PORT>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3430
msgid ""
"This specifies an alternate TCP port number to use rather than the default "
"of 873.  This is only needed if you are using the double-colon (::) syntax "
"to connect with an rsync daemon (since the URL syntax has a way to specify "
"the port as a part of the URL)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3432
msgid "See also the daemon version of the B<--port> option."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3432
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--sockopts=OPTIONS>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3439
msgid ""
"This option can provide endless fun for people who like to tune their "
"systems to the utmost degree.  You can set all sorts of socket options which "
"may make transfers faster (or slower!).  Read the manpage for the "
"B<setsockopt()> system call for details on some of the options you may be "
"able to set.  By default no special socket options are set.  This only "
"affects direct socket connections to a remote rsync daemon."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3441
msgid "See also the daemon version of the B<--sockopts> option."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3441
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--blocking-io>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3446
msgid ""
"This tells rsync to use blocking I/O when launching a remote shell "
"transport.  If the remote shell is either rsh or remsh, rsync defaults to "
"using blocking I/O, otherwise it defaults to using non-blocking I/O. (Note "
"that ssh prefers non-blocking I/O.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3446
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--outbuf=MODE>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3450
msgid ""
"This sets the output buffering mode.  The mode can be None (aka Unbuffered), "
"Line, or Block (aka Full).  You may specify as little as a single letter for "
"the mode, and use upper or lower case."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3453
msgid ""
"The main use of this option is to change Full buffering to Line buffering "
"when rsync's output is going to a file or pipe."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3453
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--itemize-changes>, B<-i>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3460
msgid ""
"Requests a simple itemized list of the changes that are being made to each "
"file, including attribute changes.  This is exactly the same as specifying "
"B<--out-format='%i\\ %n%L'>.  If you repeat the option, unchanged files will "
"also be output, but only if the receiving rsync is at least version 2.6.7 "
"(March 2006). You can use B<-vv> with older versions of rsync, but that also "
"turns on the output of other verbose messages."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3465
msgid ""
"The \"%i\" escape has a cryptic output that is 11 letters long.  The general "
"format is like the string B<YXcstpoguax>, where B<Y> is replaced by the type "
"of update being done, B<X> is replaced by the file-type, and the other "
"letters represent attributes that may be output if they are being modified."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3467
msgid "The update types that replace the B<Y> are as follows:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3471
msgid ""
"A B<E<lt>> means that a file is being transferred to the remote host (sent)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3474
msgid ""
"A B<E<gt>> means that a file is being transferred to the local host "
"(received)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3477
msgid ""
"A B<c> means that a local change/creation is occurring for the item (such as "
"the creation of a directory or the changing of a symlink, etc.)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3480
msgid ""
"A B<h> means that the item is a hard link to another item (requires B<--hard-"
"links>)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3483
msgid ""
"A B<.> means that the item is not being updated (though it might have "
"attributes that are being modified)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3486
msgid ""
"A B<*> means that the rest of the itemized-output area contains a message (e."
"g. \"deleting\")."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3491
msgid ""
"The file-types that replace the B<X> are: B<f> for a file, a B<d> for a "
"directory, an B<L> for a symlink, a B<D> for a device, and a B<S> for a "
"special file (e.g. named sockets and fifos)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3494
msgid ""
"The other letters in the string indicate if some attributes of the file have "
"changed, as follows:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3498
msgid "\"B<.>\" - the attribute is unchanged."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3500
msgid "\"B<+>\" - the file is newly created."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3502
msgid ""
"\"B<\\ >\" - all the attributes are unchanged (all dots turn to spaces)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3504
msgid "\"B<?>\" - the change is unknown (when the remote rsync is old)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3506
msgid "A letter indicates an attribute is being updated."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3509
msgid "The attribute that is associated with each letter is as follows:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3517
msgid ""
"A B<c> means either that a regular file has a different checksum (requires "
"B<--checksum>) or that a symlink, device, or special file has a changed "
"value.  Note that if you are sending files to an rsync prior to 3.0.1 (April "
"2008), this change flag will be present only for checksum-differing regular "
"files."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3520
msgid ""
"A B<s> means the size of a regular file is different and will be updated by "
"the file transfer."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3527
msgid ""
"A B<t> means the modification time is different and is being updated to the "
"sender's value (requires B<--times>).  An alternate value of B<T> means that "
"the modification time will be set to the transfer time, which happens when a "
"file/symlink/device is updated without B<--times> and when a symlink is "
"changed and the receiver can't set its time."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3530
msgid ""
"A B<p> means the permissions are different and are being updated to the "
"sender's value (requires B<--perms>)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3533
msgid ""
"An B<o> means the owner is different and is being updated to the sender's "
"value (requires B<--owner> and super-user privileges)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3536
msgid ""
"A B<g> means the group is different and is being updated to the sender's "
"value (requires B<--group> and the authority to set the group)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3541
msgid "A B<u>|B<n>|B<b> indicates the following information:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3544
msgid ""
"B<u> means the access (use) time is different and is being updated to the "
"sender's value (requires B<--atimes>)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3547
msgid ""
"B<n> means the create time (newness) is different and is being updated to "
"the sender's value (requires B<--crtimes>)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3549
msgid "B<b> means that both the access and create times are being updated"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3552
msgid "An B<a> means that the ACL information is being changed."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3554
msgid "An B<x> means that the extended attribute information is being changed."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3560
msgid ""
"One other output is possible: when deleting files, the \"%i\" will output "
"the string \"B<*deleting>\" for each item that is being removed (assuming "
"that you are talking to a recent enough rsync that it logs deletions instead "
"of outputting them as a verbose message)."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3560
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--out-format=FORMAT>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3568
msgid ""
"This allows you to specify exactly what the rsync client outputs to the user "
"on a per-update basis.  The format is a text string containing embedded "
"single-character escape sequences prefixed with a percent (%)  character.  A "
"default format of \"%n%L\" is assumed if either B<--info=name> or B<-v> is "
"specified (this tells you just the name of the file and, if the item is a "
"link, where it points).  For a full list of the possible escape characters, "
"see the B<log\\ format> setting in the rsyncd.conf manpage."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3577
msgid ""
"Specifying the B<--out-format> option implies the B<--info=name> option, "
"which will mention each file, directory, etc. that gets updated in a "
"significant way (a transferred file, a recreated symlink/device, or a "
"touched directory).  In addition, if the itemize-changes escape (%i) is "
"included in the string (e.g. if the B<--itemize-changes> option was used), "
"the logging of names increases to mention any item that is changed in any "
"way (as long as the receiving side is at least 2.6.4).  See the B<--itemize-"
"changes> option for a description of the output of \"%i\"."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3584
msgid ""
"Rsync will output the out-format string prior to a file's transfer unless "
"one of the transfer-statistic escapes is requested, in which case the "
"logging is done at the end of the file's transfer.  When this late logging "
"is in effect and B<--progress> is also specified, rsync will also output the "
"name of the file being transferred prior to its progress information "
"(followed, of course, by the out-format output)."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3584 original/man1/rsync.1:4258
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--log-file=FILE>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3591
msgid ""
"This option causes rsync to write the log of what it is doing to a file.  "
"This is similar to the logging that a daemon does, but can be requested for "
"the client side and/or the server side of a non-daemon transfer.  If "
"specified as a client option, transfer logging will be enabled with a "
"default format of \"%i %n%L\".  See the B<--log-file-format> option if you "
"wish to override this."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3594
msgid ""
"Here's an example command that requests the remote side to log what is "
"happening:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3598
#, no-wrap
msgid "rsync -av --remote-option=--log-file=/tmp/rlog src/ dest/\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3603
msgid ""
"This is very useful if you need to debug why a connection is closing "
"unexpectedly."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3605
msgid "See also the daemon version of the B<--log-file> option."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3605 original/man1/rsync.1:4263
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--log-file-format=FORMAT>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3612
msgid ""
"This allows you to specify exactly what per-update logging is put into the "
"file specified by the B<--log-file> option (which must also be specified for "
"this option to have any effect).  If you specify an empty string, updated "
"files will not be mentioned in the log file.  For a list of the possible "
"escape characters, see the B<log\\ format> setting in the rsyncd.conf "
"manpage."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3615
msgid ""
"The default FORMAT used if B<--log-file> is specified and this option is not "
"is '%i %n%L'."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3618
msgid "See also the daemon version of the B<--log-file-format> option."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3618
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--stats>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3624
msgid ""
"This tells rsync to print a verbose set of statistics on the file transfer, "
"allowing you to tell how effective rsync's delta-transfer algorithm is for "
"your data.  This option is equivalent to B<--info=stats2> if combined with 0 "
"or 1 B<-v> options, or B<--info=stats3> if combined with 2 or more B<-v> "
"options."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3626
msgid "The current statistics are as follows:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3635
msgid ""
"B<Number\\ of\\ files> is the count of all \"files\" (in the generic sense), "
"which includes directories, symlinks, etc.  The total count will be followed "
"by a list of counts by filetype (if the total is non-zero).  For example: "
"\"(reg: 5, dir: 3, link: 2, dev: 1, special: 1)\" lists the totals for "
"regular files, directories, symlinks, devices, and special files.  If any "
"value is 0, it is completely omitted from the list."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3639
msgid ""
"B<Number\\ of\\ created\\ files> is the count of how many \"files\" (generic "
"sense) were created (as opposed to updated).  The total count will be "
"followed by a list of counts by filetype (if the total is non-zero)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3646
msgid ""
"B<Number\\ of\\ deleted\\ files> is the count of how many \"files\" (generic "
"sense) were deleted.  The total count will be followed by a list of counts "
"by filetype (if the total is non-zero).  Note that this line is only output "
"if deletions are in effect, and only if the negotiated protocol is at least "
"31 (the default when both sides are 3.1.0, September 2013, or newer)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3653
msgid ""
"B<Number\\ of\\ 4\\ KiB\\ logical\\ blocks\\ touched> is the number of "
"unique 4 KiB logical file regions covered by rsync's own write operations. "
"This metric decouples network payload from the scope of local file "
"modifications.  For example, a small amount of I<Literal data> can result in "
"a massive number of touched blocks if the modifications are highly scattered "
"across a file, especially when using B<--inplace>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3657
msgid ""
"B<Number\\ of\\ regular\\ files\\ transferred> is the count of normal files "
"that were updated via rsync's delta-transfer algorithm, which does not "
"include directories, symlinks, etc."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3661
msgid ""
"B<Total\\ file\\ size> is the total sum of all file sizes in the transfer.  "
"This does not count any size for directories or special files, but does "
"include the size of symlinks."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3664
msgid ""
"B<Total\\ transferred\\ file\\ size> is the total sum of all files sizes for "
"just the transferred files."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3667
msgid ""
"B<Literal\\ data> is how much unmatched file-update data the sender had to "
"send to the receiver for it to recreate the updated files."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3670
msgid ""
"B<Matched\\ data> is how much data the receiver got locally when recreating "
"the updated files."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3675
msgid ""
"B<File\\ list\\ size> is how big the file-list data was when the sender sent "
"it to the receiver.  This is smaller than the in-memory size for the file "
"list due to some compressing of duplicated data when rsync sends the list."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3679
msgid ""
"B<File\\ list\\ generation\\ time> is the number of seconds that the sender "
"spent creating the file list.  This requires a modern rsync on the sending "
"side for this to be present."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3682
msgid ""
"B<File\\ list\\ transfer\\ time> is the number of seconds that the sender "
"spent sending the file list to the receiver."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3685
msgid ""
"B<Total\\ bytes\\ sent> is the count of all the bytes that rsync sent from "
"the client side to the server side."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3690
msgid ""
"B<Total\\ bytes\\ received> is the count of all non-message bytes that rsync "
"received by the client side from the server side. \"Non-message\" bytes "
"means that we don't count the bytes for a verbose message that the server "
"sent to us, which makes the stats more consistent."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3691
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--8-bit-output>, B<-8>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3697
msgid ""
"This tells rsync to leave all high-bit characters unescaped in the output "
"(to standard output or standard error)  instead of trying to test them to "
"see if they're valid in the current locale and escaping the invalid ones.  "
"All control characters (but never tabs) are always escaped, regardless of "
"this option's setting."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3702
msgid ""
"The escape idiom that started in 2.6.7 (March 2006) is to output a literal "
"backslash (B<\\e>) and a hash (B<#>), followed by exactly 3 octal digits.  "
"For example, a newline would output as \"B<\\e#012>\".  A literal backslash "
"that is in a filename is not escaped unless it is followed by a hash and 3 "
"digits (0-9)."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3702
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--human-readable>, B<-h>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3704
msgid ""
"Output numbers in a more human-readable format.  There are 3 possible levels:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3710
msgid ""
"output numbers with a separator between each set of 3 digits (either a comma "
"or a period, depending on if the decimal point is represented by a period or "
"a comma)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3713
msgid ""
"output numbers in units of 1000 (with a character suffix for larger units\\ "
"-- see below)."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3713
#, no-wrap
msgid "3."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3715
msgid "output numbers in units of 1024."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3720
msgid ""
"The default is human-readable level 1.  Each B<-h> option increases the "
"level by one.  You can take the level down to 0 (to output numbers as pure "
"digits) by specifying the B<--no-human-readable> (B<--no-h>) option."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3725
msgid ""
"The unit letters that are appended in levels 2 and 3 are: B<K> (kilo), B<M> "
"(mega), B<G> (giga), B<T> (tera), or B<P> (peta).  For example, a 1234567-"
"byte file would output as 1.23M in level-2 (assuming that a period is your "
"local decimal point)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3732
msgid ""
"Backward compatibility note: versions of rsync prior to 3.1.0 (September "
"2013) do not support human-readable level 1, and they default to level 0.  "
"Thus, specifying one or two B<-h> options will behave in a comparable manner "
"in old and new versions as long as you didn't specify a B<--no-h> option "
"prior to one or more B<-h> options.  See the B<--list-only> option for one "
"difference."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3732
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--partial>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3738
msgid ""
"By default, rsync will delete any partially transferred file if the transfer "
"is interrupted.  In some circumstances it is more desirable to keep "
"partially transferred files.  Using the B<--partial> option tells rsync to "
"keep the partial file which should make a subsequent transfer of the rest of "
"the file much faster."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3738
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--partial-dir=DIR>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3745
msgid ""
"This option modifies the behavior of the B<--partial> option while also "
"implying that it be enabled.  This enhanced partial-file method puts any "
"partially transferred files into the specified I<DIR> instead of writing the "
"partial file out to the destination file.  On the next transfer, rsync will "
"use a file found in this directory as data to speed up the resumption of the "
"transfer and then delete it after it has served its purpose."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3750
msgid ""
"Note that if B<--whole-file> is specified (or implied), any partial-dir "
"files that are found for a file that is being updated will simply be removed "
"(since rsync is sending files without using rsync's delta-transfer "
"algorithm)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3759
msgid ""
"Rsync will create the I<DIR> if it is missing, but just the last directory\\ "
"-- not the whole path.  This makes it easy to use a relative path (such as "
"\"B<--partial-dir=.rsync-partial>\") to have rsync create the partial-"
"directory in the destination file's directory when it is needed, and then "
"remove it again when the partial file is deleted.  Note that this directory "
"removal is only done for a relative pathname, as it is expected that an "
"absolute path is to a directory that is reserved for partial-dir work."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3767
msgid ""
"If the partial-dir value is not an absolute path, rsync will add an exclude "
"rule at the end of all your existing excludes.  This will prevent the "
"sending of any partial-dir files that may exist on the sending side, and "
"will also prevent the untimely deletion of partial-dir items on the "
"receiving side.  An example: the above B<--partial-dir> option would add the "
"equivalent of this \"perishable\" exclude at the end of any other filter "
"rules: B<-f\\ '-p\\ .rsync-partial/'>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3770
msgid ""
"If you are supplying your own exclude rules, you may need to add your own "
"exclude/hide/protect rule for the partial-dir because:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3774
msgid ""
"the auto-added rule may be ineffective at the end of your other rules, or"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3776
msgid "you may wish to override rsync's exclude choice."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3784
msgid ""
"For instance, if you want to make rsync clean-up any left-over partial-"
"directories that may be lying around, you should specify B<--delete-after> "
"and add a \"risk\" filter rule, e.g.  B<-f\\ 'R\\ .rsync-partial/'>. Avoid "
"using B<--delete-before> or B<--delete-during> unless you don't need rsync "
"to use any of the left-over partial-dir data during the current run."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3787
msgid ""
"IMPORTANT: the partial-dir directory should not be writable by other users "
"or it is a security risk! E.g. AVOID \"/tmp\"!"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3797
msgid ""
"You can also set the partial-dir value the B<RSYNC_PARTIAL_DIR> environment "
"variable.  Setting this in the environment does not force B<--partial> to be "
"enabled, but rather it affects where partial files go when B<--partial> is "
"specified.  For instance, instead of using B<--partial-dir=.rsync-tmp> along "
"with B<--progress>, you could set B<RSYNC_PARTIAL_DIR=.rsync-tmp> in your "
"environment and then use the B<-P> option to turn on the use of the .rsync-"
"tmp dir for partial transfers.  The only times that the B<--partial> option "
"does not look for this environment value are:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3802
msgid ""
"when B<--inplace> was specified (since B<--inplace> conflicts with B<--"
"partial-dir>), and"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3804
msgid "when B<--delay-updates> was specified (see below)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3811
msgid ""
"When a modern rsync resumes the transfer of a file in the partial-dir, that "
"partial file is now updated in-place instead of creating yet another tmp-"
"file copy (so it maxes out at dest + tmp instead of dest + partial + tmp).  "
"This requires both ends of the transfer to be at least version 3.2.0 (June "
"2020)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3817
msgid ""
"For the purposes of the daemon-config's \"B<refuse\\ options>\" setting, B<--"
"partial-dir> does I<not> imply B<--partial>.  This is so that a refusal of "
"the B<--partial> option can be used to disallow the overwriting of "
"destination files with a partial transfer, while still allowing the safer "
"idiom provided by B<--partial-dir>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3829
msgid ""
"This option puts the temporary file from each updated file into a holding "
"directory until the end of the transfer, at which time all the files are "
"renamed into place in rapid succession.  This attempts to make the updating "
"of the files a little closer to atomic.  By default the files are placed "
"into a directory named B<.~tmp~> in each file's destination directory, but "
"if you've specified the B<--partial-dir> option, that directory will be used "
"instead.  See the comments in the B<--partial-dir> section for a discussion "
"of how this B<.~tmp~> dir will be excluded from the transfer, and what you "
"can do if you want rsync to cleanup old B<.~tmp~> directories that might be "
"lying around.  Conflicts with B<--inplace> and B<--append>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3832
msgid ""
"This option implies B<--no-inc-recursive> since it needs the full file list "
"in memory in order to be able to iterate over it at the end."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3837
msgid ""
"This option uses more memory on the receiving side (one bit per file "
"transferred) and also requires enough free disk space on the receiving side "
"to hold an additional copy of all the updated files.  Note also that you "
"should not use an absolute path to B<--partial-dir> unless:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3843
msgid ""
"there is no chance of any of the files in the transfer having the same name "
"(since all the updated files will be put into a single directory if the path "
"is absolute), and"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3846
msgid ""
"there are no mount points in the hierarchy (since the delayed updates will "
"fail if they can't be renamed into place)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3851
msgid ""
"See also the \"atomic-rsync\" python script in the \"support\" subdirectory "
"for an update algorithm that is even closer to atomic (it uses B<--link-"
"dest> and a parallel hierarchy of files)."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3851
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--prune-empty-dirs>, B<-m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3857
msgid ""
"This option tells the receiving rsync to get rid of empty directories from "
"the file-list, including nested directories that have no non-directory "
"children.  This is useful for avoiding the creation of a bunch of useless "
"directories when the sending rsync is recursively scanning a hierarchy of "
"files using include/exclude/filter rules."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3860
msgid ""
"This option can still leave empty directories on the receiving side if you "
"make use of TRANSFER_RULES."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3867
msgid ""
"Because the file-list is actually being pruned, this option also affects "
"what directories get deleted when a delete is active.  However, keep in mind "
"that excluded files and directories can prevent existing items from being "
"deleted due to an exclude both hiding source files and protecting "
"destination files.  See the perishable filter-rule option for how to avoid "
"this."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3871
msgid ""
"You can prevent the pruning of certain empty directories from the file-list "
"by using a global \"protect\" filter.  For instance, this option would "
"ensure that the directory \"emptydir\" was kept in the file-list:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3875
#, no-wrap
msgid "--filter 'protect emptydir/'\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3882
msgid ""
"Here's an example that copies all .pdf files in a hierarchy, only creating "
"the necessary destination directories to hold the .pdf files, and ensures "
"that any superfluous files and directories in the destination are removed "
"(note the hide filter of non-directories being used instead of an exclude):"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3886
#, no-wrap
msgid "rsync -avm --del --include='*.pdf' -f 'hide,! */' src/ dest\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3892
msgid ""
"If you didn't want to remove superfluous destination files, the more time-"
"honored options of B<--include='*/'\\ --exclude='*'> would work fine in "
"place of the hide-filter (if that is more natural to you)."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3892
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--progress>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3898
msgid ""
"This option tells rsync to print information showing the progress of the "
"transfer.  This gives a bored user something to watch.  With a modern rsync "
"this is the same as specifying B<--info=flist2,name,progress>, but any user-"
"supplied settings for those info flags takes precedence (e.g.  B<--"
"info=flist0\\ --progress>)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3901
msgid ""
"While rsync is transferring a regular file, it updates a progress line that "
"looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3905
#, no-wrap
msgid "782448  63%  110.64kB/s    0:00:04\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3912
msgid ""
"In this example, the receiver has reconstructed 782448 bytes or 63% of the "
"sender's file, which is being reconstructed at a rate of 110.64 kilobytes "
"per second, and the transfer will finish in 4 seconds if the current rate is "
"maintained until the end."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3919
msgid ""
"These statistics can be misleading if rsync's delta-transfer algorithm is in "
"use.  For example, if the sender's file consists of the basis file followed "
"by additional data, the reported rate will probably drop dramatically when "
"the receiver gets to the literal data, and the transfer will probably take "
"much longer to finish than the receiver estimated as it was finishing the "
"matched part of the file."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3922
msgid ""
"When the file transfer finishes, rsync replaces the progress line with a "
"summary line that looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3926
#, no-wrap
msgid "1,238,099 100%  146.38kB/s    0:00:08  (xfr#5, to-chk=169/396)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3935
msgid ""
"In this example, the file was 1,238,099 bytes long in total, the average "
"rate of transfer for the whole file was 146.38 kilobytes per second over the "
"8 seconds that it took to complete, it was the 5th transfer of a regular "
"file during the current rsync session, and there are 169 more files for the "
"receiver to check (to see if they are up-to-date or not) remaining out of "
"the 396 total files in the file-list."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3945
msgid ""
"In an incremental recursion scan, rsync won't know the total number of files "
"in the file-list until it reaches the ends of the scan, but since it starts "
"to transfer files during the scan, it will display a line with the text \"ir-"
"chk\" (for incremental recursion check) instead of \"to-chk\" until the "
"point that it knows the full size of the list, at which point it will switch "
"to using \"to-chk\".  Thus, seeing \"ir-chk\" lets you know that the total "
"count of files in the file list is still going to increase (and each time it "
"does, the count of files left to check will increase by the number of the "
"files added to the list)."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3945
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<-P>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3949
msgid ""
"The B<-P> option is equivalent to \"B<--partial> B<--progress>\".  Its "
"purpose is to make it much easier to specify these two options for a long "
"transfer that may be interrupted."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3957
msgid ""
"There is also a B<--info=progress2> option that outputs statistics based on "
"the whole transfer, rather than individual files.  Use this flag without "
"outputting a filename (e.g. avoid B<-v> or specify B<--info=name0>) if you "
"want to see how the transfer is doing without scrolling the screen with a "
"lot of names. (You don't need to specify the B<--progress> option in order "
"to use B<--info=progress2>.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3968
msgid ""
"Finally, you can get an instant progress report by sending rsync a signal of "
"either SIGINFO or SIGVTALRM.  On BSD systems, a SIGINFO is generated by "
"typing a Ctrl+T (Linux doesn't currently support a SIGINFO signal).  When "
"the client-side process receives one of those signals, it sets a flag to "
"output a single progress report which is output when the current file "
"transfer finishes (so it may take a little time if a big file is being "
"handled when the signal arrives).  A filename is output (if needed)  "
"followed by the B<--info=progress2> format of progress info.  If you don't "
"know which of the 3 rsync processes is the client process, it's OK to signal "
"all of them (since the non-client processes ignore the signal)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3970
msgid ""
"CAUTION: sending SIGVTALRM to an older rsync (pre-3.2.0, June 2020) will "
"kill it."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3970
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--password-file=FILE>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3976
msgid ""
"This option allows you to provide a password for accessing an rsync daemon "
"via a file or via standard input if B<FILE> is B<->.  The file should "
"contain just the password on the first line (all other lines are ignored).  "
"Rsync will exit with an error if B<FILE> is world readable or if a root-run "
"rsync command finds a non-root-owned file."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3983
msgid ""
"This option does not supply a password to a remote shell transport such as "
"ssh; to learn how to do that, consult the remote shell's documentation.  "
"When accessing an rsync daemon using a remote shell as the transport, this "
"option only comes into effect after the remote shell finishes its "
"authentication (i.e. if you have also specified a password in the daemon's "
"config file)."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3983
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--early-input=FILE>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3988
msgid ""
"This option allows rsync to send up to 5K of data to the \"early exec\" "
"script on its stdin.  One possible use of this data is to give the script a "
"secret that can be used to mount an encrypted filesystem (which you should "
"unmount in the the \"post-xfer exec\" script)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3990
msgid "The daemon must be at least version 3.2.1 (June 2020)."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3990
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--list-only>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3994
msgid ""
"This option will cause the source files to be listed instead of "
"transferred.  This option is inferred if there is a single source argument "
"and no destination specified, so its main uses are:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:3999
msgid ""
"to turn a copy command that includes a destination argument into a file-"
"listing command, or"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4002
msgid ""
"to be able to specify more than one source argument.  Note: be sure to "
"include the destination."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4007
msgid ""
"CAUTION: keep in mind that a source argument with a wild-card is expanded by "
"the shell into multiple arguments, so it is never safe to try to specify a "
"single wild-card argument to try to infer this option. A safe example is:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4011
#, no-wrap
msgid "rsync -av --list-only foo* dest/\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4015
msgid "This option always uses an output format that looks similar to this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4020
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"drwxrwxr-x          4,096 2022/09/30 12:53:11 support\n"
"-rw-rw-r--             80 2005/01/11 10:37:37 support/Makefile\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4029
msgid ""
"The only option that affects this output style is the B<--human-readable> "
"(B<-h>) option.  The default is to output sizes as byte counts with digit "
"separators (in a 14-character-width column).  Specifying at least one B<-h> "
"option makes the sizes output with unit suffixes.  If you want old-style "
"bytecount sizes without digit separators (and an 11-character-width column) "
"use B<--no-h>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4038
msgid ""
"Compatibility note: when requesting a remote listing of files from an rsync "
"that is version 2.6.3 or older (i.e., pre-2005), you may encounter an error "
"if you ask for a non-recursive listing.  This is because a file listing "
"implies the B<--dirs> option without B<--recursive>, and older rsyncs don't "
"have that option.  To avoid this problem, either specify the B<--no-dirs> "
"option (if you don't need to expand a directory's content), or turn on "
"recursion and exclude the content of subdirectories: B<-r\\ --exclude='/*/"
"*'>."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4038 original/man1/rsync.1:4220
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--bwlimit=RATE>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4046
msgid ""
"This option allows you to specify the maximum transfer rate for the data "
"sent over the socket, specified in units per second.  The RATE value can be "
"suffixed with a string to indicate a size multiplier, and may be a "
"fractional value (e.g. B<--bwlimit=1.5m>).  If no suffix is specified, the "
"value will be assumed to be in units of 1024 bytes (as if \"K\" or \"KiB\" "
"had been appended).  See the B<--max-size> option for a description of all "
"the available suffixes.  A value of 0 specifies no limit."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4050
msgid ""
"For backward-compatibility reasons, the rate limit will be rounded to the "
"nearest KiB unit, so no rate smaller than 1024 bytes per second is possible."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4056
msgid ""
"Rsync writes data over the socket in blocks, and this option both limits the "
"size of the blocks that rsync writes, and tries to keep the average transfer "
"rate at the requested limit.  Some burstiness may be seen where rsync writes "
"out a block of data and then sleeps to bring the average rate into "
"compliance."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4062
msgid ""
"Due to the internal buffering of data, the B<--progress> option may not be "
"an accurate reflection on how fast the data is being sent.  This is because "
"some files can show up as being rapidly sent when the data is quickly "
"buffered, while other can show up as very slow when the flushing of the "
"output buffer occurs.  This may be fixed in a future version."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4064
msgid "See also the daemon version of the B<--bwlimit> option."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4064
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--stop-after=MINS>, (B<--time-limit=MINS>)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4067
msgid ""
"This option tells rsync to stop copying when the specified number of minutes "
"has elapsed."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4073
msgid ""
"For maximal flexibility, rsync does not communicate this option to the "
"remote rsync since it is usually enough that one side of the connection "
"quits as specified.  This allows the option's use even when only one side of "
"the connection supports it.  You can tell the remote side about the time "
"limit using B<--remote-option> (B<-M>), should the need arise."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4075
msgid "The B<--time-limit> version of this option is deprecated."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4075
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--stop-at=y-m-dTh:m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4081
msgid ""
"This option tells rsync to stop copying when the specified point in time has "
"been reached. The date & time can be fully specified in a numeric format of "
"year-month-dayThour:minute (e.g. 2000-12-31T23:59) in the local timezone.  "
"You may choose to separate the date numbers using slashes instead of dashes."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4087
msgid ""
"The value can also be abbreviated in a variety of ways, such as specifying a "
"2-digit year and/or leaving off various values.  In all cases, the value "
"will be taken to be the next possible point in time where the supplied "
"information matches.  If the value specifies the current time or a past "
"time, rsync exits with an error."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4092
msgid ""
"For example, \"1-30\" specifies the next January 30th (at midnight local "
"time), \"14:00\" specifies the next 2 P.M., \"1\" specifies the next 1st of "
"the month at midnight, \"31\" specifies the next month where we can stop on "
"its 31st day, and \":59\" specifies the next 59th minute after the hour."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4100
msgid ""
"For maximal flexibility, rsync does not communicate this option to the "
"remote rsync since it is usually enough that one side of the connection "
"quits as specified.  This allows the option's use even when only one side of "
"the connection supports it.  You can tell the remote side about the time "
"limit using B<--remote-option> (B<-M>), should the need arise.  Do keep in "
"mind that the remote host may have a different default timezone than your "
"local host."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4100
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--fsync>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4104
msgid ""
"Cause the receiving side to fsync each finished file.  This may slow down "
"the transfer, but can help to provide peace of mind when updating critical "
"files."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4104
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--write-batch=FILE>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4108
msgid ""
"Record a file that can later be applied to another identical destination "
"with B<--read-batch>.  See the \"BATCH MODE\" section for details, and also "
"the B<--only-write-batch> option."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4113
msgid ""
"This option overrides the negotiated checksum & compress lists and always "
"negotiates a choice based on old-school md5/md4/zlib choices.  This means "
"batch mode is not compatible with newer compression choices such as zstd or "
"lz4."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4113
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--only-write-batch=FILE>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4118
msgid ""
"Works like B<--write-batch>, except that no updates are made on the "
"destination system when creating the batch.  This lets you transport the "
"changes to the destination system via some other means and then apply the "
"changes via B<--read-batch>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4125
msgid ""
"Note that you can feel free to write the batch directly to some portable "
"media: if this media fills to capacity before the end of the transfer, you "
"can just apply that partial transfer to the destination and repeat the whole "
"process to get the rest of the changes (as long as you don't mind a "
"partially updated destination system while the multi-update cycle is "
"happening)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4130
msgid ""
"Also note that you only save bandwidth when pushing changes to a remote "
"system because this allows the batched data to be diverted from the sender "
"into the batch file without having to flow over the wire to the receiver "
"(when pulling, the sender is remote, and thus can't write the batch)."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4130
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--read-batch=FILE>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4134
msgid ""
"Apply all of the changes stored in FILE, a file previously generated by B<--"
"write-batch>.  If I<FILE> is B<->, the batch data will be read from standard "
"input. See the \"BATCH MODE\" section for details."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4134
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--protocol=NUM>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4142
msgid ""
"Force an older protocol version to be used.  This is useful for creating a "
"batch file that is compatible with an older version of rsync.  For instance, "
"if rsync 2.6.4 is being used with the B<--write-batch> option, but rsync "
"2.6.3 is what will be used to run the B<--read-batch> option, you should use "
"\"--protocol=28\" when creating the batch file to force the older protocol "
"version to be used in the batch file (assuming you can't upgrade the rsync "
"on the reading system)."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4142
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--iconv=CONVERT_SPEC>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4153
msgid ""
"Rsync can convert filenames between character sets using this option.  Using "
"a CONVERT_SPEC of \".\" tells rsync to look up the default character-set via "
"the locale setting.  Alternately, you can fully specify what conversion to "
"do by giving a local and a remote charset separated by a comma in the order "
"B<--iconv=LOCAL,REMOTE>, e.g. B<--iconv=utf8,iso88591>.  This order ensures "
"that the option will stay the same whether you're pushing or pulling files.  "
"Finally, you can specify either B<--no-iconv> or a CONVERT_SPEC of \"-\" to "
"turn off any conversion.  The default setting of this option is site-"
"specific, and can also be affected via the B<RSYNC_ICONV> environment "
"variable."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4156
msgid ""
"For a list of what charset names your local iconv library supports, you can "
"run \"B<iconv\\ --list>\"."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4160
msgid ""
"If you specify the B<--secluded-args> (B<-s>) option, rsync will translate "
"the filenames you specify on the command-line that are being sent to the "
"remote host.  See also the B<--files-from> option."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4166
msgid ""
"Note that rsync does not do any conversion of names in filter files "
"(including include/exclude files).  It is up to you to ensure that you're "
"specifying matching rules that can match on both sides of the transfer.  For "
"instance, you can specify extra include/exclude rules if there are filename "
"differences on the two sides that need to be accounted for."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4172
msgid ""
"When you pass an B<--iconv> option to an rsync daemon that allows it, the "
"daemon uses the charset specified in its \"charset\" configuration parameter "
"regardless of the remote charset you actually pass.  Thus, you may feel free "
"to specify just the local charset for a daemon transfer (e.g.  B<--"
"iconv=utf8>)."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4172 original/man1/rsync.1:4283
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--ipv4>, B<-4> or B<--ipv6>, B<-6>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4180
msgid ""
"Tells rsync to prefer IPv4/IPv6 when creating sockets or running ssh.  This "
"affects sockets that rsync has direct control over, such as the outgoing "
"socket when directly contacting an rsync daemon, as well as the forwarding "
"of the B<-4> or B<-6> option to ssh when rsync can deduce that ssh is being "
"used as the remote shell.  For other remote shells you'll need to specify "
"the \"B<--rsh\\ SHELL\\ -4>\" option directly (or whatever IPv4/IPv6 hint "
"options it uses)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4182
msgid "See also the daemon version of these options."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4186 original/man1/rsync.1:4296
msgid ""
"If rsync was compiled without support for IPv6, the B<--ipv6> option will "
"have no effect.  The B<rsync\\ --version> output will contain \"B<no\\ "
"IPv6>\" if is the case."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4186
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--checksum-seed=NUM>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4195
msgid ""
"Set the checksum seed to the integer NUM.  This 4 byte checksum seed is "
"included in each block and MD4 file checksum calculation (the more modern "
"file checksums don't use a seed).  By default the checksum seed is generated "
"by the server and defaults to the current B<time>().  This option is used to "
"set a specific checksum seed, which is useful for applications that want "
"repeatable block checksums, or in the case where the user wants a more "
"random checksum seed.  Setting NUM to 0 causes rsync to use the default of "
"B<time>() for checksum seed."
msgstr ""

#. type: SH
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4196
#, no-wrap
msgid "DAEMON OPTIONS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4199
msgid "The options allowed when starting an rsync daemon are as follows:"
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4200
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--daemon>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4204
msgid ""
"This tells rsync that it is to run as a daemon.  The daemon you start "
"running may be accessed using an rsync client using the B<host::module> or "
"B<rsync://host/module/> syntax."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4209
msgid ""
"If standard input is a socket then rsync will assume that it is being run "
"via inetd, otherwise it will detach from the current terminal and become a "
"background daemon.  The daemon will read the config file (rsyncd.conf) on "
"each connect made by a client and respond to requests accordingly."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4211
msgid "See the B<rsyncd.conf>(5) manpage for more details."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4216
msgid ""
"By default rsync will bind to the wildcard address when run as a daemon with "
"the B<--daemon> option.  The B<--address> option allows you to specify a "
"specific IP address (or hostname) to bind to.  This makes virtual hosting "
"possible in conjunction with the B<--config> option."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4220
msgid ""
"See also the address global option in the rsyncd.conf manpage and the client "
"version of the B<--address> option."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4224
msgid ""
"This option allows you to specify the maximum transfer rate for the data the "
"daemon sends over the socket.  The client can still specify a smaller B<--"
"bwlimit> value, but no larger value will be allowed."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4227
msgid ""
"See the client version of the B<--bwlimit> option for some extra details."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4227
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--config=FILE>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4233
msgid ""
"This specifies an alternate config file than the default.  This is only "
"relevant when B<--daemon> is specified.  The default is /etc/rsyncd.conf "
"unless the daemon is running over a remote shell program and the remote user "
"is not the super-user; in that case the default is rsyncd.conf in the "
"current directory (typically $HOME)."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4233
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--dparam=OVERRIDE>, B<-M>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4239
msgid ""
"This option can be used to set a daemon-config parameter when starting up "
"rsync in daemon mode.  It is equivalent to adding the parameter at the end "
"of the global settings prior to the first module's definition.  The "
"parameter names can be specified without spaces, if you so desire.  For "
"instance:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4243
#, no-wrap
msgid "rsync --daemon -M pidfile=/path/rsync.pid\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4245
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--no-detach>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4252
msgid ""
"When running as a daemon, this option instructs rsync to not detach itself "
"and become a background process.  This option is required when running as a "
"service on Cygwin, and may also be useful when rsync is supervised by a "
"program such as B<daemontools> or AIX's B<System\\ Resource\\ Controller>.  "
"B<--no-detach> is also recommended when rsync is run under a debugger.  This "
"option has no effect if rsync is run from inetd or sshd."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4255
msgid ""
"This specifies an alternate TCP port number for the daemon to listen on "
"rather than the default of 873."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4258
msgid ""
"See also the client version of the B<--port> option and the port global "
"setting in the rsyncd.conf manpage."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4261
msgid ""
"This option tells the rsync daemon to use the given log-file name instead of "
"using the \"B<log\\ file>\" setting in the config file."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4263
msgid "See also the client version of the B<--log-file> option."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4268
msgid ""
"This option tells the rsync daemon to use the given FORMAT string instead of "
"using the \"B<log\\ format>\" setting in the config file.  It also enables "
"\"B<transfer\\ logging>\" unless the string is empty, in which case transfer "
"logging is turned off."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4271
msgid "See also the client version of the B<--log-file-format> option."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4271
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--sockopts>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4274
msgid ""
"This overrides the B<socket\\ options> setting in the rsyncd.conf file and "
"has the same syntax."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4276
msgid "See also the client version of the B<--sockopts> option."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4281
msgid ""
"This option increases the amount of information the daemon logs during its "
"startup phase.  After the client connects, the daemon's verbosity level will "
"be controlled by the options that the client used and the \"B<max\\ "
"verbosity>\" setting in the module's config section."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4283
msgid "See also the client version of the B<--verbose> option."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4290
msgid ""
"Tells rsync to prefer IPv4/IPv6 when creating the incoming sockets that the "
"rsync daemon will use to listen for connections.  One of these options may "
"be required in older versions of Linux to work around an IPv6 bug in the "
"kernel (if you see an \"address already in use\" error when nothing else is "
"using the port, try specifying B<--ipv6> or B<--ipv4> when starting the "
"daemon)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4292
msgid "See also the client version of these options."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4296
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--help>, B<-h>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4299
msgid ""
"When specified after B<--daemon>, print a short help page describing the "
"options available for starting an rsync daemon."
msgstr ""

#. type: SH
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4300
#, no-wrap
msgid "FILTER RULES"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4304
msgid ""
"The filter rules allow for custom control of several aspects of how files "
"are handled:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4308
msgid ""
"Control which files the sending side puts into the file list that describes "
"the transfer hierarchy"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4311
msgid ""
"Control which files the receiving side protects from deletion when the file "
"is not in the sender's file list"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4313
msgid "Control which extended attribute names are skipped when copying xattrs"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4318
msgid ""
"The rules are either directly specified via option arguments or they can be "
"read in from one or more files.  The filter-rule files can even be a part of "
"the hierarchy of files being copied, affecting different parts of the tree "
"in different ways."
msgstr ""

#. type: SS
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4319
#, no-wrap
msgid "SIMPLE INCLUDE/EXCLUDE RULES"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4325
msgid ""
"We will first cover the basics of how include & exclude rules affect what "
"files are transferred, ignoring any deletion side-effects.  Filter rules "
"mainly affect the contents of directories that rsync is \"recursing\" into, "
"but they can also affect a top-level item in the transfer that was specified "
"as a argument."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4331
msgid ""
"The default for any unmatched file/directory is for it to be included in the "
"transfer, which puts the file/directory into the sender's file list.  The "
"use of an exclude rule causes one or more matching files/directories to be "
"left out of the sender's file list.  An include rule can be used to limit "
"the effect of an exclude rule that is matching too many files."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4336
msgid ""
"The order of the rules is important because the first rule that matches is "
"the one that takes effect.  Thus, if an early rule excludes a file, no "
"include rule that comes after it can have any effect. This means that you "
"must place any include overrides somewhere prior to the exclude that it is "
"intended to limit."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4340
msgid ""
"When a directory is excluded, all its contents and sub-contents are also "
"excluded.  The sender doesn't scan through any of it at all, which can save "
"a lot of time when skipping large unneeded sub-trees."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4351
msgid ""
"It is also important to understand that the include/exclude rules are "
"applied to every file and directory that the sender is recursing into. Thus, "
"if you want a particular deep file to be included, you have to make sure "
"that none of the directories that must be traversed on the way down to that "
"file are excluded or else the file will never be discovered to be included. "
"As an example, if the directory \"B<a/path>\" was given as a transfer "
"argument and you want to ensure that the file \"B<a/path/down/deep/wanted."
"txt>\" is a part of the transfer, then the sender must not exclude the "
"directories \"B<a/path>\", \"B<a/path/down>\", or \"B<a/path/down/deep>\" as "
"it makes it way scanning through the file tree."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4359
msgid ""
"When you are working on the rules, it can be helpful to ask rsync to tell "
"you what is being excluded/included and why.  Specifying B<--debug=FILTER> "
"or (when pulling files) B<-M--debug=FILTER> turns on level 1 of the FILTER "
"debug information that will output a message any time that a file or "
"directory is included or excluded and which rule it matched.  Beginning in "
"3.2.4 it will also warn if a filter rule has trailing whitespace, since an "
"exclude of \"foo\\ \" (with a trailing space) will not exclude a file named "
"\"foo\"."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4363
msgid ""
"Exclude and include rules can specify wildcard PATTERN MATCHING RULES "
"(similar to shell wildcards) that allow you to match things like a file "
"suffix or a portion of a filename."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4366
msgid ""
"A rule can be limited to only affecting a directory by putting a trailing "
"slash onto the filename."
msgstr ""

#. type: SS
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4367
#, no-wrap
msgid "SIMPLE INCLUDE/EXCLUDE EXAMPLE"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4370
msgid "With the following file tree created on the sending side:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4380
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"mkdir x/\n"
"touch x/file.txt\n"
"mkdir x/y/\n"
"touch x/y/file.txt\n"
"touch x/y/zzz.txt\n"
"mkdir x/z/\n"
"touch x/z/file.txt\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4386
msgid ""
"Then the following rsync command will transfer the file \"B<x/y/file.txt>\" "
"and the directories needed to hold it, resulting in the path \"B</tmp/x/y/"
"file.txt>\" existing on the remote host:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4390
#, no-wrap
msgid "rsync -ai -f'+ x/' -f'+ x/y/' -f'+ x/y/file.txt' -f'- *' x host:/tmp/\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4395
msgid ""
"Aside: this copy could also have been accomplished using the B<-R> option "
"(though the 2 commands behave differently if deletions are enabled):"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4399
#, no-wrap
msgid "rsync -aiR x/y/file.txt host:/tmp/\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4405
msgid ""
"The following command does not need an include of the \"x\" directory "
"because it is not a part of the transfer (note the trailing slash).  Running "
"this command would copy just \"B</tmp/x/file.txt>\" because the \"y\" and "
"\"z\" directories get excluded:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4409
#, no-wrap
msgid "rsync -ai -f'+ file.txt' -f'- *' x/ host:/tmp/x/\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4414
msgid ""
"This command would omit the zzz.txt file while copying \"x\" and everything "
"else it contains:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4418
#, no-wrap
msgid "rsync -ai -f'- zzz.txt' x host:/tmp/\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: SS
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4421
#, no-wrap
msgid "FILTER RULES WHEN DELETING"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4430
msgid ""
"By default the include & exclude filter rules affect both the sender (as it "
"creates its file list)  and the receiver (as it creates its file lists for "
"calculating deletions).  If no delete option is in effect, the receiver "
"skips creating the delete-related file lists.  This two-sided default can be "
"manually overridden so that you are only specifying sender rules or receiver "
"rules, as described in the FILTER RULES IN DEPTH section."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4435
msgid ""
"When deleting, an exclude protects a file from being removed on the "
"receiving side while an include overrides that protection (putting the file "
"at risk of deletion). The default is for a file to be at risk\\ -- its "
"safety depends on it matching a corresponding file from the sender."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4441
msgid ""
"An example of the two-sided exclude effect can be illustrated by the copying "
"of a C development directory between 2 systems.  When doing a touch-up copy, "
"you might want to skip copying the built executable and the B<.o> files "
"(sender hide) so that the receiving side can build their own and not lose "
"any object files that are already correct (receiver protect).  For instance:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4445
#, no-wrap
msgid "rsync -ai --del -f'- *.o' -f'- cmd' src host:/dest/\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4451
msgid ""
"Note that using B<-f'-p\\ *.o'> is even better than B<-f'-\\ *.o'> if there "
"is a chance that the directory structure may have changed.  The \"p\" "
"modifier is discussed in FILTER RULE MODIFIERS."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4456
msgid ""
"One final note, if your shell doesn't mind unexpanded wildcards, you could "
"simplify the typing of the filter options by using an underscore in place of "
"the space and leaving off the quotes.  For instance, B<-f\\ -_*.o\\ -f\\ -"
"_cmd> (and similar) could be used instead of the filter options above."
msgstr ""

#. type: SS
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4457
#, no-wrap
msgid "FILTER RULES IN DEPTH"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4466
msgid ""
"Rsync supports old-style include/exclude rules and new-style filter rules.  "
"The older rules are specified using B<--include> and B<--exclude> as well as "
"the B<--include-from> and B<--exclude-from>. These are limited in behavior "
"but they don't require a \"-\" or \"+\" prefix.  An old-style exclude rule "
"is turned into a \"B<-\\ name>\" filter rule (with no modifiers) and an old-"
"style include rule is turned into a \"B<+\\ name>\" filter rule (with no "
"modifiers)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4469
msgid ""
"Rsync builds an ordered list of filter rules as specified on the command-"
"line and/or read-in from files.  New style filter rules have the following "
"syntax:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4474
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"RULE [PATTERN_OR_FILENAME]\n"
"RULE,MODIFIERS [PATTERN_OR_FILENAME]\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4483
msgid ""
"You have your choice of using either short or long RULE names, as described "
"below.  If you use a short-named rule, the ',' separating the RULE from the "
"MODIFIERS is optional.  The PATTERN or FILENAME that follows (when present)  "
"must come after either a single space or an underscore (_). Any additional "
"spaces and/or underscores are considered to be a part of the pattern name.  "
"Here are the available rule prefixes:"
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4484
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<exclude,\\ '-'>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4487
msgid ""
"specifies an exclude pattern that (by default) is both a B<hide> and a "
"B<protect>."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4487
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<include,\\ '+'>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4490
msgid ""
"specifies an include pattern that (by default) is both a B<show> and a "
"B<risk>."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4490
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<merge,\\ '.'>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4493
msgid "specifies a merge-file on the client side to read for more rules."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4493
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<dir-merge,\\ ':'>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4497
msgid ""
"specifies a per-directory merge-file.  Using this kind of filter rule "
"requires that you trust the sending side's filter checking, so it has the "
"side-effect mentioned under the B<--trust-sender> option."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4497
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<hide,\\ 'H'>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4501
msgid ""
"specifies a pattern for hiding files from the transfer.  Equivalent to a "
"sender-only exclude, so B<-f'H\\ foo'> could also be specified as B<-f'-s\\ "
"foo'>."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4501
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<show,\\ 'S'>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4504
msgid ""
"files that match the pattern are not hidden. Equivalent to a sender-only "
"include, so B<-f'S\\ foo'> could also be specified as B<-f'+s\\ foo'>."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4504
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<protect,\\ 'P'>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4508
msgid ""
"specifies a pattern for protecting files from deletion.  Equivalent to a "
"receiver-only exclude, so B<-f'P\\ foo'> could also be specified as B<-f'-"
"r\\ foo'>."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4508
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<risk,\\ 'R'>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4511
msgid ""
"files that match the pattern are not protected. Equivalent to a receiver-"
"only include, so B<-f'R\\ foo'> could also be specified as B<-f'+r\\ foo'>."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4511
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<clear,\\ '!'>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4513
msgid "clears the current include/exclude list (takes no argument)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4517
msgid ""
"When rules are being read from a file (using merge or dir-merge), empty "
"lines are ignored, as are whole-line comments that start with a 'B<#>' "
"(filename rules that contain a hash character are unaffected)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4523
msgid ""
"Note also that the B<--filter>, B<--include>, and B<--exclude> options take "
"one rule/pattern each.  To add multiple ones, you can repeat the options on "
"the command-line, use the merge-file syntax of the B<--filter> option, or "
"the B<--include-from> / B<--exclude-from> options."
msgstr ""

#. type: SS
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4524
#, no-wrap
msgid "PATTERN MATCHING RULES"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4530
msgid ""
"Most of the rules mentioned above take an argument that specifies what the "
"rule should match.  If rsync is recursing through a directory hierarchy, "
"keep in mind that each pattern is matched against the name of every "
"directory in the descent path as rsync finds the filenames to send."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4532
msgid "The matching rules for the pattern argument take several forms:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4542
msgid ""
"If a pattern contains a B</> (not counting a trailing slash) or a \"B<**>\" "
"(which can match a slash), then the pattern is matched against the full "
"pathname, including any leading directories within the transfer.  If the "
"pattern doesn't contain a (non-trailing) B</> or a \"B<**>\", then it is "
"matched only against the final component of the filename or pathname. For "
"example, B<foo> means that the final path component must be \"foo\" while "
"B<foo/bar> would match the last 2 elements of the path (as long as both "
"elements are within the transfer)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4545
msgid ""
"A pattern that ends with a B</> only matches a directory, not a regular "
"file, symlink, or device."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4553
msgid ""
"A pattern that starts with a B</> is anchored to the start of the transfer "
"path instead of the end.  For example, B</foo/**> or B</foo/bar/**> match "
"only leading elements in the path.  If the rule is read from a per-directory "
"filter file, the transfer path being matched will begin at the level of the "
"filter file instead of the top of the transfer.  See the section on "
"ANCHORING INCLUDE/EXCLUDE PATTERNS for a full discussion of how to specify a "
"pattern that matches at the root of the transfer."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4557
msgid ""
"Rsync chooses between doing a simple string match and wildcard matching by "
"checking if the pattern contains one of these three wildcard characters: "
"'B<*>', \\&'B<?>', and 'B<[>' :"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4560
msgid "a 'B<?>' matches any single character except a slash (B</>)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4562
msgid "a 'B<*>' matches zero or more non-slash characters."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4564
msgid "a 'B<**>' matches zero or more characters, including slashes."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4567
msgid ""
"a 'B<[>' introduces a character class, such as B<[a-z]> or B<[[:alpha:]]>, "
"that must match one character."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4573
msgid ""
"a trailing B<***> in the pattern is a shorthand that allows you to match a "
"directory and all its contents using a single rule.  For example, specifying "
"\"B<dir_name/***>\" will match both the \"dir_name\" directory (as if "
"\"B<dir_name/>\" had been specified) and everything in the directory (as if "
"\"B<dir_name/**>\" had been specified)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4579
msgid ""
"a backslash can be used to escape a wildcard character, but it is only "
"interpreted as an escape character if at least one wildcard character is "
"present in the match pattern. For instance, the pattern \"B<foo\\ebar>\" "
"matches that single backslash literally, while the pattern \"B<foo\\ebar*>\" "
"would need to be changed to \"B<foo\\e\\ebar*>\" to avoid the \"B<\\eb>\" "
"becoming just \"b\"."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4581
msgid "Here are some examples of exclude/include matching:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4584
msgid "Option B<-f'-\\ *.o'> would exclude all filenames ending with B<.o>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4587
msgid ""
"Option B<-f'-\\ /foo'> would exclude a file (or directory) named foo in the "
"transfer-root directory"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4589
msgid "Option B<-f'-\\ foo/'> would exclude any directory named foo"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4592
msgid ""
"Option B<-f'-\\ foo/*/bar'> would exclude any file/directory named bar which "
"is at two levels below a directory named foo (if foo is in the transfer)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4596
msgid ""
"Option B<-f'-\\ /foo/**/bar'> would exclude any file/directory named bar "
"that was two or more levels below a top-level directory named foo (note "
"that /foo/bar is B<not> excluded by this)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4599
msgid ""
"Options B<-f'+\\ */'\\ -f'+\\ *.c'\\ -f'-\\ *'> would include all "
"directories and .c source files but nothing else"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4603
msgid ""
"Options B<-f'+\\ foo/'\\ -f'+\\ foo/bar.c'\\ -f'-\\ *'> would include only "
"the foo directory and foo/bar.c (the foo directory must be explicitly "
"included or it would be excluded by the \"B<-\\ *>\")"
msgstr ""

#. type: SS
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4604
#, no-wrap
msgid "FILTER RULE MODIFIERS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4607
msgid ""
"The following modifiers are accepted after an include (+) or exclude (-) "
"rule:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4615
msgid ""
"A B</> specifies that the include/exclude rule should be matched against the "
"absolute pathname of the current item.  For example, B<-f'-/\\ /etc/passwd'> "
"would exclude the passwd file any time the transfer was sending files from "
"the \"/etc\" directory, and \"-/ subdir/foo\" would always exclude \"foo\" "
"when it is in a directory named \"subdir\", even if \"foo\" is at the root "
"of the current transfer."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4618
msgid ""
"A B<!> specifies that the include/exclude should take effect if the pattern "
"fails to match.  For instance, B<-f'-!\\ */'> would exclude all non-"
"directories."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4621
msgid ""
"A B<C> is used to indicate that all the global CVS-exclude rules should be "
"inserted as excludes in place of the \"-C\".  No argument should follow."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4628
msgid ""
"An B<s> is used to indicate that the rule applies to the sending side.  When "
"a rule affects the sending side, it affects what files are put into the "
"sender's file list.  The default is for a rule to affect both sides unless "
"B<--delete-excluded> was specified, in which case default rules become "
"sender-side only.  See also the hide (H) and show (S) rules, which are an "
"alternate way to specify sending-side includes/excludes."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4633
msgid ""
"An B<r> is used to indicate that the rule applies to the receiving side.  "
"When a rule affects the receiving side, it prevents files from being "
"deleted.  See the B<s> modifier for more info.  See also the protect (P) and "
"risk (R) rules, which are an alternate way to specify receiver-side includes/"
"excludes."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4640
msgid ""
"A B<p> indicates that a rule is perishable, meaning that it is ignored in "
"directories that are being deleted.  For instance, the B<--cvs-exclude> (B<-"
"C>) option's default rules that exclude things like \"CVS\" and \"B<*.o>\" "
"are marked as perishable, and will not prevent a directory that was removed "
"on the source from being deleted on the destination."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4645
msgid ""
"An B<x> indicates that a rule affects xattr names in xattr copy/delete "
"operations (and is thus ignored when matching file/directory names).  If no "
"xattr-matching rules are specified, a default xattr filtering rule is used "
"(see the B<--xattrs> option)."
msgstr ""

#. type: SS
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4646
#, no-wrap
msgid "MERGE-FILE FILTER RULES"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4651
msgid ""
"You can merge whole files into your filter rules by specifying either a "
"merge (.) or a dir-merge (:) filter rule (as introduced in the FILTER RULES "
"section above)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4662
msgid ""
"There are two kinds of merged files\\ -- single-instance ('.') and per-"
"directory (':').  A single-instance merge file is read one time, and its "
"rules are incorporated into the filter list in the place of the \".\" rule.  "
"For per-directory merge files, rsync will scan every directory that it "
"traverses for the named file, merging its contents when the file exists into "
"the current list of inherited rules.  These per-directory rule files must be "
"created on the sending side because it is the sending side that is being "
"scanned for the available files to transfer.  These rule files may also need "
"to be transferred to the receiving side if you want them to affect what "
"files don't get deleted (see PER-DIRECTORY RULES AND DELETE below)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4664
msgid "Some examples:"
msgstr "次に例を示します:"

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4672
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"merge /etc/rsync/default.rules\n"
"\\&. /etc/rsync/default.rules\n"
"dir-merge .per-dir-filter\n"
"dir-merge,n- .non-inherited-per-dir-excludes\n"
":n- .non-inherited-per-dir-excludes\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4676
msgid "The following modifiers are accepted after a merge or dir-merge rule:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4680
msgid ""
"A B<-> specifies that the file should consist of only exclude patterns, with "
"no other rule-parsing except for in-file comments."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4683
msgid ""
"A B<+> specifies that the file should consist of only include patterns, with "
"no other rule-parsing except for in-file comments."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4688
msgid ""
"A B<C> is a way to specify that the file should be read in a CVS-compatible "
"manner.  This turns on 'n', 'w', and '-', but also allows the list-clearing "
"token (!) to be specified.  If no filename is provided, \".cvsignore\" is "
"assumed."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4691
msgid ""
"A B<e> will exclude the merge-file name from the transfer; e.g.  \"dir-merge,"
"e \\&.rules\" is like \"dir-merge .rules\" and \"- .rules\"."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4693
msgid "An B<n> specifies that the rules are not inherited by subdirectories."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4698
msgid ""
"A B<w> specifies that the rules are word-split on whitespace instead of the "
"normal line-splitting.  This also turns off comments.  Note: the space that "
"separates the prefix from the rule is treated specially, so \"- foo + bar\" "
"is parsed as two rules (assuming that prefix-parsing wasn't also disabled)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4708
msgid ""
"You may also specify any of the modifiers for the \"+\" or \"-\" rules "
"(above) in order to have the rules that are read in from the file default to "
"having that modifier set (except for the B<!> modifier, which would not be "
"useful).  For instance, \"merge,-/ .excl\" would treat the contents of .excl "
"as absolute-path excludes, while \"dir-merge,s .filt\" and \":sC\" would "
"each make all their per-directory rules apply only on the sending side.  If "
"the merge rule specifies sides to affect (via the B<s> or B<r> modifier or "
"both), then the rules in the file must not specify sides (via a modifier or "
"a rule prefix such as B<hide>)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4718
msgid ""
"Per-directory rules are inherited in all subdirectories of the directory "
"where the merge-file was found unless the 'n' modifier was used.  Each "
"subdirectory's rules are prefixed to the inherited per-directory rules from "
"its parents, which gives the newest rules a higher priority than the "
"inherited rules.  The entire set of dir-merge rules are grouped together in "
"the spot where the merge-file was specified, so it is possible to override "
"dir-merge rules via a rule that got specified earlier in the list of global "
"rules.  When the list-clearing rule (\"!\") is read from a per-directory "
"file, it only clears the inherited rules for the current merge file."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4724
msgid ""
"Another way to prevent a single rule from a dir-merge file from being "
"inherited is to anchor it with a leading slash.  Anchored rules in a per-"
"directory merge-file are relative to the merge-file's directory, so a "
"pattern \"/foo\" would only match the file \"foo\" in the directory where "
"the dir-merge filter file was found."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4726
msgid ""
"Here's an example filter file which you'd specify via B<--filter=\".\\ "
"file\":>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4735
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"merge /home/user/.global-filter\n"
"- *.gz\n"
"dir-merge .rules\n"
"+ *.[ch]\n"
"- *.o\n"
"- foo*\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4743
msgid ""
"This will merge the contents of the /home/user/.global-filter file at the "
"start of the list and also turns the \".rules\" filename into a per-"
"directory filter file.  All rules read in prior to the start of the "
"directory scan follow the global anchoring rules (i.e. a leading slash "
"matches at the root of the transfer)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4748
msgid ""
"If a per-directory merge-file is specified with a path that is a parent "
"directory of the first transfer directory, rsync will scan all the parent "
"directories from that starting point to the transfer directory for the "
"indicated per-directory file.  For instance, here is a common filter (see B<-"
"F>):"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4752
#, no-wrap
msgid "--filter=': /.rsync-filter'\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4760
msgid ""
"That rule tells rsync to scan for the file .rsync-filter in all directories "
"from the root down through the parent directory of the transfer prior to the "
"start of the normal directory scan of the file in the directories that are "
"sent as a part of the transfer. (Note: for an rsync daemon, the root is "
"always the same as the module's \"path\".)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4762
msgid "Some examples of this pre-scanning for per-directory files:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4768
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"rsync -avF /src/path/ /dest/dir\n"
"rsync -av --filter=': ../../.rsync-filter' /src/path/ /dest/dir\n"
"rsync -av --filter=': .rsync-filter' /src/path/ /dest/dir\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4775
msgid ""
"The first two commands above will look for \".rsync-filter\" in \"/\" and \"/"
"src\" before the normal scan begins looking for the file in \"/src/path\" "
"and its subdirectories.  The last command avoids the parent-directory scan "
"and only looks for the \".rsync-filter\" files in each directory that is a "
"part of the transfer."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4784
msgid ""
"If you want to include the contents of a \".cvsignore\" in your patterns, "
"you should use the rule \":C\", which creates a dir-merge of the .cvsignore "
"file, but parsed in a CVS-compatible manner.  You can use this to affect "
"where the B<--cvs-exclude> (B<-C>) option's inclusion of the per-directory "
"\\&.cvsignore file gets placed into your rules by putting the \":C\" "
"wherever you like in your filter rules.  Without this, rsync would add the "
"dir-merge rule for the .cvsignore file at the end of all your other rules "
"(giving it a lower priority than your command-line rules).  For example:"
msgstr "If you want to include the contents of a \".cvsignore\" in your patterns, you should use the rule \":C\", which creates a dir-merge of the .cvsignore file, but parsed in a CVS-compatible manner.  You can use this to affect where the B<--cvs-exclude> (B<-C>) option's inclusion of the per-directory \\&.cvsignore file gets placed into your rules by putting the \":C\" wherever you like in your filter rules.  Without this, rsync would add the dir-merge rule for the .cvsignore file at the end of all your other rules (giving it a lower priority than your command-line rules).  例:"

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4793
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"cat E<lt>E<lt>EOT | rsync -avC --filter='. -' a/ b\n"
"+ foo.o\n"
":C\n"
"- *.old\n"
"EOT\n"
"rsync -avC --include=foo.o -f :C --exclude='*.old' a/ b\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4804
msgid ""
"Both of the above rsync commands are identical.  Each one will merge all the "
"per-directory .cvsignore rules in the middle of the list rather than at the "
"end.  This allows their directory-specific rules to supersede the rules that "
"follow the :C instead of being subservient to all your rules.  To affect the "
"other CVS exclude rules (i.e. the default list of exclusions, the contents "
"of $HOME/.cvsignore, and the value of $CVSIGNORE) you should omit the B<-C> "
"command-line option and instead insert a \"-C\" rule into your filter rules; "
"e.g.  \"B<--filter=-C>\"."
msgstr ""

#. type: SS
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4805
#, no-wrap
msgid "LIST-CLEARING FILTER RULE"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4812
msgid ""
"You can clear the current include/exclude list by using the \"!\" filter "
"rule (as introduced in the FILTER RULES section above).  The \"current\" "
"list is either the global list of rules (if the rule is encountered while "
"parsing the filter options) or a set of per-directory rules (which are "
"inherited in their own sub-list, so a subdirectory can use this to clear out "
"the parent's rules)."
msgstr ""

#. type: SS
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4813
#, no-wrap
msgid "ANCHORING INCLUDE/EXCLUDE PATTERNS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4821
msgid ""
"As mentioned earlier, global include/exclude patterns are anchored at the "
"\"root of the transfer\" (as opposed to per-directory patterns, which are "
"anchored at the merge-file's directory).  If you think of the transfer as a "
"subtree of names that are being sent from sender to receiver, the transfer-"
"root is where the tree starts to be duplicated in the destination "
"directory.  This root governs where patterns that start with a / match."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4827
msgid ""
"Because the matching is relative to the transfer-root, changing the trailing "
"slash on a source path or changing your use of the B<--relative> option "
"affects the path you need to use in your matching (in addition to changing "
"how much of the file tree is duplicated on the destination host).  The "
"following examples demonstrate this."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4831
msgid ""
"Let's say that we want to match two source files, one with an absolute path "
"of \"/home/me/foo/bar\", and one with a path of \"/home/you/bar/baz\".  Here "
"is how the various command choices differ for a 2-source transfer:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4839
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Example cmd: rsync -a /home/me /home/you /dest\n"
"+/- pattern: /me/foo/bar\n"
"+/- pattern: /you/bar/baz\n"
"Target file: /dest/me/foo/bar\n"
"Target file: /dest/you/bar/baz\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4849
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Example cmd: rsync -a /home/me/ /home/you/ /dest\n"
"+/- pattern: /foo/bar               (note missing \"me\")\n"
"+/- pattern: /bar/baz               (note missing \"you\")\n"
"Target file: /dest/foo/bar\n"
"Target file: /dest/bar/baz\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4859
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Example cmd: rsync -a --relative /home/me/ /home/you /dest\n"
"+/- pattern: /home/me/foo/bar       (note full path)\n"
"+/- pattern: /home/you/bar/baz      (ditto)\n"
"Target file: /dest/home/me/foo/bar\n"
"Target file: /dest/home/you/bar/baz\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4869
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Example cmd: cd /home; rsync -a --relative me/foo you/ /dest\n"
"+/- pattern: /me/foo/bar      (starts at specified path)\n"
"+/- pattern: /you/bar/baz     (ditto)\n"
"Target file: /dest/me/foo/bar\n"
"Target file: /dest/you/bar/baz\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4875
msgid ""
"The easiest way to see what name you should filter is to just look at the "
"output when using B<--verbose> and put a / in front of the name (use the B<--"
"dry-run> option if you're not yet ready to copy any files)."
msgstr ""

#. type: SS
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4876
#, no-wrap
msgid "PER-DIRECTORY RULES AND DELETE"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4882
msgid ""
"Without a delete option, per-directory rules are only relevant on the "
"sending side, so you can feel free to exclude the merge files themselves "
"without affecting the transfer.  To make this easy, the 'e' modifier adds "
"this exclude for you, as seen in these two equivalent commands:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4887
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"rsync -av --filter=': .excl' --exclude=.excl host:src/dir /dest\n"
"rsync -av --filter=':e .excl' host:src/dir /dest\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4896
msgid ""
"However, if you want to do a delete on the receiving side AND you want some "
"files to be excluded from being deleted, you'll need to be sure that the "
"receiving side knows what files to exclude.  The easiest way is to include "
"the per-directory merge files in the transfer and use B<--delete-after>, "
"because this ensures that the receiving side gets all the same exclude rules "
"as the sending side before it tries to delete anything:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4900
#, no-wrap
msgid "rsync -avF --delete-after host:src/dir /dest\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4908
msgid ""
"However, if the merge files are not a part of the transfer, you'll need to "
"either specify some global exclude rules (i.e. specified on the command "
"line), or you'll need to maintain your own per-directory merge files on the "
"receiving side.  An example of the first is this (assume that the remote ."
"rules files exclude themselves):"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4913
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"rsync -av --filter=': .rules' --filter='. /my/extra.rules'\n"
"   --delete host:src/dir /dest\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4920
msgid ""
"In the above example the extra.rules file can affect both sides of the "
"transfer, but (on the sending side) the rules are subservient to the rules "
"merged from the .rules files because they were specified after the per-"
"directory merge rule."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4927
msgid ""
"In one final example, the remote side is excluding the .rsync-filter files "
"from the transfer, but we want to use our own .rsync-filter files to control "
"what gets deleted on the receiving side.  To do this we must specifically "
"exclude the per-directory merge files (so that they don't get deleted) and "
"then put rules into the local files to control what else should not get "
"deleted.  Like one of these commands:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4933
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"rsync -av --filter=':e /.rsync-filter' --delete \\e\n"
"    host:src/dir /dest\n"
"rsync -avFF --delete host:src/dir /dest\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: SH
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4936
#, no-wrap
msgid "TRANSFER RULES"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4943
msgid ""
"In addition to the FILTER RULES that affect the recursive file scans that "
"generate the file list on the sending and (when deleting) receiving sides, "
"there are transfer rules. These rules affect which files the generator "
"decides need to be transferred without the side effects of an exclude filter "
"rule.  Transfer rules affect only files and never directories."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4954
msgid ""
"Because a transfer rule does not affect what goes into the sender's (and "
"receiver's) file list, it cannot have any effect on which files get deleted "
"on the receiving side.  For example, if the file \"foo\" is present in the "
"sender's list but its size is such that it is omitted due to a transfer "
"rule, the receiving side does not request the file.  However, its presence "
"in the file list means that a delete pass will not remove a matching file "
"named \"foo\" on the receiving side.  On the other hand, a server-side "
"exclude (hide) of the file \"foo\" leaves the file out of the server's file "
"list, and absent a receiver-side exclude (protect) the receiver will remove "
"a matching file named \"foo\" if deletions are requested."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4958
msgid ""
"Given that the files are still in the sender's file list, the B<--prune-"
"empty-dirs> option will not judge a directory as being empty even if it "
"contains only files that the transfer rules omitted."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4962
msgid ""
"Similarly, a transfer rule does not have any extra effect on which files are "
"deleted on the receiving side, so setting a maximum file size for the "
"transfer does not prevent big files from being deleted."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4967
msgid ""
"Examples of transfer rules include the default \"quick check\" algorithm "
"(which compares size & modify time), the B<--update> option, the B<--max-"
"size> option, the B<--ignore-non-existing> option, and a few others."
msgstr ""

#. type: SH
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4968
#, no-wrap
msgid "BATCH MODE"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4978
msgid ""
"Batch mode can be used to apply the same set of updates to many identical "
"systems.  Suppose one has a tree which is replicated on a number of hosts.  "
"Now suppose some changes have been made to this source tree and those "
"changes need to be propagated to the other hosts.  In order to do this using "
"batch mode, rsync is run with the write-batch option to apply the changes "
"made to the source tree to one of the destination trees.  The write-batch "
"option causes the rsync client to store in a \"batch file\" all the "
"information needed to repeat this operation against other, identical "
"destination trees."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4984
msgid ""
"Generating the batch file once saves having to perform the file status, "
"checksum, and data block generation more than once when updating multiple "
"destination trees.  Multicast transport protocols can be used to transfer "
"the batch update files in parallel to many hosts at once, instead of sending "
"the same data to every host individually."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4989
msgid ""
"To apply the recorded changes to another destination tree, run rsync with "
"the read-batch option, specifying the name of the same batch file, and the "
"destination tree.  Rsync updates the destination tree using the information "
"stored in the batch file."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:4998
msgid ""
"For your convenience, a script file is also created when the write-batch "
"option is used: it will be named the same as the batch file with \".sh\" "
"appended.  This script file contains a command line suitable for updating a "
"destination tree using the associated batch file.  It can be executed using "
"a Bourne (or Bourne-like) shell, optionally passing in an alternate "
"destination tree pathname which is then used instead of the original "
"destination path.  This is useful when the destination tree path on the "
"current host differs from the one used to create the batch file."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5000
msgid "Examples:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5006
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$ rsync --write-batch=foo -a host:/source/dir/ /adest/dir/\n"
"$ scp foo* remote:\n"
"$ ssh remote ./foo.sh /bdest/dir/\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5013
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$ rsync --write-batch=foo -a /source/dir/ /adest/dir/\n"
"$ ssh remote rsync --read-batch=- -a /bdest/dir/ E<lt>foo\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5021
msgid ""
"In these examples, rsync is used to update /adest/dir/ from /source/dir/ and "
"the information to repeat this operation is stored in \"foo\" and \"foo."
"sh\".  The host \"remote\" is then updated with the batched data going into "
"the directory /bdest/dir.  The differences between the two examples reveals "
"some of the flexibility you have in how you deal with batches:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5026
msgid ""
"The first example shows that the initial copy doesn't have to be local\\ -- "
"you can push or pull data to/from a remote host using either the remote-"
"shell syntax or rsync daemon syntax, as desired."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5029
msgid ""
"The first example uses the created \"foo.sh\" file to get the right rsync "
"options when running the read-batch command on the remote host."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5036
msgid ""
"The second example reads the batch data via standard input so that the batch "
"file doesn't need to be copied to the remote machine first.  This example "
"avoids the foo.sh script because it needed to use a modified B<--read-batch> "
"option, but you could edit the script file if you wished to make use of it "
"(just be sure that no other option is trying to use standard input, such as "
"the B<--exclude-from=-> option)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5038
msgid "Caveats:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5051
msgid ""
"The read-batch option expects the destination tree that it is updating to be "
"identical to the destination tree that was used to create the batch update "
"fileset.  When a difference between the destination trees is encountered the "
"update might be discarded with a warning (if the file appears to be up-to-"
"date already) or the file-update may be attempted and then, if the file "
"fails to verify, the update discarded with an error.  This means that it "
"should be safe to re-run a read-batch operation if the command got "
"interrupted.  If you wish to force the batched-update to always be attempted "
"regardless of the file's size and date, use the B<-I> option (when reading "
"the batch).  If an error occurs, the destination tree will probably be in a "
"partially updated state.  In that case, rsync can be used in its regular "
"(non-batch) mode of operation to fix up the destination tree."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5059
msgid ""
"The rsync version used on all destinations must be at least as new as the "
"one used to generate the batch file.  Rsync will die with an error if the "
"protocol version in the batch file is too new for the batch-reading rsync to "
"handle.  See also the B<--protocol> option for a way to have the creating "
"rsync generate a batch file that an older rsync can understand.  (Note that "
"batch files changed format in version 2.6.3 (September 2004), so mixing "
"versions older than that with newer versions will not work.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5067
msgid ""
"When reading a batch file, rsync will force the value of certain options to "
"match the data in the batch file if you didn't set them to the same as the "
"batch-writing command.  Other options can (and should) be changed.  For "
"instance B<--write-batch> changes to B<--read-batch>, B<--files-from> is "
"dropped, and the B<--filter> / B<--include> / B<--exclude> options are not "
"needed unless one of the B<--delete> options is specified."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5074
msgid ""
"The code that creates the BATCH.sh file transforms any filter/include/"
"exclude options into a single list that is appended as a \"here\" document "
"to the shell script file.  An advanced user can use this to modify the "
"exclude list if a change in what gets deleted by B<--delete> is desired.  A "
"normal user can ignore this detail and just use the shell script as an easy "
"way to run the appropriate B<--read-batch> command for the batched data."
msgstr ""

#. type: SH
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5075
#, no-wrap
msgid "SYMBOLIC LINKS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5079
msgid ""
"Three basic behaviors are possible when rsync encounters a symbolic link in "
"the source directory."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5082
msgid ""
"By default, symbolic links are not transferred at all.  A message \"skipping "
"non-regular\" file is emitted for any symlinks that exist."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5087
msgid ""
"If B<--links> is specified, then symlinks are added to the transfer (instead "
"of being noisily ignored), and the default handling is to recreate them with "
"the same target on the destination.  Note that B<--archive> implies B<--"
"links>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5090
msgid ""
"If B<--copy-links> is specified, then symlinks are \"collapsed\" by copying "
"their referent, rather than the symlink."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5099
msgid ""
"Rsync can also distinguish \"safe\" and \"unsafe\" symbolic links.  An "
"example where this might be used is a web site mirror that wishes to ensure "
"that the rsync module that is copied does not include symbolic links to B</"
"etc/passwd> in the public section of the site.  Using B<--copy-unsafe-links> "
"will cause any links to be copied as the file they point to on the "
"destination.  Using B<--safe-links> will cause unsafe links to be omitted by "
"the receiver.  (Note that you must specify or imply B<--links> for B<--safe-"
"links> to have any effect.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5103
msgid ""
"Symbolic links are considered unsafe if they are absolute symlinks (start "
"with B</>), empty, or if they contain enough \"..\" components to ascend "
"from the top of the transfer."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5107
msgid ""
"Here's a summary of how the symlink options are interpreted.  The list is in "
"order of precedence, so if your combination of options isn't mentioned, use "
"the first line that is a complete subset of your options:"
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5108
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--copy-links>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5111
msgid ""
"Turn all symlinks into normal files and directories (leaving no symlinks in "
"the transfer for any other options to affect)."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5111
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--copy-dirlinks>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5114
msgid ""
"Turn just symlinks to directories into real directories, leaving all other "
"symlinks to be handled as described below."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5114
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--links\\ --copy-unsafe-links>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5117
msgid "Turn all unsafe symlinks into files and create all safe symlinks."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5120
msgid "Turn all unsafe symlinks into files, noisily skip all safe symlinks."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5120
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--links\\ --safe-links>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5123
msgid ""
"The receiver skips creating unsafe symlinks found in the transfer and "
"creates the safe ones."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5123
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<--links>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5125
msgid "Create all symlinks."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5128
msgid ""
"For the effect of B<--munge-links>, see the discussion in that option's "
"section."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5132
msgid ""
"Note that the B<--keep-dirlinks> option does not affect symlinks in the "
"transfer but instead affects how rsync treats a symlink to a directory that "
"already exists on the receiving side.  See that option's section for a "
"warning."
msgstr ""

#. type: SH
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5133
#, no-wrap
msgid "DIAGNOSTICS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5138
msgid ""
"Rsync occasionally produces error messages that may seem a little cryptic.  "
"The one that seems to cause the most confusion is \"protocol version "
"mismatch\\ -- is your shell clean?\"."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5142
msgid ""
"This message is usually caused by your startup scripts or remote shell "
"facility producing unwanted garbage on the stream that rsync is using for "
"its transport.  The way to diagnose this problem is to run your remote shell "
"like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5146
#, no-wrap
msgid "ssh remotehost /bin/true E<gt> out.dat\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5155
msgid ""
"then look at out.dat.  If everything is working correctly then out.dat "
"should be a zero length file.  If you are getting the above error from rsync "
"then you will probably find that out.dat contains some text or data.  Look "
"at the contents and try to work out what is producing it.  The most common "
"cause is incorrectly configured shell startup scripts (such as .cshrc or ."
"profile) that contain output statements for non-interactive logins."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5159
msgid ""
"If you are having trouble debugging filter patterns, then try specifying the "
"B<-vv> option.  At this level of verbosity rsync will show why each "
"individual file is included or excluded."
msgstr ""

#. type: SH
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5160
#, no-wrap
msgid "EXIT VALUES"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5164
msgid "B<0> - Success"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5166
msgid "B<1> - Syntax or usage error"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5168
msgid "B<2> - Protocol incompatibility"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5170
msgid "B<3> - Errors selecting input/output files, directories"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5175
msgid "B<4> - Requested action not supported. Either:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5177
msgid ""
"an attempt was made to manipulate 64-bit files on a platform that cannot "
"support them"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5179
msgid ""
"an option was specified that is supported by the client and not by the server"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5182
msgid "B<5> - Error starting client-server protocol"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5184
msgid "B<10> - Error in socket I/O"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5186
msgid "B<11> - Error in file I/O"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5188
msgid "B<12> - Error in rsync protocol data stream"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5190
msgid "B<13> - Errors with program diagnostics"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5192
msgid "B<14> - Error in IPC code"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5194
msgid "B<15> - Sibling process crashed (e.g. core dumped)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5196
msgid "B<16> - Sibling process was killed by a signal."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5198
msgid "B<19> - Received SIGUSR1."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5200
msgid "B<20> - Received SIGINT, SIGTERM, or SIGHUP."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5202
msgid "B<21> - Some error returned by B<waitpid()>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5204
msgid "B<22> - Error allocating core memory buffers"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5206
msgid "B<23> - Partial transfer due to error"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5208
msgid "B<24> - Partial transfer due to vanished source files"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5210
msgid "B<25> - The --max-delete limit stopped deletions"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5212
msgid "B<30> - Timeout in data send/receive"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5214
msgid "B<35> - Timeout waiting for daemon connection"
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5217
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<CVSIGNORE>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5220
msgid ""
"The CVSIGNORE environment variable supplements any ignore patterns in \\&."
"cvsignore files.  See the B<--cvs-exclude> option for more details."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5220
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<RSYNC_ICONV>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5223
msgid "Specify a default B<--iconv> setting using this environment variable."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5223
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<RSYNC_OLD_ARGS>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5229
msgid ""
"Specify a \"1\" if you want the B<--old-args> option to be enabled by "
"default, a \"2\" (or more) if you want it to be enabled in the repeated-"
"option state, or a \"0\" to make sure that it is disabled by default. When "
"this environment variable is set to a non-zero value, it supersedes the "
"B<RSYNC_PROTECT_ARGS> variable."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5232
msgid ""
"This variable is ignored if B<--old-args>, B<--no-old-args>, or B<--secluded-"
"args> is specified on the command line."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5234
msgid "First supported in 3.2.4 (April 2022)."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5234
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<RSYNC_PROTECT_ARGS>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5238
msgid ""
"Specify a non-zero numeric value if you want the B<--secluded-args> option "
"to be enabled by default, or a zero value to make sure that it is disabled "
"by default."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5241
msgid ""
"This variable is ignored if B<--secluded-args>, B<--no-secluded-args>, or "
"B<--old-args> is specified on the command line."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5244
msgid ""
"Starting in 3.2.4 (April 2022), this variable is ignored if "
"B<RSYNC_OLD_ARGS> is set to a non-zero value."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5244
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<RSYNC_RSH>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5248
msgid ""
"This environment variable allows you to override the default shell used as "
"the transport for rsync.  Command line options are permitted after the "
"command name, just as in the B<--rsh> (B<-e>) option."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5248
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<RSYNC_PROXY>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5252
msgid ""
"This environment variable allows you to redirect your rsync client to use a "
"web proxy when connecting to an rsync daemon.  You should set B<RSYNC_PROXY> "
"to a hostname:port pair."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5252
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<RSYNC_PASSWORD>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5257
msgid ""
"This environment variable allows you to set the password for an rsync "
"B<daemon> connection, which avoids the password prompt.  Note that this does "
"B<not> supply a password to a remote shell transport such as ssh (consult "
"its documentation for how to do that)."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5257
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<USER> or B<LOGNAME>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5261
msgid ""
"The USER or LOGNAME environment variables are used to determine the default "
"username sent to an rsync daemon.  If neither is set, the username defaults "
"to \"nobody\".  If both are set, B<USER> takes precedence."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5261
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<RSYNC_PARTIAL_DIR>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5265
msgid ""
"This environment variable specifies the directory to use for a B<--partial> "
"transfer without implying that partial transfers be enabled.  See the B<--"
"partial-dir> option for full details."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5265
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<RSYNC_COMPRESS_LIST>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5270
msgid ""
"This environment variable allows you to customize the negotiation of the "
"compression algorithm by specifying an alternate order or a reduced list of "
"names.  Use the command B<rsync\\ --version> to see the available "
"compression names.  See the B<--compress> option for full details."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5270
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<RSYNC_CHECKSUM_LIST>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5275
msgid ""
"This environment variable allows you to customize the negotiation of the "
"checksum algorithm by specifying an alternate order or a reduced list of "
"names.  Use the command B<rsync\\ --version> to see the available checksum "
"names.  See the B<--checksum-choice> option for full details."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5275
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<RSYNC_MAX_ALLOC>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5278
msgid ""
"This environment variable sets an allocation maximum as if you had used the "
"B<--max-alloc> option."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5278
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<RSYNC_PORT>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5284
msgid ""
"This environment variable is not read by rsync, but is instead set in its "
"sub-environment when rsync is running the remote shell in combination with a "
"daemon connection.  This allows a script such as B<rsync-ssl> to be able to "
"know the port number that the user specified on the command line."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5284
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<HOME>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5287
msgid ""
"This environment variable is used to find the user's default .cvsignore file."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5287
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<RSYNC_CONNECT_PROG>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5291
msgid ""
"This environment variable is mainly used in debug setups to set the program "
"to use when making a daemon connection.  See CONNECTING TO AN RSYNC DAEMON "
"for full details."
msgstr ""

#. type: IP
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5291
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<RSYNC_SHELL>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5295
msgid ""
"This environment variable is mainly used in debug setups to set the program "
"to use to run the program specified by B<RSYNC_CONNECT_PROG>.  See "
"CONNECTING TO AN RSYNC DAEMON for full details."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5299
msgid "/etc/rsyncd.conf or rsyncd.conf"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5303
msgid "B<rsync-ssl>(1), B<rsyncd.conf>(5), B<rrsync>(1)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5308
msgid "Times are transferred as *nix time_t values."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5311
msgid ""
"When transferring to FAT filesystems rsync may re-sync unmodified files.  "
"See the comments on the B<--modify-window> option."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5313
msgid ""
"File permissions, devices, etc. are transferred as native numerical values."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5315
msgid "See also the comments on the B<--delete> option."
msgstr ""

#. type: SH
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5324
#, no-wrap
msgid "INTERNAL OPTIONS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5332
msgid ""
"The options B<--server> and B<--sender> are used internally by rsync, and "
"should never be typed by a user under normal circumstances.  Some awareness "
"of these options may be needed in certain scenarios, such as when setting up "
"a login that can only run an rsync command.  For instance, the support "
"directory of the rsync distribution has an example script named rrsync (for "
"restricted rsync)  that can be used with a restricted ssh login."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5344
msgid ""
"An rsync web site is available at E<.UR https://rsync.samba.org/> E<.UE .> "
"The site includes an FAQ-O-Matic which may cover questions unanswered by "
"this manual page."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5348
msgid ""
"The rsync github project is E<.UR https://github.com/RsyncProject/rsync> E<."
"UE .>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5354
msgid ""
"We would be delighted to hear from you if you like this program.  Please "
"contact the mailing-list at E<.UR mailto:rsync@lists.samba.org> rsync@lists."
"samba.org E<.UE .>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5357
msgid ""
"This program uses the excellent zlib compression library written by Jean-"
"loup Gailly and Mark Adler."
msgstr ""

#. type: SH
#: original/man1/rsync.1:5358
#, no-wrap
msgid "THANKS"
msgstr ""

